Guide.content._register("w05", {"id":"w05","order":5,"region":"knowledge","title":"Retrieval and RAG Patterns","short":"Retrieve and rank","tagline":"The right chunk is in the index. Will the system find it, rank it first and know when it found nothing?","story":"The content is chunked, embedded and indexed, and the question arrives. The system has to turn a few words into the right three passages out of millions. It can miss an exact identifier, bury the best chunk at rank 31, rewrite the question into something else, or answer confidently from a weak shortlist. This world is the choice of retrieval stack and the discipline of debugging it.","oneLiner":"Choose how to search, fuse, rerank, rewrite, plan and cite, and know which dial flips each choice.","youWillLearn":["Compare lexical, dense, learned sparse and hybrid retrieval and say which query mix favours each","Compute reciprocal rank fusion by hand, tune k and weights, and explain when convex combination wins","Pick a reranker by latency, data path and text length, and set coarse cutoffs and abstention rules","Choose between rewrite, multi-query, HyDE, step-back, decomposition and self-query, and know how each can hurt","Decide when RAPTOR, GraphRAG, LazyGraphRAG or agentic retrieval pays for its cost, with preview status labelled","Choose between RAG, long context and cache-augmented generation, and enforce citations","Debug a bad answer by symptom, stage by stage"],"minutes":115,"prereqs":["w04"],"flashcards":[{"id":"w05-fc-001","front":"What does reciprocal rank fusion compute for a document?","back":"The sum over lists of `weight / (k + rank)`, with ranks starting at 1. It uses ranks only, so BM25 and cosine scores never need a shared scale.","tags":["rrf"]},{"id":"w05-fc-002","front":"At `k = 60`, what is the highest RRF score a document can reach from two lists?","back":"Rank 1 in both lists gives 2/61, about 0.0328. RRF scores look tiny, and only their order matters.","tags":["rrf"]},{"id":"w05-fc-003","front":"Small `k` or large `k`: which one rewards agreement between lists?","back":"Large `k` flattens the curve, so a document found by both lists beats one that is first in only one list. Small `k` trusts top ranks.","tags":["rrf"]},{"id":"w05-fc-004","front":"What did Bruch et al. (2022) find about RRF versus convex combination (CC)?","back":"CC of normalised scores beat RRF in and out of domain. RRF was sensitive to its parameters. CC needed only a small labelled set to tune.","tags":["rrf","fusion"]},{"id":"w05-fc-005","front":"What can the Azure semantic ranker not fix?","back":"Recall. It reorders at most the top 50 candidates and cannot find new documents, so fix candidate recall before you tune the ranker.","tags":["rerank"]},{"id":"w05-fc-006","front":"Why avoid fine thresholds on `@search.rerankerScore` (0 to 4)?","back":"The score distribution shifts with infrastructure and model updates. Use coarse cutoffs, watch the abstention rate and re-test on a labelled set.","tags":["rerank","threshold"]},{"id":"w05-fc-007","front":"Which sizing rule links `k` and `maxTextRecallSize` to the semantic ranker?","back":"Make them sum to at least 50, because the ranker takes the top 50 of BM25 or RRF results. `maxTextRecallSize` is a preview parameter (2026-08-01-preview).","tags":["rerank","azure"]},{"id":"w05-fc-008","front":"Why can LLM query rewriting hurt identifier queries?","back":"A rewrite can drop the exact identifier or product code, and Azure warns about this. Keep the original query as one of the searches you fuse.","tags":["query"]},{"id":"w05-fc-009","front":"What does HyDE embed, and why does it tolerate false details?","back":"An LLM-written hypothetical answer. The encoder maps it near real documents, so invented details drop out because retrieval returns only real text. It needs no relevance labels.","tags":["query"]},{"id":"w05-fc-010","front":"When is query decomposition worth its extra LLM call?","back":"When one question has several parts or hops. Each sub-query fetches its own evidence in parallel. Skip it for single-fact lookups, where it only adds latency.","tags":["query"]},{"id":"w05-fc-011","front":"Which question type is GraphRAG built for?","back":"Global, corpus-wide questions such as main themes. It extracts a graph, clusters it with Leiden and summarises communities, at a high indexing cost.","tags":["graph"]},{"id":"w05-fc-012","front":"What is LazyGraphRAG's headline claim and its caveat?","back":"Indexing cost equal to vector RAG (0.1% of full GraphRAG) and comparable global-query quality at over 700 times lower query cost. It is a vendor benchmark.","tags":["graph"]},{"id":"w05-fc-013","front":"What does RAPTOR build and what does it cost?","back":"A tree of recursively clustered and summarised chunks, searched at several abstraction levels. Index build needs LLM calls that grow with corpus size.","tags":["graph","tree"]},{"id":"w05-fc-014","front":"Name the Azure agentic retrieval reasoning efforts.","back":"`minimal` (no LLM planning), `low` (default, one planning pass), `medium` (adds a classifier and one retry) and `auto` (preview, escalates up to medium).","tags":["agentic"]},{"id":"w05-fc-015","front":"Which agentic retrieval API version is stable, and what does it allow?","back":"`2026-04-01`: `minimal` effort, extractive output and `intents` input. Query planning, answer synthesis, low, medium and auto need `2026-08-01-preview`.","tags":["agentic","preview"]},{"id":"w05-fc-016","front":"What happens when a knowledge base is queried without a user identity header?","back":"Indexed sources return unfiltered results. Pass the user token in `x-ms-query-source-authorization` and set `ingestionPermissionOptions` when you ingest.","tags":["agentic","security"]},{"id":"w05-fc-017","front":"CRAG versus Self-RAG: which needs a fine-tuned model?","back":"Self-RAG trains one LM to emit reflection tokens. CRAG is plug-and-play: a light evaluator scores retrieval and triggers fallbacks such as web search.","tags":["corrective"]},{"id":"w05-fc-018","front":"Anthropic's rule of thumb for skipping RAG?","back":"A knowledge base under 200,000 tokens (about 500 pages) can go straight into the prompt. It is vendor guidance, and update rate and permissions still decide.","tags":["long-context"]},{"id":"w05-fc-019","front":"What is cache-augmented generation (CAG) and where does it break?","back":"Preload a small, stable corpus and cache its KV state so no retrieval runs per query. It cannot hide documents per user and needs a rebuild on every change.","tags":["cag"]},{"id":"w05-fc-020","front":"Does a citation prove the answer is right?","back":"No. A citation proves provenance, not entailment. Check that the cited span supports the claim, with quote-first prompting or an entailment check.","tags":["citations"]},{"id":"w05-t01-fc1","front":"Name the query-time stages of an advanced RAG pipeline.","back":"Query transformation, retrieval, fusion, reranking, cutoff, context assembly, generation, citation.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t01"},{"id":"w05-t01-fc2","front":"What is the oracle test for a bad RAG answer?","back":"Put the gold chunk in the prompt. Still wrong means a generation fault. Right means retrieval missed it.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t01"},{"id":"w05-t01-fc3","front":"Naive, advanced and modular RAG: what separates them?","back":"Naive is one search then one prompt. Advanced adds pre- and post-retrieval steps. Modular makes stages swappable and routable.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t01"},{"id":"w05-t02-fc1","front":"What are the Azure BM25 defaults for `k1` and `b`?","back":"`k1` is 1.2 and `b` is 0.75. BM25 is fixed on services created after July 2020.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t02"},{"id":"w05-t02-fc2","front":"What does SPLADE add to sparse retrieval?","back":"Learned term weights with expansion terms, sparsity regularisation and log saturation, still served from an inverted index.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t02"},{"id":"w05-t02-fc3","front":"Why can identical BM25 queries reorder results?","back":"Tied scores have undefined order, and each replica computes index statistics separately.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t02"},{"id":"w05-t03-fc1","front":"Why does dense retrieval return results for a nonsense query?","back":"Nearest-neighbour search always returns the k closest vectors. Only a threshold or abstention rule can stop weak ones.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t03"},{"id":"w05-t03-fc2","front":"What did EntityQuestions show about dense retrievers?","back":"They drastically underperformed sparse methods on simple entity-rich questions, generalising only to common entities.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t03"},{"id":"w05-t03-fc3","front":"Why must the query be cleaned like the chunks?","back":"A normalisation mismatch between query and chunk text, such as casing, quietly lowers similarity.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t03"},{"id":"w05-t04-fc1","front":"What does RRF compute for a document?","back":"The sum over lists of `weight / (k + rank)`, with ranks from 1. Missing documents add nothing.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t04"},{"id":"w05-t04-fc2","front":"Why can you not threshold on an RRF score?","back":"It is bounded by about 1/k per list and depends on the number of lists, so it orders results but does not measure relevance.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t04"},{"id":"w05-t04-fc3","front":"How does hybrid search feed the Azure semantic ranker?","back":"Only the top 50 results go to the ranker, so vector `k` plus `maxTextRecallSize` should sum to at least 50.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t04"},{"id":"w05-t04-fc4","front":"Which paper argues RRF is not tuning-free?","back":"Bruch et al. 2022: a convex combination beat RRF and RRF was sensitive to its parameters.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t04"},{"id":"w05-t05-fc1","front":"Why can a reranker not fix poor recall?","back":"It only reorders the candidates it receives. Azure's semantic ranker takes the top 50 and cannot find new documents.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t05"},{"id":"w05-t05-fc2","front":"What is the Azure `@search.rerankerScore` range?","back":"0.00 to 4.00. The distribution shifts with model updates, so use coarse cutoffs only.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t05"},{"id":"w05-t05-fc3","front":"Cross-encoder versus bi-encoder?","back":"A cross-encoder scores query and chunk together, which is accurate but slow. A bi-encoder embeds them separately, which is fast for first-stage search.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t05"},{"id":"w05-t06-fc1","front":"Why avoid fine-grained cutoffs on the Azure reranker score?","back":"The score distribution shifts with infrastructure and model updates, so use coarse bands, monitor and recalibrate.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t06"},{"id":"w05-t06-fc2","front":"What does the vector `threshold` parameter do?","back":"It drops low-scoring vector matches before fusion, even if fewer than `k` remain. Preview in `2026-08-01-preview`.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t06"},{"id":"w05-t06-fc3","front":"How do you calibrate an abstention cutoff?","back":"Measure coverage and false answers by cutoff on a golden set that includes unanswerable queries, then pick by error cost.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t06"},{"id":"w05-t07-fc1","front":"What are the three jobs called query rewriting?","back":"Cleaning to match chunk normalisation, standalone rewriting from chat history, and expansion into variants.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t07"},{"id":"w05-t07-fc2","front":"What is the main risk of an LLM query rewrite?","back":"Drift: it can paraphrase away identifiers or change meaning. Keep the original query in the fusion.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t07"},{"id":"w05-t07-fc3","front":"What does Azure's `queryRewrites` accept?","back":"`generative|count-N` with N from 1 to 10, plus a supported `queryLanguage`. Preview.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t07"},{"id":"w05-t08-fc1","front":"What does HyDE embed?","back":"A hypothetical answer written by a model, not the question. Nearest real documents are then retrieved.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t08"},{"id":"w05-t08-fc2","front":"Why can a false hypothetical document still retrieve well?","back":"The encoder compresses the text and drops fabricated specifics, keeping a vector near relevant passages.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t08"},{"id":"w05-t08-fc3","front":"Name a safe way to use HyDE next to identifiers.","back":"Use the hypothetical text only on the vector leg and keep the original query on the BM25 leg.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t08"},{"id":"w05-t09-fc1","front":"What is RAG-Fusion?","back":"Generate multiple queries, retrieve for each and merge the lists with reciprocal rank fusion.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t09"},{"id":"w05-t09-fc2","front":"What is step-back prompting?","back":"Ask for a higher-level concept or principle first, then reason. In RAG, retrieve for both the specific and the abstract question.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t09"},{"id":"w05-t09-fc3","front":"Name two controls that keep multi-query cheap and clean.","back":"Cap N and dedupe by chunk id. Keep the original query as one list and rerank after fusion.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t09"},{"id":"w05-t10-fc1","front":"Decomposition versus multi-hop?","back":"Decomposition splits independent sub-questions that can run in parallel. Multi-hop chains searches where each depends on the last.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t10"},{"id":"w05-t10-fc2","front":"What does IRCoT do?","back":"It interleaves retrieval with chain-of-thought steps so each retrieval depends on what has been derived so far.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t10"},{"id":"w05-t10-fc3","front":"List four stop conditions for a retrieval loop.","back":"Maximum hops, token or time budget, sufficiency check, and no-new-evidence rule.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t10"},{"id":"w05-t11-fc1","front":"What does self-query output?","back":"A semantic query plus a structured metadata filter over declared fields, validated before it reaches the search engine.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t11"},{"id":"w05-t11-fc2","front":"Why must authorization not rely on a model-generated filter?","back":"The model is steerable and best effort. Build access filters in code from identity and AND them with generated ones.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t11"},{"id":"w05-t11-fc3","front":"What do you do when a generated filter returns zero results?","back":"Relax the weakest constraint and retry, and log the rejected or empty filter.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t11"},{"id":"w05-t12-fc1","front":"What does GraphRAG add that top-k RAG lacks?","back":"A graph of entities and relationships with hierarchical community summaries, which lets it answer global questions.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t12"},{"id":"w05-t12-fc2","front":"What cost numbers did Microsoft report for LazyGraphRAG (2024-11-25)?","back":"Indexing like vector RAG and 0.1% of GraphRAG, over 700 times lower query cost for comparable global quality. Vendor benchmark.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t12"},{"id":"w05-t12-fc3","front":"Why can community summaries leak data?","back":"They blend facts from many documents, so readers with different permissions can see information they should not.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t12"},{"id":"w05-t13-fc1","front":"What does agentic retrieval do at `low` effort?","back":"One LLM planning pass makes focused subqueries and picks sources. Subqueries run in parallel, are reranked and merged.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t13"},{"id":"w05-t13-fc2","front":"Which API version holds stable agentic retrieval, and what does it allow?","back":"`2026-04-01`: `minimal` effort, `intents` input and extractive output. Planning, synthesis and SSE need `2026-08-01-preview`.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t13"},{"id":"w05-t13-fc3","front":"How are permissions enforced in Foundry IQ indexed sources?","back":"Ingest ACLs with `ingestionPermissionOptions` and send the user token in `x-ms-query-source-authorization`. Without it results are unfiltered.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t13"},{"id":"w05-t14-fc1","front":"What does the CRAG retrieval evaluator do?","back":"Scores retrieved document quality and triggers different actions, including web search fallback, then filters with decompose-then-recompose.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t14"},{"id":"w05-t14-fc2","front":"What does Self-RAG require?","back":"A fine-tuned language model that emits reflection tokens to decide when to retrieve and to critique passages and its output.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t14"},{"id":"w05-t14-fc3","front":"What bounds a corrective loop?","back":"A retry ceiling, a token and time budget, and abstention when the budget is spent.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t14"},{"id":"w05-t15-fc1","front":"What is cache-augmented generation?","back":"Preload a small, manageable knowledge set into the context and cache its KV state, so no retrieval runs per query.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t15"},{"id":"w05-t15-fc2","front":"What does Self-Route do?","back":"Routes each query to RAG or long context based on model self-reflection, keeping accuracy near long context at lower cost.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t15"},{"id":"w05-t15-fc3","front":"What did NoLiMa find at 32K tokens?","back":"11 of 13 models fell below 50% of their short-context baseline. GPT-4o dropped from 99.3% to 69.7%.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t15"},{"id":"w05-t16-fc1","front":"Citation versus entailment: what is the difference?","back":"A citation shows where a claim came from. Entailment shows the source actually supports it. Check it separately.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t16"},{"id":"w05-t16-fc2","front":"What is the incompatibility in Anthropic's Citations feature?","back":"Citations cannot be combined with structured outputs. Enabling both returns HTTP 400.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t16"},{"id":"w05-t16-fc3","front":"What does OpenAI file search return for citations?","back":"`file_citation` annotations on the answer. Raw hits need `include: [\"file_search_call.results\"]`.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t16"},{"id":"w05-t17-fc1","front":"What is the oracle test?","back":"Put the gold chunk in the prompt. Still wrong is a generation fault. Right means retrieval, ranking, filters or context assembly failed.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t17"},{"id":"w05-t17-fc2","front":"What should a RAG trace record?","back":"Raw and rewritten query, filters, per-leg ranks, fused and reranked order with scores, cutoff decision, final context, prompt, answer and citations.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t17"},{"id":"w05-t17-fc3","front":"In which order do you debug a bad answer?","back":"Corpus, index and ACLs, candidates, ranking and cutoff, prompt context, then generation.","tags":[],"topic":"w05-t17"}],"references":[{"id":"r1","title":"Relevance scoring in hybrid search using Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/hybrid-search-ranking","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Exact RRF formula, parallel query counts, score ranges and vector query weights.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r2","title":"Create a hybrid query in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/hybrid-search-how-to-query","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"k, top, maxTextRecallSize and the sum-to-50 rule for semantic ranking.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r3","title":"Semantic ranking in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/semantic-search-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Inputs, the top-50 limit, reranker scores 0 to 4, captions, answers and billing.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r4","title":"Agentic retrieval in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/agentic-retrieval-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Pipeline, components, API versions and what is stable versus preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r5","title":"Set the retrieval reasoning effort","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/agentic-retrieval-how-to-set-retrieval-reasoning-effort","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The minimal, low, medium and auto trade-offs with their limits.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r6","title":"Query a knowledge base using the retrieve action or MCP endpoint","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/agentic-retrieval-how-to-retrieve","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Response shape, activity log, SSE streaming and the permission headers.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r7","title":"What is Foundry IQ?","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/concepts/what-is-foundry-iq","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Managed knowledge bases for agents and permission-aware retrieval.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r8","title":"Information retrieval phase of a RAG solution","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/ai-ml/guide/rag/rag-information-retrieval","type":"doc","publisher":"Azure Architecture Center","note":"Vendor-neutral view of search types, query translation and reranking.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r9","title":"Reciprocal Rank Fusion outperforms Condorcet and individual Rank Learning Methods","url":"https://cormack.uwaterloo.ca/cormacksigir09-rrf.pdf","type":"paper","publisher":"SIGIR 2009 (Cormack, Clarke, Buettcher)","note":"Source of the RRF formula and the k = 60 pilot.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r10","title":"An Analysis of Fusion Functions for Hybrid Retrieval","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.11934","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Bruch et al.)","note":"Evidence that convex combination can beat RRF and that RRF is parameter sensitive.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r11","title":"BEIR: A Heterogeneous Benchmark for Zero-shot Evaluation of Information Retrieval Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.08663","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Why BM25 stays a strong out-of-domain baseline and what re-ranking costs.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r12","title":"ColBERTv2: Effective and Efficient Retrieval via Lightweight Late Interaction","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.01488","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Late interaction, index size inflation and residual compression.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r13","title":"Is ChatGPT Good at Search? Investigating Large Language Models as Re-Ranking Agents (RankGPT)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.09542","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"LLM listwise reranking versus supervised rerankers.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r14","title":"Precise Zero-Shot Dense Retrieval without Relevance Labels (HyDE)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.10496","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Gao et al.)","note":"The original HyDE method.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r15","title":"Take a Step Back: Evoking Reasoning via Abstraction in Large Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.06117","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Zheng et al.)","note":"Step-back prompting and its reasoning-task gains.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r16","title":"From Local to Global: A Graph RAG Approach to Query-Focused Summarization","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.16130","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Microsoft Research)","note":"GraphRAG method and why baseline RAG fails on global questions.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r17","title":"LazyGraphRAG: Setting a new standard for quality and cost","url":"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/blog/lazygraphrag-setting-a-new-standard-for-quality-and-cost/","type":"blog","publisher":"Microsoft Research","note":"Cost versus quality dial for graph-style RAG, with vendor-reported numbers.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r18","title":"RAPTOR: Recursive Abstractive Processing for Tree-Organized Retrieval","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.18059","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Summary-tree retrieval and its QuALITY result.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r19","title":"Corrective Retrieval Augmented Generation","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.15884","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"A retrieval evaluator with fallbacks, a model for confidence gating.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r20","title":"Self-RAG: Learning to Retrieve, Generate, and Critique through Self-Reflection","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.11511","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Reflection tokens that decide when to retrieve and critique output.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r21","title":"Retrieval Augmented Generation or Long-Context LLMs? A Comprehensive Study and Hybrid Approach","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.16833","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Cost and accuracy trade-off and the Self-Route router.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r22","title":"Don't Do RAG: When Cache-Augmented Generation is All You Need for Knowledge Tasks","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.15605","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Preloading a small corpus and caching KV state instead of retrieving.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r23","title":"NoLiMa: Long-Context Evaluation Beyond Literal Matching","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.05167","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Accuracy of long-context models falls at 32K tokens when literal matches disappear.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r24","title":"Lost in the Middle: How Language Models Use Long Contexts","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Position effects: evidence in the middle of the context is used least.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r25","title":"Context Rot: How Increasing Input Tokens Impacts LLM Performance","url":"https://www.trychroma.com/research/context-rot","type":"blog","publisher":"Chroma Research","note":"Reliability of 18 LLMs as input grows, reported 2025-07-14.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r26","title":"Introducing Contextual Retrieval","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/contextual-retrieval","type":"blog","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Failure-rate numbers for contextual embeddings, BM25 and reranking.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r27","title":"Citations","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/citations","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"API-native citations, citation types and the structured-output incompatibility.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r28","title":"Query rewriting in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/semantic-how-to-query-rewrite","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Generative query rewrites, their request shape and the warning about exact identifiers.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r29","title":"Create a search index knowledge source","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/agentic-knowledge-source-how-to-search-index","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Query hints, base filters and why generated filters are not an authorization boundary.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r30","title":"File search and Retrieval guides","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/retrieval","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Result counts, rewrite_query, ranking_options, score_threshold and hybrid weights.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r31","title":"Cohere models overview","url":"https://docs.cohere.com/docs/models","type":"doc","publisher":"Cohere","note":"Rerank model names and context lengths.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r32","title":"Reciprocal rank fusion in Elasticsearch","url":"https://www.elastic.co/docs/reference/elasticsearch/rest-apis/reciprocal-rank-fusion","type":"doc","publisher":"Elastic","note":"A second engine's RRF defaults: rank_constant and rank_window_size.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r33","title":"Azure OpenAI On Your Data (deprecated)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/openai/concepts/use-your-data","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Retirement date and the recommended move to Foundry IQ.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"deprecated"},{"id":"r34","title":"Migrate agentic retrieval code to the latest version","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/agentic-retrieval-how-to-migrate","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Version-by-version breaking changes and the stable versus preview split.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r35","title":"Create a vector query in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/vector-search-how-to-query","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Vector similarity threshold and filters (threshold is preview).","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r36","title":"Develop generative AI apps on Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/paths/develop-generative-ai-apps/","type":"course","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Learning path that covers grounding with RAG and responsible AI.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r37","title":"Advanced Retrieval for AI with Chroma","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/advanced-retrieval-for-ai","type":"course","publisher":"DeepLearning.AI","note":"Query expansion, cross-encoder reranking and embedding adaptors.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r38","title":"Azure AI Search service limits","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-limits-quotas-capacity","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Agentic retrieval runtime and knowledge source limits.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r39","title":"Create a knowledge base in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/agentic-retrieval-how-to-create-knowledge-base","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Planner model list, output budget and billing split.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r40","title":"GraphRAG documentation","url":"https://microsoft.github.io/graphrag/","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Research","note":"Indexing methods, query modes (Global, Local, DRIFT, Basic) and configuration.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r41","title":"Reduce hallucinations","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-hallucinations","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Permission to say I don't know and quote-first prompting for long documents.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r42","title":"Create a semantic query in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/semantic-how-to-query-request","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Request shape, and the orderBy and empty-search incompatibilities with semantic ranking.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r43","title":"Hybrid search overview in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/hybrid-search-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"When keyword search wins (codes, jargon, dates, names) and why hybrid plus semantic ranking is the default.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr1","title":"SPLADE: Sparse Lexical and Expansion Model for First Stage Ranking","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.05720","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Learned sparse retrieval with sparsity regularisation and log saturation, servable from an inverted index.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr2","title":"Simple Entity-Centric Questions Challenge Dense Retrievers","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.08535","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Evidence that dense retrievers underperform sparse methods on rare entities.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr3","title":"Interleaving Retrieval with Chain-of-Thought Reasoning for Knowledge-Intensive Multi-Step Questions (IRCoT)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.10509","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Iterative retrieval for multi-hop questions, with measured retrieval and QA gains.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr4","title":"RAG-Fusion: a New Take on Retrieval-Augmented Generation","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.03367","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Multiple generated queries fused with reciprocal rank fusion, with an honest note on off-topic drift.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr5","title":"BM25 Relevance Scoring in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/index-similarity-and-scoring","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"BM25 as the fixed algorithm, unbounded scores, ties, per-replica statistics and unscored query forms.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr6","title":"Configure BM25 relevance scoring in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/index-ranking-similarity","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The k1 and b parameters with defaults 1.2 and 0.75 and what they control.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr7","title":"Retrieve and Re-Rank (Sentence Transformers documentation)","url":"https://sbert.net/examples/sentence_transformer/applications/retrieve_rerank/README.html","type":"doc","publisher":"Sentence Transformers","note":"Plain explanation of bi-encoder retrieval followed by cross-encoder re-ranking of a candidate set.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr8","title":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Large Language Models: A Survey","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.10997","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Defines the Naive, Advanced and Modular RAG paradigms.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr9","title":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"The original RAG paper: a generator paired with a dense index of Wikipedia.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr10","title":"Enabling Large Language Models to Generate Text with Citations (ALCE)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14627","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Benchmark and metrics for citation quality, with the finding that citation support is often incomplete.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr11","title":"ColBERT: Efficient and Effective Passage Search via Contextualized Late Interaction over BERT","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.12832","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"The original late-interaction design: independent encodings plus a cheap token-level interaction step.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr12","title":"File search tool (OpenAI API guide)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/tools-file-search","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"file_citation annotations and the include option that returns raw search results.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"}],"glossary":[{"term":"BM25","def":"A lexical ranking function over an inverted index. It scores term overlap with length normalisation and has no upper score bound."},{"term":"Dense retrieval","def":"Nearest-neighbour search over embedding vectors. It matches meaning across different wording and can blur exact tokens."},{"term":"Learned sparse retrieval","def":"A trained model assigns weights to vocabulary terms, so an inverted index can still serve the query (SPLADE style)."},{"term":"Late interaction","def":"Query and document are encoded separately into token vectors and compared with a cheap token-level step (ColBERT style). The index is much larger."},{"term":"Hybrid search","def":"Run a keyword query and one or more vector queries in parallel, then fuse the ranked lists into one."},{"term":"RRF","def":"Reciprocal rank fusion. A document scores `1 / (k + rank)` per list, optionally times a weight, and the scores are summed."},{"term":"Convex combination","def":"Fusion by a weighted sum of normalised scores, with weights that add to 1. It needs comparable scores and a little tuning data."},{"term":"Cross-encoder","def":"A model that reads the query and one passage together and outputs a relevance score. It reranks a shortlist because it is too slow for the whole corpus."},{"term":"Semantic ranker","def":"Azure AI Search's managed reranker. It rescores the top 50 results and returns `@search.rerankerScore` from 0 to 4."},{"term":"HyDE","def":"Hypothetical document embeddings. An LLM writes a fake answer, and the system retrieves real documents near its embedding."},{"term":"Parent-child retrieval","def":"Match small child chunks for precision, then return the larger parent passage for context."},{"term":"GraphRAG","def":"RAG over an LLM-extracted entity graph with hierarchical community summaries, aimed at corpus-wide questions."},{"term":"LazyGraphRAG","def":"A Microsoft Research variant that defers LLM work to query time, so indexing cost matches vector RAG."},{"term":"RAPTOR","def":"A tree of recursively clustered and summarised chunks that lets retrieval pick the abstraction level."},{"term":"Agentic retrieval","def":"An Azure AI Search pipeline in which an LLM plans subqueries over knowledge sources, runs them in parallel, reranks and merges the results."},{"term":"Abstention","def":"The system declines to answer, or says it found nothing, when evidence is missing or weak."},{"term":"CAG","def":"Cache-augmented generation. Preload a small corpus into the context and reuse the cached KV state instead of retrieving per query."},{"term":"Inverted index","def":"A map from each term to the documents that contain it. It serves BM25 and learned sparse retrieval."},{"term":"SPLADE","def":"A learned sparse retrieval model that assigns weights to terms, including expansion terms, with sparsity regularisation so an inverted index can serve it."},{"term":"Bi-encoder","def":"A model that embeds query and document separately, so document vectors can be precomputed and searched fast. Used for first-stage dense retrieval."},{"term":"Recall@k","def":"The share of questions for which at least one gold chunk appears in the top k results. It is the ceiling for answer quality in RAG."},{"term":"Candidate window","def":"The shortlist passed to a reranker. In Azure AI Search the semantic ranker receives the top 50 results."},{"term":"Query rewriting","def":"Cleaning, expanding or restating a query, often using chat history, so the index can match it. It can also drift from the original meaning."},{"term":"Multi-query","def":"Generating several variants of a question, retrieving for each and fusing the lists, commonly with RRF."},{"term":"Step-back prompting","def":"Asking the model for a higher-level concept or principle before reasoning. In RAG it adds an abstract question beside the specific one."},{"term":"Query decomposition","def":"Splitting a compound question into sub-questions. Independent parts can run in parallel."},{"term":"Multi-hop retrieval","def":"Retrieval where each search depends on what earlier searches found, as in IRCoT. It needs hop, budget and sufficiency limits."},{"term":"Self-query","def":"A model turns a natural-language question into a semantic query plus a structured metadata filter. The filter must be validated and never replaces authorization."},{"term":"Query hints","def":"In Azure agentic retrieval, `searchIndexParameters.queryHints` stores filter hints and boosts that guide the query-planning model on a search index knowledge source. Best effort, preview."},{"term":"CRAG","def":"Corrective RAG: a lightweight retrieval evaluator scores document quality and triggers actions such as web fallback and decompose-then-recompose filtering."},{"term":"Self-RAG","def":"A fine-tuned language model that decides when to retrieve and critiques passages and its own output with reflection tokens."},{"term":"Knowledge base","def":"In Azure AI Search agentic retrieval, the object that holds knowledge sources and a retrieval reasoning effort, and answers the retrieve action."},{"term":"Knowledge source","def":"A connection a knowledge base searches, indexed (search index, Blob, OneLake and others) or remote (web, MCP server and others)."},{"term":"Reasoning effort","def":"The retrieval planning level in agentic retrieval: `minimal`, `low`, `medium` or `auto`. Higher levels add planning, a classifier and retries."},{"term":"Foundry IQ","def":"Microsoft's managed knowledge layer: knowledge bases that many agents can share, built on Azure AI Search, with permission-aware retrieval."},{"term":"Self-Route","def":"A router that sends each query to RAG or long context based on model self-reflection, aiming for long-context accuracy at lower cost."},{"term":"Context rot","def":"Declining reliability of language models as input length grows, even on simple tasks."},{"term":"Oracle test","def":"Put the gold chunk in the prompt. A wrong answer then points to generation, and a right answer points to retrieval, ranking, filters or context assembly."},{"term":"Entailment check","def":"Testing whether a cited source span supports a claim, using an NLI model or an LLM judge. A citation alone proves provenance, not support."}],"levels":[{"id":"w05-l1","order":1,"title":"Keywords, vectors or both?","kind":"problem","minutes":14,"brief":"One search box receives an error code, a product name and a paraphrased policy question in the same hour. Which retriever do you trust first, and what does each one lose?","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Four ways to find a chunk","level":2,"id":"four-ways-to-find-a-chunk"},{"t":"p","md":"A retriever turns a query into a ranked list. **Lexical** retrieval (BM25) matches the words. **Dense** retrieval matches meaning through embeddings. **Learned sparse** retrieval (SPLADE style) lets a model choose which vocabulary terms matter, so an inverted index can still serve it. **Late interaction** (ColBERT style) compares token vectors, which gains accuracy and inflates the index by roughly an order of magnitude, so ColBERTv2 adds residual compression [[r12]].\n\nThe evidence is mixed on purpose. On the BEIR benchmark of 18 datasets, BM25 is a reliable baseline out of domain, re-ranking and late-interaction models give the best zero-shot average at high compute cost, and dense and sparse retrievers are cheaper but often weaker out of domain [[r11]]. Azure AI Search says product codes, specialised jargon, dates and names do better with keyword search, and that hybrid plus the semantic ranker gives significant relevance gains, which is a vendor claim you should test on your own queries [[r43]]."},{"t":"compare","title":"Retrieval modes","intro":"Each mode fails on a different query class. The fusion level that follows is how you combine them.","options":[{"name":"BM25 keyword","tag":"GA","summary":"Score term overlap over an inverted index.","pros":["Exact identifiers, names and dates match","Cheap, no embedding call per query","Explainable: you can show which terms matched"],"cons":["No match when the query and the page share no words","Scores are unbounded, so they do not mix with cosine scores"],"bestWhen":"Queries are codes, names or jargon, or the corpus is far from your embedding model's training data.","avoidWhen":"Users paraphrase and the best pages use different vocabulary.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"lowest"},{"k":"Out-of-domain","v":"strong baseline"}]},{"name":"Dense vector","tag":"GA","summary":"Embed query and chunks, return nearest neighbours.","pros":["Handles paraphrase and vocabulary mismatch","Works across languages when the model does"],"cons":["Rare tokens and identifiers blur into look-alikes","Often weaker out of domain","Re-embedding is needed when you change the model"],"bestWhen":"Questions are natural language and the embedding model fits the domain.","avoidWhen":"Exact strings decide relevance.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"low to medium"},{"k":"Ops","v":"embedding model and vector index"}]},{"name":"Hybrid (BM25 plus vector, fused)","tag":"GA","summary":"Run both in parallel and merge the ranked lists, usually with RRF.","pros":["Each list covers the other's blind spot","One engine can serve both in Azure AI Search"],"cons":["Two retrievals per query","A noisy list can drag the fused order unless you weight it"],"bestWhen":"The query mix is unknown or mixed, which is the normal case.","avoidWhen":"Every query is one class and you have proved a single retriever is enough.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"medium"},{"k":"Default for","v":"mixed query traffic"}]},{"name":"Learned sparse or late interaction","tag":"Other engine","summary":"SPLADE-style term weights or ColBERT-style token matching as an add-on.","pros":["Learned term weights keep term-level matching","Late interaction is among the best zero-shot systems in BEIR"],"cons":["The Azure AI Search vector pages checked on 2026-10-13 do not document either, so it means another engine and index","Late-interaction indexes are much larger"],"bestWhen":"You own the engine, have the infrastructure and a measured gap that hybrid plus reranking cannot close.","avoidWhen":"You want one managed service and a short path to production.","meta":[{"k":"Index size","v":"larger"},{"k":"Ops","v":"highest"}]}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w05-l1-mode","title":"Pick the first-stage retrieval mode","prompt":"Flip the query mix, the domain fit and your operating budget. The ranking and the reasons update.","dials":[{"id":"qmix","label":"Query mix","values":[{"id":"ids","label":"Identifiers, names, codes"},{"id":"para","label":"Paraphrased questions"},{"id":"mixed","label":"Both in one query"}]},{"id":"shift","label":"Domain fit","values":[{"id":"fit","label":"Embeddings fit the domain"},{"id":"jargon","label":"Jargon heavy, out of domain"}]},{"id":"ops","label":"Operating budget","values":[{"id":"lean","label":"One managed engine"},{"id":"rich","label":"Extra indexes or GPUs are fine"}]}],"options":[{"id":"bm25","name":"BM25 only","summary":"Keyword retrieval over an inverted index."},{"id":"dense","name":"Dense only","summary":"Vector nearest neighbours."},{"id":"hybrid","name":"Hybrid with RRF","summary":"BM25 and vector lists fused by rank."},{"id":"hybridrr","name":"Hybrid plus reranker","summary":"Fuse, then rescore the shortlist with a reranker."},{"id":"late","name":"Late interaction or learned sparse","summary":"ColBERT or SPLADE style retrieval on a separate engine."}],"scores":{"bm25":{"qmix":{"ids":{"s":2,"why":"Exact tokens match, and no embedding blurs the identifier."},"para":{"s":-2,"why":"No shared words means no match."},"mixed":{"s":0,"why":"It finds the identifier half and misses the paraphrase half."}},"shift":{"fit":{"s":0,"why":"A well-fitting embedding model does not help a lexical index."},"jargon":{"s":1,"why":"BM25 is a dependable out-of-domain baseline in BEIR."}},"ops":{"lean":{"s":2,"why":"An inverted index needs no embedding calls."},"rich":{"s":0,"why":"Extra budget buys nothing on its own here."}}},"dense":{"qmix":{"ids":{"s":-2,"why":"Identifiers embed near look-alike strings, so an exact hit can sit at rank 9."},"para":{"s":2,"why":"Meaning matches across different wording."},"mixed":{"s":0,"why":"It gets the paraphrase and can bury the identifier."}},"shift":{"fit":{"s":2,"why":"A tuned or well-matched model is where dense retrieval shines."},"jargon":{"s":-1,"why":"Dense models often lose out of domain in BEIR."}},"ops":{"lean":{"s":0,"why":"It needs an embedding call per query plus a vector index."},"rich":{"s":1,"why":"You can afford larger models and re-embedding."}}},"hybrid":{"qmix":{"ids":{"s":1,"why":"The BM25 list keeps the exact hit in the fused set."},"para":{"s":1,"why":"The vector list leads and fusion mutes the keyword noise."},"mixed":{"s":2,"why":"Each list covers one half of the query."}},"shift":{"fit":{"s":1,"why":"Fusion still helps and costs little."},"jargon":{"s":2,"why":"Two independent signals hedge the domain shift."}},"ops":{"lean":{"s":1,"why":"One managed service can run both legs, at the price of two retrievals."},"rich":{"s":1,"why":"No extra infrastructure needed, so budget adds little."}}},"hybridrr":{"qmix":{"ids":{"s":1,"why":"Keyword leg finds it, the reranker confirms the order."},"para":{"s":2,"why":"The reranker reads full text and repairs the first stage order."},"mixed":{"s":2,"why":"Fusion keeps both halves and the reranker sorts them."}},"shift":{"fit":{"s":0,"why":"The gain is smaller when the first stage already fits."},"jargon":{"s":2,"why":"Re-ranking has the best zero-shot average in BEIR."}},"ops":{"lean":{"s":-1,"why":"It adds a model hop, a cost line and a second latency to watch."},"rich":{"s":2,"why":"You can afford the extra stage and measure it."}}},"late":{"qmix":{"ids":{"s":1,"why":"Learned sparse keeps term-level matching with learned weights."},"para":{"s":1,"why":"Token-level interaction handles wording changes."},"mixed":{"s":1,"why":"It covers both halves, but you pay with a second engine."}},"shift":{"fit":{"s":0,"why":"No special advantage when embeddings already fit."},"jargon":{"s":1,"why":"Late interaction scores well zero-shot in BEIR."}},"ops":{"lean":{"s":-2,"why":"Not documented in the Azure AI Search vector pages read on 2026-10-13, so it means another engine."},"rich":{"s":1,"why":"You can host the larger index and the extra service."}}}},"takeaway":"Hybrid is the default for mixed traffic because it hedges, not because it always wins. Say which query class drove your pick and how you would measure it."},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes by retrieval mode","items":[{"symptom":"The runbook for an exact error code is at rank 9 and the answer is generic.","cause":"Dense-only retrieval blurs identifiers into similar-looking strings.","fix":"Add a BM25 leg and fuse. Detect it with identifier probe queries and recall@k by query class."},{"symptom":"Paraphrased questions return pages that share one stray word.","cause":"BM25-only retrieval cannot bridge a vocabulary gap.","fix":"Add a vector leg or a query rewrite. Detect it with a paraphrase slice in the eval set."},{"symptom":"Hybrid scores worse than BM25 on one query class.","cause":"Equal weights let a noisy list drag the fused order.","fix":"Weight the legs, or tune a convex combination. Detect it by splitting metrics by query type."},{"symptom":"Quality was fine on the pilot and is weak on a new product line full of new jargon.","cause":"The embedding model is out of domain for the new vocabulary.","fix":"Keep the BM25 leg, then test a domain-fitted or adapted embedding. Compare to the BM25 baseline on a labelled set."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What interviewers listen for","md":"Name the **query mix** and the **vocabulary gap** before you name a mode. Then say how you would prove the choice: recall@k and nDCG on a labelled set, sliced by identifier, paraphrase and mixed queries."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-l1-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":1,"q":"Users report that searching for an exact error code returns related runbooks but not the one for that code. Which cause fits this log best?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"query: ERR_4021\nretriever: dense (cosine), k=10\nrank 1: ERR_4012 runbook   0.84\nrank 2: ERR_4201 runbook   0.83\nrank 3: timeout settings   0.82\n...\nrank 9: ERR_4021 runbook   0.74"},"options":["The chunks are too large for the embedding model to represent them well","Dense-only retrieval blurs identifiers, so a keyword leg is missing","The top-k value is set too high","The model temperature is too high"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct runbook is present at rank 9 with a close score, which is the signature of embedding blur on identifiers. A BM25 leg would rank it first. Chunk size does not explain look-alike neighbours, a larger k only widens the list, and temperature acts at generation, after retrieval."},{"id":"w05-l1-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":2,"q":"Select every condition that argues for keeping a BM25 leg next to the vector leg.","options":["Many queries are identifiers, names, codes or dates","The corpus is jargon heavy and the embedding model is general purpose","Queries are long paraphrases that share no vocabulary with the documents","Reviewers need to see which query terms matched a hit"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Exact tokens, out-of-domain jargon and explainable matches are the lexical strengths. Pure paraphrase with no shared vocabulary is where BM25 fails and the vector leg carries the query, so it argues for the vector leg, not for keeping BM25 on its own."},{"id":"w05-l1-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","difficulty":2,"q":"Match each retriever to the cost or blind spot you plan around.","pairs":[{"l":"BM25","r":"Vocabulary mismatch when no query words appear in the page"},{"l":"Dense retrieval","r":"Exact identifiers blur into similar-looking strings"},{"l":"Learned sparse (SPLADE style)","r":"Needs a trained term-weighting model and a sparse index"},{"l":"Late interaction (ColBERT style)","r":"Index size grows by about an order of magnitude without compression"}],"explain":"Each mode pays for its strength somewhere else. ColBERTv2 adds residual compression because token-level vectors inflate the index, and SPLADE keeps an inverted index but depends on a trained model."}],"defend":null,"refs":["r43","r1","r8","r11","r12"],"takeaways":["Lexical, dense, learned sparse and late interaction each fail on a different query class","BM25 stays a strong out-of-domain baseline, so measure dense retrieval against it","Hybrid is the default for mixed traffic because it hedges, not because it always wins","Late interaction and learned sparse are not in the Azure AI Search vector pages read on 2026-10-13"]},{"id":"w05-l2","order":2,"title":"Fuse the lists without tuning a dozen knobs","kind":"lab","minutes":16,"brief":"Two ranked lists disagree. One speaks BM25 and one speaks cosine, and their scores sit on different scales. How do you merge them, and when is the zero-tuning default the wrong choice?","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Why ranks travel better than scores","level":2,"id":"why-ranks-travel-better-than-scores"},{"t":"p","md":"BM25 `@search.score` has no upper limit. Cosine similarity in Azure AI Search runs from 0.333 to 1.00. The semantic ranker reports `@search.rerankerScore` from 0 to 4 after fusion, separately from both [[r1]]. Adding raw numbers across those scales lets whichever list has bigger numbers win.\n\n**Reciprocal rank fusion** sidesteps the problem by using only the rank each list gives a document. Azure AI Search applies it whenever two or more queries run in parallel: a text query plus one vector query is 2 executions, one vector query on two vector fields makes 3, and two vector queries over five vector fields make 11 [[r1]]. Cormack, Clarke and Buettcher fixed `k = 60` in a pilot on TREC data and reported that the exact choice was not critical [[r9]]."},{"t":"formula","title":"Reciprocal rank fusion with optional weights","expr":"RRF(d) = sum over lists i of  w_i / (k + rank_i(d))","vars":[{"k":"rank_i(d)","v":"position of document d in list i, starting at 1 (a missing document adds 0)"},{"k":"k","v":"a constant, 60 in the original paper. It is not the neighbour count `k` of a vector query"},{"k":"w_i","v":"per-list weight, default 1.0. In Azure it is `vectorQueries[].weight`"}],"example":"At `k = 60`, rank 1 contributes 1/61 = 0.0164 and rank 50 contributes 1/110 = 0.0091. Two lists can give at most 2/61 = **0.0328**, which is why fused scores look tiny. Only their order matters."},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"RRF constant k","value":"60","note":"Cormack 2009 pilot and the Elasticsearch rank_constant default. Azure says a small value such as 60, and the exact value it uses is not stated."},{"label":"Default hybrid result count","value":"50","note":"Top 50 of the fused set when you do not page."},{"label":"maxTextRecallSize default","value":"1,000","note":"Preview parameter under hybridSearch, 2026-08-01-preview."},{"label":"Default vector query weight","value":"1.0","note":"The Azure example uses 0.5 and 2.0 to bias one query."}]},{"t":"lab","id":"rrf-playground","intro":"Load the three presets (exact identifier, conceptual paraphrase, mixed query), then run the guided experiments under What to notice. On the mixed query, document D10 is first in the vector list only: watch where it lands at k = 1, 60 and 200."},{"t":"compare","title":"Fusion functions","intro":"Pick the function by what you can measure, not by what is fashionable.","options":[{"name":"Reciprocal rank fusion","tag":"GA in Azure AI Search","summary":"Sum of weight / (k + rank) over lists.","pros":["Needs no score normalisation","Sensible with zero labelled queries","Scales to many lists"],"cons":["Throws away score gaps: a runaway first hit and a near tie look alike","Bruch et al. found it sensitive to its parameters"],"bestWhen":"You have no labels yet, the lists use different scales, or you fuse several vector queries.","avoidWhen":"Score gaps carry signal and you have labelled queries to tune against.","meta":[{"k":"Tuning","v":"k and weights"},{"k":"Labels needed","v":"none to start"}]},{"name":"Convex combination of normalised scores","tag":"Research","summary":"Normalise each list's scores, then take a weighted sum with weights adding to 1.","pros":["Beat RRF in and out of domain in Bruch et al.","Needed only a small labelled set","Weight is directly interpretable"],"cons":["Normalisation choice matters, and one outlier score can squash a list","Needs scores you can trust, so a labelled check is mandatory"],"bestWhen":"You have a few hundred labelled queries and stable scores.","avoidWhen":"Scores are not exposed by the engine or you cannot label anything.","meta":[{"k":"Tuning","v":"one weight plus normaliser"},{"k":"Labels needed","v":"small set"}]},{"name":"Fuse, then rerank","tag":"GA","summary":"Use any cheap fusion for recall, then let a reranker decide the final order.","pros":["Reranker reads full text, so fusion quality matters less","Fits the funnel of the next level"],"cons":["Adds a model hop and cost","Only as good as the candidate set"],"bestWhen":"Latency allows one more stage and precision at the top matters.","avoidWhen":"The budget is a single fast query.","meta":[{"k":"Extra stage","v":"yes"},{"k":"Cost","v":"per query"}]}]},{"t":"failure","title":"Fusion failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"Fused results look like the vector list only.","cause":"Raw scores were summed or min-max normalised, and one BM25 outlier squashed the keyword list toward 0.","fix":"Fuse by rank, or normalise with a method that resists outliers. Replay a query with a runaway score as a regression test."},{"symptom":"A document first in one list sits below documents found by both.","cause":"With k = 60 agreement outweighs a single top rank. That is the design, not a bug.","fix":"If top-rank trust is wrong for your data, lower k or raise that list's weight, and measure the change."},{"symptom":"Raising `k` in a hybrid request does not change how ranks are weighted in fusion.","cause":"The vector query `k` is the neighbour count, not the RRF constant, which has no documented request parameter.","fix":"Tune the per-query `weight` and `maxTextRecallSize` instead, and name the two k values separately in reviews."},{"symptom":"Recall drops after adding a weak third list.","cause":"Each extra list adds noise and its own top-rank points at equal weight.","fix":"Weight it below the others, or remove it. Slice recall by query class before and after."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Azure details that trip people up","md":"Weights are GA. `maxTextRecallSize` is a **preview** parameter (2026-08-01-preview, checked 2026-10-13). The vector `threshold` parameter is preview too and drops weak matches before fusion even if fewer than `k` remain [[r35]]. Elasticsearch exposes the constant as `rank_constant` with a default of 60 and a `rank_window_size` that defaults to the request size [[r32]]. OpenAI file search takes `embedding_weight` and `text_weight` under `ranking_options.hybrid_search` [[r30]]."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-l2-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","difficulty":1,"q":"With `k = 60` and equal weights, a document is rank 4 in the keyword list and rank 2 in the vector list. What is its RRF score?","answer":0.0318,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"RRF score","hint":"1/(60 + 4) + 1/(60 + 2)","explain":"1/64 = 0.015625 and 1/62 = 0.016129, so the sum is 0.031754, about 0.0318. Ranks start at 1 and the constant is added to the rank, so using rank 3 and 1 as zero-based positions gives a different, wrong number."},{"id":"w05-l2-q2","type":"single","tag":"flip","difficulty":2,"q":"Document A is rank 1 in one list only. Document B is rank 10 in both lists. Which setting makes A outscore B?","options":["k = 60","k = 1","k = 200","No value of k"],"answer":1,"explain":"At k = 1, A scores 1/2 = 0.5 and B scores 2/11 = 0.18, so A wins. At k = 60, A is 0.0164 and B is 0.0286, and at k = 200 A is 0.0050 and B is 0.0095, so agreement wins. Small k trusts top ranks and large k trusts agreement, so a value of k does exist."},{"id":"w05-l2-q3","type":"single","tag":"bug","difficulty":2,"q":"A team reports that their hybrid results are almost identical to BM25 alone. What is wrong with this fusion code?","code":{"lang":"python","code":"def fuse(keyword_hits, vector_hits):\n    scores = {}\n    for doc_id, score in keyword_hits + vector_hits:\n        scores[doc_id] = scores.get(doc_id, 0) + score\n    return sorted(scores, key=scores.get, reverse=True)"},"options":["It sums raw scores from different scales, so the larger scale dominates","It should sort ascending, because a lower fused score means a better match","It must divide each total by the number of lists before sorting the documents","It should start from a default of 1 instead of 0 for documents missing from a list"],"answer":0,"explain":"BM25 scores are unbounded and cosine scores sit between 0.333 and 1.00, so adding them lets BM25 decide. Fix it with rank-based fusion or normalised scores. Sorting ascending would invert relevance, dividing by the list count rescales every document the same, and a default of 1 adds a constant to every missing list entry."},{"id":"w05-l2-q4","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":3,"q":"Select every situation in which a tuned convex combination is a better bet than RRF.","options":["A small labelled query set is available to tune the weight","There are no labels and no time to create any","Your own evaluation shows RRF results swing when k or the weights change","You will not normalise scores and the engine does not expose them"],"answers":[0,2],"explain":"Bruch et al. found convex combination beat RRF and needed only a small labelled set, and they found RRF sensitive to its parameters, so evidence of that on your data points the same way. With no labels you cannot tune the weight, and with unexposed or unnormalised scores convex combination cannot run, so RRF is the fallback."}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r2","r9","r10","r30","r32","r35"],"takeaways":["RRF fuses ranks, so BM25 and cosine never need to share a scale","Small k trusts top ranks and large k trusts agreement between lists","The RRF constant and the vector query k are different things","Convex combination can win when you have a small labelled set, and RRF is the zero-label default"]},{"id":"w05-l3","order":3,"title":"Rerank the shortlist, set the cutoff, know when to abstain","kind":"problem","minutes":16,"brief":"Hybrid search returned fifty plausible chunks and the answer needs three. Who decides the order, where do you cut, and what does the system do when nothing is good enough?","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"A funnel, not a ranking","level":2,"id":"a-funnel-not-a-ranking"},{"t":"diagram","id":"w05-l3-funnel","title":"The retrieval funnel","alt":"A query goes through recall with BM25 and vector search, fusion with RRF, reranking of the top 50, and a good-enough check that either generates with citations or abstains.","src":"flowchart LR\n  Q[Query] --> R[Recall: BM25 and vector]\n  R --> F[Fuse with RRF]\n  F --> RR[Rerank top 50]\n  RR --> C{Good enough?}\n  C -->|yes| G[Generate with citations]\n  C -->|no| A[Abstain or fall back]","caption":"Recall decides what can be found. The reranker only reorders what recall supplied."},{"t":"compare","title":"Rerankers","intro":"A reranker reads the query and each candidate together, so it can judge relevance that embeddings and term counts miss. Late-interaction models (ColBERT style) sit between: they keep token vectors per document and score a cheap interaction, so they can retrieve or rerank at an index size cost [[r12]].","options":[{"name":"Azure semantic ranker","tag":"GA","summary":"Managed reranker inside Azure AI Search.","pros":["One request, no extra service to run","Returns `@search.rerankerScore` (0 to 4), captions and optional answers"],"cons":["Reranks only the top 50 and cannot find new documents","About 2,000 tokens per document reach summarisation, and the summary string is capped at 2,048 tokens","`orderBy` with semantic ranking returns HTTP 400, and an empty or `*` search string is incompatible with it [[r42]]"],"bestWhen":"You already use Azure AI Search and want a managed precision stage.","avoidWhen":"Candidates must be scored beyond 50 or the text cannot leave your own hardware.","meta":[{"k":"Billing","v":"premium feature, pay as you go on the standard plan"},{"k":"Input","v":"about 2,000 tokens per document"}]},{"name":"Hosted rerank API","tag":"GA","summary":"Send the query and candidates to a vendor model such as Cohere Rerank.","pros":["Strong multilingual models","Long context: rerank-v4.0 models take 32k tokens and rerank-v3.5 takes 4096","Accepts structured documents, and Cohere recommends YAML strings"],"cons":["Candidates leave your service boundary","Adds a network hop and a per-query cost"],"bestWhen":"You need long or structured candidates and data may go to a vendor.","avoidWhen":"Residency or egress rules forbid sending chunk text out.","meta":[{"k":"Output","v":"index and relevance_score"},{"k":"Data path","v":"external API"}]},{"name":"Self-hosted cross-encoder","tag":"Open","summary":"Run a cross-encoder model on your own GPU or CPU.","pros":["You control data path, model choice and fine-tuning","No per-call vendor fee"],"cons":["You operate a model service and its scaling","Input length is limited by the model, so long chunks need splitting"],"bestWhen":"Data must stay in your network or you want to fine-tune on your own judgements.","avoidWhen":"You cannot staff a model service and a managed option meets the need.","meta":[{"k":"Ops","v":"yours"},{"k":"Data path","v":"internal"}]},{"name":"LLM reranker","tag":"Research","summary":"Prompt a GPT-class model to order a window of candidates (RankGPT style).","pros":["Properly instructed LLMs gave competitive and sometimes better ranking than supervised rerankers on standard benchmarks","Flexible: add domain rules in the prompt"],"cons":["An extra model call per candidate window, so the slowest and costliest","Benchmark contamination is a risk, which is why the authors built NovelEval"],"bestWhen":"Latency is relaxed and ranking quality is the top concern.","avoidWhen":"You need a tight latency budget or high query volume.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"highest"},{"k":"Cost","v":"highest"}]}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w05-l3-rerank","title":"Pick a reranking stage","prompt":"Flip the latency budget, the allowed data path and the length of your chunks.","dials":[{"id":"latency","label":"Latency budget","values":[{"id":"tight","label":"Tight"},{"id":"relaxed","label":"Relaxed"}]},{"id":"path","label":"Where the text may go","values":[{"id":"service","label":"Stay inside the search service"},{"id":"vendor","label":"A vendor API is fine"},{"id":"own","label":"Only our own hardware"}]},{"id":"text","label":"Candidate length","values":[{"id":"short","label":"Short, focused chunks"},{"id":"long","label":"Long or structured chunks"}]}],"options":[{"id":"none","name":"No reranker","summary":"Use the fused order."},{"id":"semantic","name":"Azure semantic ranker","summary":"Managed rerank of the top 50."},{"id":"hosted","name":"Hosted rerank API","summary":"Vendor rerank model over the candidates."},{"id":"selfhost","name":"Self-hosted cross-encoder","summary":"Your own model service."},{"id":"llm","name":"LLM reranker","summary":"A prompted model orders candidate windows."}],"scores":{"none":{"latency":{"tight":{"s":2,"why":"No extra hop."},"relaxed":{"s":0,"why":"Spare latency is left unused, and precision stays on the table."}},"path":{"service":{"s":2,"why":"Nothing leaves the service."},"vendor":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"own":{"s":1,"why":"Nothing extra to host."}},"text":{"short":{"s":0,"why":"Fusion order is often acceptable for short, focused chunks."},"long":{"s":-1,"why":"Long mixed chunks stay ordered by coarse signals."}}},"semantic":{"latency":{"tight":{"s":1,"why":"It runs in the service on the top 50 within the same request."},"relaxed":{"s":1,"why":"Adds precision at little cost."}},"path":{"service":{"s":2,"why":"Candidates never leave the search service."},"vendor":{"s":1,"why":"It is a managed Azure feature, so no extra vendor contract."},"own":{"s":-2,"why":"You cannot move it onto your own hardware."}},"text":{"short":{"s":1,"why":"Short chunks fit the 2,048-token summary budget."},"long":{"s":0,"why":"About 2,000 tokens per document reach summarisation, so very long chunks are cut off."}}},"hosted":{"latency":{"tight":{"s":0,"why":"A network hop plus model time."},"relaxed":{"s":1,"why":"The hop is affordable."}},"path":{"service":{"s":-2,"why":"Candidates leave the service boundary."},"vendor":{"s":2,"why":"A hosted API is the intended path."},"own":{"s":-2,"why":"It is not self-hosted."}},"text":{"short":{"s":1,"why":"Fine for short chunks."},"long":{"s":2,"why":"Cohere rerank-v4.0 models take 32k tokens of context and rerank-v3.5 takes 4096."}}},"selfhost":{"latency":{"tight":{"s":1,"why":"A small cross-encoder near the index avoids the vendor hop."},"relaxed":{"s":0,"why":"Spare latency could fund a bigger model, but you carry the operations."}},"path":{"service":{"s":-1,"why":"You add another service to the request path."},"vendor":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"own":{"s":2,"why":"You control data path, model choice and tuning."}},"text":{"short":{"s":1,"why":"Cross-encoders suit short passages."},"long":{"s":0,"why":"Check the model's maximum input length, and split or truncate long chunks."}}},"llm":{"latency":{"tight":{"s":-2,"why":"At least one extra LLM call per candidate window makes it the slowest option."},"relaxed":{"s":1,"why":"Competitive rankings when latency is spare."}},"path":{"service":{"s":-1,"why":"It needs a model endpoint outside the search service."},"vendor":{"s":1,"why":"It reuses a model API you may already call."},"own":{"s":0,"why":"Possible with an open model, at GPU cost."}},"text":{"short":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but cost per candidate is unchanged."},"long":{"s":1,"why":"Large context windows can read longer passages."}}}},"takeaway":"The reranker choice is mostly a data path and latency decision. The ranking quality gap between the options is smaller than the cost of getting those two wrong."},{"t":"table","caption":"Signals you can cut on","head":["Signal","Scale","How stable it is","How to use it"],"rows":[["Azure `@search.rerankerScore`","0.00 to 4.00","The distribution shifts with infrastructure and model updates [[r3]]","Coarse cutoff only. Track the abstention rate and recalibrate after updates."],["Vector similarity `threshold`","Cosine 0.333 to 1.00","Depends on the embedding model. Preview in 2026-08-01-preview [[r35]]","Drops weak vector matches before fusion, even if fewer than `k` remain."],["RRF score","About 1/k per list","Depends on how many lists and which k, not on relevance [[r1]]","Use for ordering only. Never threshold on it."],["OpenAI file search `score_threshold`","0.0 to 1.0","Depends on the `ranker` (`auto` or `default-2024-08-21`) [[r30]]","Filters retrieved results. Pair with `max_num_results` (default 10, up to 50)."],["Gap to the top score, or top n","Relative","Moves with the model, but the shape is steadier","Keep hits within a margin of the best, capped at n. Author suggestion, calibrate on your eval set."]],"highlightCol":3},{"t":"compare","title":"Confidence gates: what to do when the shortlist is weak","intro":"Abstention is a design, not an error path. Pick the cheapest gate that meets the cost of a wrong answer.","options":[{"name":"Score cutoff","summary":"Abstain when the best reranker score is below a coarse threshold.","pros":["Free at runtime","Easy to monitor as an abstention rate"],"cons":["Brittle when the score distribution drifts","Cannot tell a good partial answer from noise"],"bestWhen":"A wrong answer is costly and you can accept some over-abstention.","avoidWhen":"You have no labelled set to calibrate against."},{"name":"CRAG-style evaluator","tag":"Research","summary":"A lightweight evaluator scores retrieved documents and triggers different actions, such as filtering them by decompose-then-recompose or falling back to web search.","pros":["Plug and play, no model fine-tuning","Gives an explicit fallback path"],"cons":["An extra model call and a fallback source to govern","Web fallback may be forbidden for private data"],"bestWhen":"The corpus is sometimes weak and a safe fallback exists.","avoidWhen":"Answers must come only from approved internal sources."},{"name":"Self-RAG","tag":"Research","summary":"One trained LM decides when to retrieve and critiques passages and its own output with reflection tokens.","pros":["Adaptive: skips retrieval when it is not needed","Critique is built into generation"],"cons":["Needs a fine-tuned model (the paper reports 7B and 13B)","Hard to reproduce with a closed hosted model"],"bestWhen":"You control the model and can train it.","avoidWhen":"You call a hosted general model and cannot fine-tune."},{"name":"Answerability instruction","summary":"Tell the model it may say it does not know, and for long documents ask for word-for-word quotes first.","pros":["No extra stage","Anthropic guidance recommends explicit permission to say I don't know"],"cons":["The model can still over-claim, so it is not a guarantee","Hard to measure without a check on the answer"],"bestWhen":"Used together with a cutoff and a citation check.","avoidWhen":"It is your only guard in a high-risk domain."}]},{"t":"failure","title":"Reranking failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"Adding the semantic ranker changed little, and the best document still never appears.","cause":"It reorders only the top 50 candidates. The document never entered the candidate set.","fix":"Raise recall first: make `k` plus `maxTextRecallSize` at least 50 and check the document's rank before reranking."},{"symptom":"The abstention rate doubled after a service update, with no change on your side.","cause":"A fixed score cutoff met a shifted reranker score distribution.","fix":"Use a coarse cutoff, alert on the abstention rate, and re-run the labelled set after updates."},{"symptom":"A chunk with the right sentence at its end scores low.","cause":"Only about 2,000 tokens per document reach the ranker summary, with the title and keywords taking a share.","fix":"Shorten chunks, move the key facts earlier, and compare scores for a chunk and its sub-chunks."},{"symptom":"A sort option added to the request returns HTTP 400.","cause":"`orderBy` is not allowed with semantic ranking.","fix":"Remove it and sort client side if the product needs a different order."}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-l3-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","difficulty":1,"q":"Put the stages of a confidence-aware retrieval funnel in order.","items":["Retrieve candidates with BM25 and vector search","Fuse the lists with RRF","Rerank the top 50 candidates","Apply a cutoff or abstain","Generate with citations"],"explain":"Recall comes first because later stages only reorder or drop what recall supplies. Fusion merges the lists before the reranker, which then reads full text. The cutoff needs the reranker's judgement and the model answers last."},{"id":"w05-l3-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":3,"q":"After enabling the semantic ranker, a relevant document still never appears. Debug output shows it at BM25 rank 35 and vector rank 40. Which change addresses the cause?","code":{"lang":"json","code":"{\n  \"search\": \"ERR_4021 retry guidance\",\n  \"queryType\": \"semantic\",\n  \"vectorQueries\": [{ \"kind\": \"text\", \"text\": \"ERR_4021 retry guidance\", \"fields\": \"contentVector\", \"k\": 10 }],\n  \"hybridSearch\": { \"maxTextRecallSize\": 20 },\n  \"top\": 10\n}"},"options":["Sort the results by `@search.rerankerScore` with an `orderBy` clause","Lower the reranker score threshold that is applied before generation","Request more extractive captions for each candidate document","Raise `k` and `maxTextRecallSize` to sum to at least 50"],"answer":3,"explain":"With `k` at 10 and `maxTextRecallSize` at 20 the candidate set is at most 30 documents, so rank 35 and 40 never reach the ranker, which takes the top 50. Raise recall, then check again. `orderBy` returns HTTP 400 with semantic ranking, a lower threshold only filters what exists and captions change what is shown, not what is retrieved."},{"id":"w05-l3-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","difficulty":2,"q":"Your abstain cutoff on `@search.rerankerScore` worked at launch. After a service model update the abstention rate doubles. What is the best response?","options":["Hard-code a lower number such as 2.4 and move on without monitoring it","Threshold on the RRF score instead, because it does not change between model updates","Keep a coarse cutoff, alert on the abstention rate and recalibrate after updates","Remove the cutoff so that the system always answers every question"],"answer":2,"explain":"The docs warn that the reranker score distribution shifts with infrastructure and model updates, so use coarse cutoffs and re-test. A new hard-coded number will drift again, the RRF score depends on how many lists and which k you use rather than on relevance, and never abstaining turns weak retrieval into confident wrong answers."}],"defend":null,"refs":["r3","r42","r2","r12","r13","r19","r20","r31","r35","r30"],"takeaways":["A reranker can only reorder the candidates recall gave it, so fix recall first","Choose the reranker by data path and latency before quality","Never threshold on RRF scores, and keep reranker cutoffs coarse because the distribution drifts","Abstention needs a signal, a fallback and a monitored rate"]},{"id":"w05-l4","order":4,"title":"Rewrite the question, resize the chunk","kind":"problem","minutes":15,"brief":"Users type three words, or five questions in one breath, or a follow-up that only makes sense with the last turn. The index expects something else. Do you change the query, the chunk grain, or both?","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Match the grain of the question to the grain of the chunk","level":2,"id":"match-the-grain-of-the-question-to-the-grain-of-the-chunk"},{"t":"table","caption":"Query transformations: what each does and how it fails","head":["Transform","What it does","Helps when","How it fails","Extra cost"],"rows":[["Clean and normalise","Apply to the query the same cleaning you applied to chunks before embedding, for example lowercasing [[r8]]","Always","Skipped cleaning makes query and chunk vectors drift apart","None"],["Conversation-aware rewrite","Turns a follow-up such as \"and for contractors?\" into a standalone query using chat history","Multi-turn chat","Pulls stale context into a new topic","1 LLM call"],["Multi-query","Writes several phrasings and fuses their result lists. Azure offers `queryRewrites: \"generative|count-N\"`, N from 1 to 10, as a preview feature [[r28]]","Short or vague queries","Near-duplicate lists, extra cost, dropped identifiers","1 LLM call plus N searches"],["HyDE","An LLM writes a hypothetical answer, and its embedding finds real neighbours [[r14]]","Dense retrieval with no relevance labels, abstract queries","The fake text can contain false details, so only the encoder's filtering protects you","1 LLM call"],["Step-back","Asks for the higher-level concept or principle first, then reasons from it [[r15]]","Questions tied to specifics that need background principles","Reported gains are on reasoning tasks, not a retrieval benchmark","1 LLM call"],["Decomposition","Splits a multi-part or multi-hop question into sub-queries that run in parallel","Several parts or hops in one question","Adds a planning call and extra searches to simple lookups, and loses links between parts","1 LLM call plus M searches"],["Self-query and query hints","Derives metadata filters and boosts from the text. Azure stores hints in `searchIndexParameters.queryHints` [[r29]]","Questions with implied filters such as year or product line","Best effort only, so never use it as an authorization boundary","Planner LLM call"]],"highlightCol":3},{"t":"flow","title":"A safe rewrite pipeline","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Clean","md":"Normalise the query like the chunks.","tag":"no LLM"},{"title":"Resolve","md":"Add chat history so a follow-up stands alone.","tag":"LLM"},{"title":"Classify","md":"Single fact, multi-part, or filterable?","tag":"route"},{"title":"Transform","md":"Rewrite or split, and keep the original query.","tag":"LLM"},{"title":"Retrieve","md":"Run every variant in parallel and fuse with RRF."},{"title":"Compare","md":"Judge against the no-rewrite baseline.","tag":"eval"}]},{"t":"compare","title":"Chunk grain at retrieval time","intro":"You indexed chunks in the previous world. At retrieval time you still choose what you match on and what you hand to the model.","options":[{"name":"Small chunks only","summary":"Match and return the same small passage.","pros":["Precise matches","Small prompt cost"],"cons":["Answers that span a section are cut","Pronouns and context are lost"],"bestWhen":"Questions are single-fact lookups.","avoidWhen":"Answers need the surrounding section."},{"name":"Parent-child (small to big)","summary":"Match small child chunks, then return the larger parent passage.","pros":["Precise match plus enough context","Fits structured documents"],"cons":["Larger prompts","Several children can return the same parent, so you must dedupe","Parent and child must inherit the same access rights"],"bestWhen":"Questions are precise but answers need the section around the hit.","avoidWhen":"Parents are huge and the token budget is tight."},{"name":"Large chunks only","summary":"Match and return big passages.","pros":["Context is intact","Fewer chunks to index"],"cons":["Embeddings average many topics and blur","Prompt cost and noise rise"],"bestWhen":"Documents are short and topic-pure.","avoidWhen":"Documents mix many topics per section."},{"name":"Contextual retrieval","tag":"Vendor-reported","summary":"Prepend a short LLM-written context to each chunk before embedding and BM25 indexing.","pros":["Anthropic reports 35% fewer top-20 retrieval failures with contextual embeddings (5.7% to 3.7%)","49% fewer with contextual BM25 too (to 2.9%), and 67% with reranking added"],"cons":["One LLM call per chunk at index time","The numbers come from the vendor's own datasets"],"bestWhen":"Chunks lose meaning when cut out of their document.","avoidWhen":"You cannot afford re-indexing the corpus."}]},{"t":"code","lang":"json","title":"Azure AI Search generative query rewrite (preview, 2026-08-01-preview)","code":"{\n  \"search\": \"newer hotel near the water with a great restaurant\",\n  \"semanticConfiguration\": \"en-semantic-config\",\n  \"queryType\": \"semantic\",\n  \"queryRewrites\": \"generative|count-5\",\n  \"queryLanguage\": \"en-US\",\n  \"debug\": \"queryRewrites\",\n  \"top\": 1\n}","caption":"From the Microsoft Learn sample, checked 2026-10-13. Semantic ranking is required, `queryLanguage` is required, and `debug` is for testing only."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Rewrites can erase the one token that mattered","md":"Azure's own guidance says rewrites might not contain all the exact terms of the original query, which can hurt highly specific queries that need exact identifiers or product codes [[r28]]. Stored query hints are best effort, are not applied at `minimal` effort, and a GPT-4o or GPT-4.1 family planner returns HTTP 400 at other efforts if stored hints contain a filter [[r29]]. Preview status checked 2026-10-13."},{"t":"failure","title":"Query transform failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"Recall on identifier queries drops after adding an LLM rewrite.","cause":"The rewrite paraphrased the identifier away.","fix":"Keep the original query as one fused search, or skip the rewrite when the query has identifier-like tokens. Detect with recall sliced by query class."},{"symptom":"\"And for contractors?\" retrieves nothing relevant.","cause":"No conversation-aware rewrite, so the retriever sees three words with no topic.","fix":"Resolve the follow-up with the chat history before retrieval and log both versions."},{"symptom":"Multi-query adds latency and cost but no gain.","cause":"The variants are near duplicates and return the same top hits.","fix":"Cap the count, dedupe, and measure uplift per query class before keeping it."},{"symptom":"Parent-child fills the prompt with the same section five times.","cause":"Five child chunks share one parent.","fix":"Dedupe parents by id before building the prompt."},{"symptom":"A generated filter hides the right document.","cause":"Self-query filters are best effort and can be wrong.","fix":"Validate against the schema, fall back to unfiltered search on zero hits, and keep access control in a deterministic filter."}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-l4-q1","type":"match","tag":"concept","difficulty":1,"q":"Match each query transformation to what it does.","pairs":[{"l":"HyDE","r":"Embeds an LLM-written hypothetical answer to find real neighbours"},{"l":"Step-back","r":"Asks for the higher-level concept before retrieving"},{"l":"Decomposition","r":"Splits a multi-part question into parallel sub-queries"},{"l":"Self-query","r":"Turns constraints in the text into metadata filters"}],"explain":"The four transforms answer different problems: a missing relevance signal, missing background, several questions in one, and implied filters. Mixing them up is the common error, for example using HyDE where decomposition is needed."},{"id":"w05-l4-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":2,"q":"After adding an LLM query rewrite, recall@10 moved as shown. What is the most likely cause and fix?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"query class        before  after\nidentifier         0.91    0.74\nparaphrase         0.62    0.71\nmixed              0.70    0.73"},"options":["The rewrite drops exact identifiers, so also search with the original query","The embedding model needs to be re-trained on identifier-heavy examples first","The BM25 weight is too high, so lower it for every class","Rewrites should be generated at a higher temperature"],"answer":0,"explain":"The paraphrase class improved while identifiers dropped, which is the signature of a rewrite that loses exact tokens. Keeping the original query as a fused search restores the identifier hits. Re-training embeddings is a heavy fix for a query-side problem, lowering BM25 would hurt identifiers further, and a higher temperature makes rewrites less faithful."},{"id":"w05-l4-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":2,"q":"A user asks: \"Compare the notice periods for contractors and employees in the UK and in Germany.\" Which transformation fits best?","options":["HyDE, because the question lacks relevance labels","Step-back, because the question needs a general principle","Decomposition into one sub-query per party and country","Self-query alone, because the countries are filters"],"answer":2,"explain":"The question has several parts and each needs its own evidence, which is what decomposition and parallel sub-queries provide. HyDE helps one abstract query rather than several parts, step-back targets reasoning that needs a principle, and a country filter alone would still leave contractors and employees in one search."}],"defend":null,"refs":["r8","r14","r15","r26","r28","r29","r37"],"takeaways":["Rewrites help vague queries and follow-ups and can erase exact identifiers","Keep the original query as one of the fused searches","Route by question shape: decompose multi-part questions, leave single lookups alone","Parent-child retrieval needs dedupe of parents and matching access rights"]},{"id":"w05-l5","order":5,"title":"Past top-k: trees, graphs and planners","kind":"problem","minutes":18,"brief":"Some questions have no single answer chunk: the main themes across thousands of reports, or a question with three parts. When does a structure beyond a flat list of chunks pay for its index bill and its latency?","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"When one flat list of chunks is not enough","level":2,"id":"when-one-flat-list-of-chunks-is-not-enough"},{"t":"compare","title":"Beyond flat top-k","intro":"Three change the index (RAPTOR, GraphRAG, LazyGraphRAG). One changes the query path (agentic retrieval). Evidence quality differs, so each card says who measured what.","options":[{"name":"RAPTOR","tag":"Paper","summary":"Recursively embed, cluster and summarise chunks into a tree, then retrieve from several levels.","pros":["Upper nodes carry cross-chunk context for broad questions","Paired with GPT-4 it improved the best QuALITY result by 20% (absolute)"],"cons":["Index build runs recursive summarisation, so LLM calls grow with corpus size","Summary nodes are not source text, which complicates exact citations"],"bestWhen":"Long documents need multi-step reasoning across sections and the corpus is stable.","avoidWhen":"Content changes daily or answers must quote source spans.","meta":[{"k":"Index cost","v":"high"},{"k":"Maturity","v":"research code"}]},{"name":"GraphRAG","tag":"Open source","summary":"An LLM extracts entities, relationships and claims, the graph is clustered with Leiden, and community summaries answer global questions.","pros":["Built for corpus-wide questions that top-k vector RAG handles poorly","Query modes Global, Local, DRIFT and Basic","`FastGraphRAG` swaps some LLM steps for classic NLP"],"cons":["The `standard` method uses an LLM for all reasoning, so indexing is costly","A changing corpus means re-extraction and rebuilt communities"],"bestWhen":"Many questions are thematic or multi-document and the corpus changes slowly.","avoidWhen":"Most questions are single-fact lookups.","meta":[{"k":"Index cost","v":"highest"},{"k":"Maturity","v":"research code"}]},{"name":"LazyGraphRAG","tag":"Vendor benchmark","summary":"Defer LLM work to query time and spend a relevance test budget instead of summarising the whole graph up front.","pros":["Microsoft Research reports indexing cost identical to vector RAG and 0.1% of full GraphRAG","Comparable global-query quality at over 700 times lower query cost than GraphRAG global search","One cost and quality dial"],"cons":["Vendor's own benchmark, posted 2024-11-25","Reached Microsoft Discovery and Azure Local as public preview (editor's note 2025-06-06), not a general Azure AI Search feature"],"bestWhen":"You want graph-style answers on a corpus that changes, and can run it where it is offered.","avoidWhen":"You need a generally available managed service today.","meta":[{"k":"Index cost","v":"same as vector RAG"},{"k":"Query cost","v":"tunable"}]},{"name":"Agentic retrieval (Azure AI Search)","tag":"Preview","summary":"A knowledge base plans subqueries with an LLM, runs them in parallel over knowledge sources, reranks and merges.","pros":["Handles multi-part and context-dependent questions","Returns merged content, references and an activity log","Queries the live index, so no graph rebuild"],"cons":["Planning adds latency over a single query, and planning tokens are billed separately","Most effort levels are preview as of 2026-10-13"],"bestWhen":"Questions are complex, chat history matters and the latency budget allows one planning pass.","avoidWhen":"Latency is tight, or you need only GA features.","meta":[{"k":"Stable API","v":"2026-04-01, minimal only"},{"k":"Preview API","v":"2026-08-01-preview"}]}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w05-l5-pays","title":"Does a graph or a planner pay?","prompt":"Flip the question shape, how fast the corpus changes and the latency budget.","dials":[{"id":"shape","label":"Question shape","values":[{"id":"local","label":"Single-fact lookups"},{"id":"multi","label":"Multi-part or context-dependent"},{"id":"global","label":"Corpus-wide themes"}]},{"id":"churn","label":"Corpus change rate","values":[{"id":"stable","label":"Slow"},{"id":"fast","label":"Daily"}]},{"id":"latency","label":"Latency budget","values":[{"id":"tight","label":"Tight"},{"id":"relaxed","label":"Relaxed"}]}],"options":[{"id":"flat","name":"Hybrid plus semantic ranker","summary":"One query, one ranked shortlist."},{"id":"agentic","name":"Agentic retrieval (low or medium)","summary":"LLM-planned parallel subqueries. Preview."},{"id":"raptor","name":"RAPTOR tree","summary":"Summary tree searched at several levels."},{"id":"graphrag","name":"GraphRAG","summary":"Entity graph plus community summaries."},{"id":"lazy","name":"LazyGraphRAG","summary":"Deferred LLM work with a relevance test budget."}],"scores":{"flat":{"shape":{"local":{"s":2,"why":"A lookup needs the right chunk, and hybrid plus reranking finds it."},"multi":{"s":-1,"why":"One query cannot cover several parts at once."},"global":{"s":-2,"why":"Top-k chunks cannot summarise a whole corpus."}},"churn":{"stable":{"s":0,"why":"Works, with no special advantage."},"fast":{"s":2,"why":"Incremental indexing keeps it fresh with no rebuild."}},"latency":{"tight":{"s":2,"why":"A single query is the fastest path."},"relaxed":{"s":0,"why":"Spare latency goes unused."}}},"agentic":{"shape":{"local":{"s":0,"why":"One planning pass adds latency a lookup does not need."},"multi":{"s":2,"why":"The planner splits the question into focused subqueries run in parallel."},"global":{"s":-1,"why":"It still returns top passages, not corpus-level summaries."}},"churn":{"stable":{"s":0,"why":"No special advantage."},"fast":{"s":1,"why":"It queries the live index, so there is no graph to rebuild."}},"latency":{"tight":{"s":-2,"why":"The docs say it adds latency compared with a single query."},"relaxed":{"s":1,"why":"One planning pass, and a retry at medium, fit a relaxed budget."}}},"raptor":{"shape":{"local":{"s":0,"why":"Leaf nodes still answer lookups, so the tree adds little."},"multi":{"s":1,"why":"Higher levels carry cross-chunk context, as in the QuALITY result."},"global":{"s":1,"why":"Upper summary nodes cover broad questions better than flat chunks."}},"churn":{"stable":{"s":1,"why":"Summaries are built once."},"fast":{"s":-2,"why":"Re-clustering and re-summarising follow every change."}},"latency":{"tight":{"s":1,"why":"Retrieval is a vector search over tree nodes."},"relaxed":{"s":0,"why":"No extra benefit from spare latency."}}},"graphrag":{"shape":{"local":{"s":0,"why":"A Local mode exists, but flat hybrid is cheaper for lookups."},"multi":{"s":1,"why":"Entity links connect evidence across documents."},"global":{"s":2,"why":"Community summaries are built for corpus-wide questions."}},"churn":{"stable":{"s":1,"why":"The indexing bill is paid once."},"fast":{"s":-2,"why":"Re-extraction and community rebuilds are costly."}},"latency":{"tight":{"s":-1,"why":"Global search reads many community summaries per query."},"relaxed":{"s":1,"why":"Quality-first answers fit a relaxed budget."}}},"lazy":{"shape":{"local":{"s":0,"why":"Not needed for single lookups."},"multi":{"s":1,"why":"Query-time graph work links evidence across documents."},"global":{"s":2,"why":"Vendor benchmark: comparable to GraphRAG global search at far lower query cost."}},"churn":{"stable":{"s":1,"why":"Cheap to index and cheap to keep."},"fast":{"s":1,"why":"Indexing cost equals vector RAG, so changes are affordable."}},"latency":{"tight":{"s":0,"why":"Query-time LLM work grows with the relevance test budget."},"relaxed":{"s":1,"why":"A single dial trades cost against quality."}}}},"takeaway":"A structure pays only when your questions are global or multi-part. Count how many of them you actually get before you pay the index bill."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w05-l5-agentic","title":"Agentic retrieval request path","alt":"An app sends a retrieve call with chat history. An LLM plans subqueries, they run in parallel, each is semantically reranked, the results are merged and returned with references and an activity log.","src":"flowchart LR\n  A[App retrieve call] --> P[LLM plans subqueries]\n  P --> S1[Subquery 1]\n  P --> S2[Subquery 2]\n  S1 --> R[Semantic rerank each]\n  S2 --> R\n  R --> M[Merge]\n  M --> O[Content, references, activity]","caption":"From the Azure AI Search agentic retrieval overview, checked 2026-10-13. Planning applies at low and medium effort."},{"t":"table","caption":"Retrieval reasoning effort (status checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Effort","LLM work","Answer token cap","Limits worth knowing","Status"],"rows":[["`minimal`","No query planning. Searches every knowledge source in the knowledge base","Extractive output only","`outputMode` must be `extractiveData`, no answer synthesis or web source, up to 10 sources per knowledge base","Stable in 2026-04-01"],["`low`","One planning pass and source selection. The default if unset","5,000","Up to 50 documents to semantic ranking, 10 with L3 classification","Preview"],["`medium`","Adds a semantic classifier and one retry with a revised plan","10,000","50 documents, 20 with L3. Select regions only (15 listed)","Preview"],["`auto`","Light first pass, escalates up to medium if grounding is weak","Not stated on the page","Needs 2026-08-01-preview and a model on the knowledge base. Earlier versions return 400","Preview"]],"highlightCol":1},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Status as of 2026-10-13","md":"Only `minimal` extractive retrieval with `intents` input is in the stable `2026-04-01` API. Query planning, answer synthesis, `low`, `medium`, `auto`, `messages` input and SSE streaming need `2026-08-01-preview` [[r4]] [[r5]]. Portal-made objects use preview schemas and may need migration [[r34]]. Planner models: the page updated 2026-09-17 lists the `gpt-5` family and marks GPT-4o and GPT-4.1 families deprecated [[r39]]. **Azure OpenAI On Your Data retires 2026-10-14**, and Microsoft recommends Foundry Agent Service with Foundry IQ [[r33]]."},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Go deeper: Foundry IQ and permissions","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"Foundry IQ is a managed knowledge layer: one knowledge base can serve Foundry Agent Service, Microsoft Agent Framework, custom apps through the Search REST API, and Copilot Studio through a Foundry agent (preview). Sources include Blob, SharePoint, OneLake and the public web. It automates chunking, embeddings and metadata extraction for indexed sources, syncs ACLs for supported sources and honours Purview sensitivity labels [[r7]].\n\nFor indexed sources, ACLs and labels must be ingested with `ingestionPermissionOptions`. At query time pass the user token (scope `https://search.azure.com/.default`) in `x-ms-query-source-authorization`. **If you omit the identity, indexed sources return unfiltered results.** If `ingestionPermissionOptions` was not set at creation, results are unfiltered whatever the header says [[r6]]. The full access-control design is the subject of world w07."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: limits, runtime and billing","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"`maxRuntimeInSeconds` has a minimum of 10, a default of 90 and a maximum of 600, and the 600 applies to the Search retrieve request only. A knowledge base holds up to 10 knowledge sources on most paid tiers and 3 on Free. The output budget is `maxOutputSizeInTokens` (`maxOutputSize` in `2026-05-01-preview` and later, and samples show both spellings), and a document larger than the budget can be dropped with a warning in `activity` and empty `references` [[r38]].\n\nSearch bills retrieval tokens, meaning subquery execution and semantic ranking. Azure OpenAI bills planning and answer synthesis tokens separately at pay-as-you-go rates [[r39]]. With `Accept: text/event-stream` (since `2026-08-01-preview`) the response streams events such as `activity.completed` and `response.completed`. The HTTP status stays 200 once the stream opens, so read the terminal event [[r6]]."}]}]},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes beyond flat retrieval","items":[{"symptom":"A retrieve call returns passages from documents the caller must not see.","cause":"No user token in `x-ms-query-source-authorization`, or `ingestionPermissionOptions` was never set. Indexed sources fail open.","fix":"Pass the user token, ingest permissions, and test with a second user who lacks access."},{"symptom":"HTTP 400 at `low` effort after adding stored filter hints.","cause":"A GPT-4o or GPT-4.1 family planner rejects stored hints that contain a filter.","fix":"Move the knowledge base to a supported `gpt-5` family planner and re-test."},{"symptom":"A large document never appears, and the activity log shows a warning.","cause":"The document exceeded the output budget and was dropped.","fix":"Raise the output budget or chunk the source, and alert on warnings in `activity`."},{"symptom":"A streamed call looks fine and has no answer.","cause":"The stream ended with an `error` event after an HTTP 200.","fix":"Check the terminal event, which is `response.completed` (200 or 206) or `error`."},{"symptom":"The index bill explodes when the corpus changes daily.","cause":"Full GraphRAG or RAPTOR rebuilds follow every change.","fix":"Limit the structure to a stable subset, or test a deferred approach such as LazyGraphRAG."}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-l5-q1","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":1,"q":"Most questions are single-fact lookups, the corpus changes daily and the latency budget is tight. Which option fits best?","options":["GraphRAG with community summaries rebuilt on every change","A RAPTOR tree rebuilt every night","Agentic retrieval at medium effort","Hybrid search with the semantic ranker"],"answer":3,"explain":"Lookups need the right chunk, daily change favours incremental indexing, and a tight budget favours one query. GraphRAG and RAPTOR pay their indexing bill again on every change and serve questions you rarely get. Agentic retrieval at medium adds a planning pass and a possible retry, which a tight budget cannot absorb."},{"id":"w05-l5-q2","type":"single","tag":"security","difficulty":2,"q":"A knowledge base retrieve call returns passages from documents the caller must not see. The request looks like this. What is the most likely cause?","code":{"lang":"http","code":"POST /knowledgebases/hr-kb/retrieve?api-version=2026-08-01-preview\nAuthorization: Bearer <application token>\nContent-Type: application/json"},"options":["No user token is sent in `x-ms-query-source-authorization`, so the call fails open","The reasoning effort is set to `low`, which skips permission trimming","The semantic ranker reordered results past the access rules during the reranking step","`maxRuntimeInSeconds` is shorter than the default value of 90 seconds"],"answer":0,"explain":"For indexed sources the service filters by the identity in `x-ms-query-source-authorization` and fails open when it is missing. The effort level, the ranker and the runtime limit do not touch access control. Also confirm `ingestionPermissionOptions` was set when the source was created, because without it results are unfiltered whatever the header says."},{"id":"w05-l5-q3","type":"multi","tag":"concept","difficulty":2,"q":"Select every statement that is true of Azure AI Search agentic retrieval as of 2026-10-13.","options":["Only `minimal` effort with `intents` input and extractive output is in the stable 2026-04-01 API","At `low` effort an LLM plans subqueries that run in parallel and are each semantically reranked","`auto` effort works on every API version","A GPT-4.1 family planner is the recommended choice"],"answers":[0,1],"explain":"The stable version is limited to `minimal`, and the planning pass at `low` fans subqueries out in parallel with semantic reranking of each. `auto` needs 2026-08-01-preview and earlier versions return 400, and the docs mark the GPT-4o and GPT-4.1 families deprecated for planning in favour of the `gpt-5` family."}],"defend":null,"refs":["r4","r5","r6","r7","r16","r17","r18","r33","r34","r38","r39","r40"],"takeaways":["Flat hybrid plus reranking wins on single-fact lookups and fast-changing corpora","Graphs and trees pay for global or multi-part questions, and cost a rebuild on every change","Only minimal-effort extractive agentic retrieval is stable as of 2026-10-13, the rest is preview","A knowledge base queried without a user identity fails open on indexed sources"]},{"id":"w05-l6","order":6,"title":"Retrieve, stuff or cache? Then cite it and debug it","kind":"problem","minutes":17,"brief":"The corpus fits in a context window. Or nearly does. Or it fits for one user and not for another. Retrieval is no longer the only way to put knowledge in a prompt, and whichever you pick, a bad answer still needs a debugging path and every claim needs a source.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Three ways to put knowledge in front of the model","level":2,"id":"three-ways-to-put-knowledge-in-front-of-the-model"},{"t":"compare","title":"RAG, long context, CAG and routing","intro":"Li et al. found that when resourced enough, long-context models beat RAG on average, but RAG is much cheaper [[r21]]. Accuracy also falls as input grows: in NoLiMa, 11 of 13 models fell below half of their short-context baseline at 32K tokens, and GPT-4o dropped from 99.3% to 69.7% [[r23]]. Chroma's report of 2025-07-14 saw 18 LLMs become less reliable as input grew [[r25]].","options":[{"name":"RAG","tag":"GA","summary":"Retrieve a few chunks per query.","pros":["Scales far beyond the window","Per-user filters trim results","Small, cheap prompts"],"cons":["Retrieval can miss, so recall and abstention need work","More components to run and evaluate"],"bestWhen":"The corpus is large, changes often or has per-user permissions.","avoidWhen":"The corpus is tiny and static, where an index adds only risk."},{"name":"Long context per query","summary":"Put the whole corpus, or a large slice, into every prompt.","pros":["No index, no retrieval misses","Best accuracy on average in Li et al. when resourced enough"],"cons":["Per-query cost grows with corpus size","Evidence in the middle is used less (Lost in the Middle)","Accuracy degrades with length and distractors"],"bestWhen":"The corpus is small. Anthropic's rule of thumb is under 200,000 tokens, about 500 pages.","avoidWhen":"The corpus is larger than the window or the cost per query matters.","meta":[{"k":"Source","v":"Anthropic vendor guidance"}]},{"name":"Cache-augmented generation (CAG)","tag":"Paper","summary":"Preload a small, manageable corpus into the context and cache the runtime KV state, so no retrieval runs per query.","pros":["Lower latency and no retrieval errors for constrained knowledge bases, as the paper claims","Simplest serving path for a shared, static corpus"],"cons":["Not suited to large or fast-changing corpora","One cached state cannot hide documents per user (a reasoned limit, not a quote from the paper)"],"bestWhen":"A small, stable, shared knowledge set serves many queries.","avoidWhen":"Access differs per user or content changes every day."},{"name":"Route per query (Self-Route)","tag":"Paper","summary":"Try RAG first and send the query to long context only if the model judges the retrieved evidence insufficient.","pros":["Keeps accuracy near long context at lower cost","Cost follows query difficulty"],"cons":["Two paths to build, secure and evaluate","The long-context fallback must fit the data it needs"],"bestWhen":"Most queries are easy and a few need the whole picture.","avoidWhen":"You cannot afford to maintain two paths."}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w05-l6-size","title":"RAG, long context or CAG?","prompt":"Flip the corpus size, the change rate and the permission model.","dials":[{"id":"size","label":"Corpus versus context window","values":[{"id":"fits","label":"Fits with room to spare"},{"id":"big","label":"Far larger than the window"}]},{"id":"churn","label":"Change rate","values":[{"id":"stable","label":"Stable"},{"id":"fast","label":"Changes daily"}]},{"id":"perm","label":"Permissions","values":[{"id":"shared","label":"Same for every user"},{"id":"peruser","label":"Different per user"}]}],"options":[{"id":"rag","name":"RAG","summary":"Retrieve chunks per query."},{"id":"lc","name":"Long context per query","summary":"Stuff the corpus into every prompt."},{"id":"cag","name":"CAG","summary":"Preload and cache the corpus."},{"id":"route","name":"Route (RAG then long context)","summary":"Escalate hard queries."}],"scores":{"rag":{"size":{"fits":{"s":0,"why":"It works, with more moving parts than the corpus needs."},"big":{"s":2,"why":"Retrieval is the only way when the corpus is far larger than the window."}},"churn":{"stable":{"s":0,"why":"No special advantage."},"fast":{"s":2,"why":"Incremental indexing keeps up."}},"perm":{"shared":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"peruser":{"s":2,"why":"Permission filters trim results per caller."}}},"lc":{"size":{"fits":{"s":1,"why":"No index to build, and long context beat RAG on average when resourced enough."},"big":{"s":-2,"why":"It does not fit."}},"churn":{"stable":{"s":0,"why":"You pay for the whole corpus on every query unless the prefix is cached."},"fast":{"s":1,"why":"No index to refresh, so new documents go straight into the prompt."}},"perm":{"shared":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"peruser":{"s":0,"why":"You can build each prompt from the caller's visible documents, but prefixes cannot be shared across users."}}},"cag":{"size":{"fits":{"s":2,"why":"A small, manageable knowledge set is its intended use."},"big":{"s":-2,"why":"It cannot preload what does not fit."}},"churn":{"stable":{"s":2,"why":"The cache is built once."},"fast":{"s":-2,"why":"The cache must be rebuilt whenever knowledge changes."}},"perm":{"shared":{"s":1,"why":"One cached state serves everyone."},"peruser":{"s":-2,"why":"One cached state cannot hide documents per user."}}},"route":{"size":{"fits":{"s":1,"why":"RAG first and long context for hard queries costs less than long context alone."},"big":{"s":-1,"why":"The long-context fallback needs a large slice of the corpus to fit."}},"churn":{"stable":{"s":1,"why":"Both paths are simple on stable data."},"fast":{"s":0,"why":"The RAG leg stays fresh, but you run two paths."}},"perm":{"shared":{"s":1,"why":"Neutral to good."},"peruser":{"s":0,"why":"Both paths must enforce the filter, which doubles the places to get it wrong."}}}},"takeaway":"Permissions and change rate usually decide before accuracy does. A shared cache is the first thing per-user access breaks."},{"t":"table","caption":"Citation strategies","head":["Method","What you get","What it does not guarantee","Watch for"],"rows":[["Prompt-only: cite your sources","Free-text markers such as [2]","That the id exists or supports the claim","Validate every cited id against the retrieved set"],["API-native citations","Anthropic `citations` on `document` or `search_result` blocks return `char_location`, `page_location` or `content_block_location` spans, and `cited_text` does not count toward output tokens [[r27]]. OpenAI file search in the Responses API returns `file_citation` annotations [[r30]]","That the cited span entails the claim","Anthropic citations with structured outputs returns HTTP 400. OpenAI raw hits come back only with `include: [\"file_search_call.results\"]`"],["Quote first","For documents over 20k tokens, ask for word-for-word quotes, then answer from them [[r41]]","That the quotes are used faithfully","A second step adds latency and tokens"],["Post-hoc support check","An entailment model or judge checks each claim against its cited span","Nothing on missed claims","Another model call, covered in the guardrails world"]],"highlightCol":2},{"t":"flow","title":"Debug a bad answer from the bottom up","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"In the corpus?","md":"Is the answer text in an indexed chunk? Check ingestion and parsing first.","tag":"ingest"},{"title":"Retrieved?","md":"Is the gold chunk in the candidate set? Measure recall@k before reranking.","tag":"recall"},{"title":"Kept?","md":"Did fusion, the reranker and the cutoff keep it? Compare its rank at each stage.","tag":"rank"},{"title":"In the prompt?","md":"Is it present, untruncated and well placed in the final prompt?","tag":"prompt"},{"title":"Used?","md":"Did the model use it and cite a span that supports the claim?","tag":"answer"}]},{"t":"failure","title":"Debug by symptom","items":[{"symptom":"A confident answer cites a chunk that is irrelevant.","cause":"A retrieval miss with no abstention gate.","fix":"Check candidate recall and the cutoff, then add an abstention rule."},{"symptom":"The fact exists in the corpus and is never retrieved.","cause":"The chunking split it, an identifier needs a keyword leg, or the query and page share no vocabulary.","fix":"Find the chunk that holds the fact, then add BM25, a rewrite or a different chunk boundary."},{"symptom":"The gold chunk sits at fused rank 31 and is absent from the prompt.","cause":"Candidates were cut before the reranker, or the reranker only sees the top 50.","fix":"Raise recall, rerank a deeper list, and log the rank at every stage."},{"symptom":"The gold chunk is in the prompt and the answer ignores it.","cause":"Position and length effects: evidence in the middle of a long context is used least.","fix":"Send fewer, better-ranked chunks with the strongest first or last."},{"symptom":"The answer cites a source that does not support the claim.","cause":"A citation proves provenance, not entailment.","fix":"Add quote-first prompting or a support check on each claim."},{"symptom":"The answer is stale.","cause":"Ingestion lag, an old index version or a cache that outlived the content.","fix":"Compare document timestamps in the prompt with the source, and check cache TTLs."},{"symptom":"A list-everything question returns an incomplete list.","cause":"Top-k limits what one retrieval can return.","fix":"Decompose, use a summary or graph index, or raise k with a cap."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What interviewers listen for","md":"They ask where you would look first for a wrong answer. A strong answer walks the stages in order, names the metric at each (recall@k, rank after rerank, prompt contents, support rate) and says which log line proves it."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-l6-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","difficulty":1,"q":"A 150,000-token corpus is placed in every prompt with no caching. A RAG pipeline sends 6,000 tokens per query. At the same price per input token, how many times more input cost does long context carry per query?","answer":25,"tolerancePct":2,"unit":"times","hint":"150,000 divided by 6,000","explain":"150,000 / 6,000 = 25, so each long-context query carries 25 times the input tokens. Prompt caching can shrink the gap and long context can be more accurate, so the real decision also weighs accuracy, update rate and permissions."},{"id":"w05-l6-q2","type":"single","tag":"security","difficulty":2,"q":"A team proposes cache-augmented generation for HR policies, where each employee may see a different subset. Which statement is correct?","options":["CAG works because a cached prefix is filtered per user at generation time","CAG removes retrieval errors for every corpus, so permissions stop mattering","One cached context cannot hide documents per user, so use filtered retrieval or per-user prompts","CAG needs a vector index, so it cannot be used with HR policies at all"],"answer":2,"explain":"A cached context is shared state, so every user would see every preloaded document. Per-user visibility needs filtering before documents enter the context, which means filtered retrieval or a per-user prompt. The cache is not filtered at generation time, the claim about every corpus overreaches, and CAG needs no vector index."},{"id":"w05-l6-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":2,"q":"The retrieval log below ends in a wrong answer. What is the best first change?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"gold chunk: C17\nrank after rerank: 3\nchunks in prompt: 20, with C17 at position 11\nanswer: ignores C17 and cites C4"},"options":["Raise k to 40 so that more of the relevant evidence is present in the prompt","Send fewer chunks and put the strongest at the start or end","Switch to a larger embedding model so retrieval ranks the chunk even higher","Add a BM25 leg to the retriever so identifiers are matched exactly"],"answer":1,"explain":"The retriever already ranked C17 third, so recall and ranking worked. The failure is position and volume: evidence in the middle of a long context is used least. A larger k adds more distractors, a bigger embedding model changes retrieval, and a BM25 leg addresses a miss that did not happen."},{"id":"w05-l6-q4","type":"multi","tag":"concept","difficulty":2,"q":"Select every true statement about Anthropic's Citations feature and citations in general.","options":["Enabling citations together with structured outputs returns HTTP 400","`cited_text` counts toward your output tokens","Citation types include `char_location`, `page_location` and `content_block_location`","A citation proves the cited span supports the claim"],"answers":[0,2],"explain":"The docs say citations and structured outputs together return HTTP 400, and list the three location types. `cited_text` is not counted toward output tokens. A citation shows where text came from, not that the claim follows from it, which is why a separate support check exists."}],"defend":null,"refs":["r21","r22","r23","r24","r25","r27","r30","r41"],"takeaways":["RAG, long context and CAG differ on corpus size, change rate and permissions more than on accuracy","A shared cached context cannot enforce per-user access","Debug in stage order: corpus, candidates, ranking, prompt, answer","A citation proves provenance, not entailment"]},{"id":"w05-l7","order":7,"title":"Boss: the identifier and the paraphrase","kind":"boss","minutes":18,"brief":"An interviewer describes an assistant whose users type error codes and paraphrased policy questions into the same box. No numbers are given. Design the retrieval stack and defend it.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Your four minutes","level":2,"id":"your-four-minutes"},{"t":"scenario","title":"What you are handed","known":["Users ask about exact identifiers and about policies in their own words","Answers must be grounded in the corpus and cite it","The interviewer will probe latency, cost and what happens when retrieval fails"],"ask":["What share of queries is each class, and is it measured?","How big and how volatile is the corpus, and what are the permissions?","What is the retrieval latency budget, and may text leave the network?","What does a wrong answer cost, and may the system abstain?"],"tension":"Exact tokens favour keyword search, paraphrases favour vectors, and every extra stage costs latency and money."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w05-l7-stack","title":"A defensible default stack","alt":"A query is cleaned and classified, searched by a BM25 leg and a vector leg in parallel, fused with RRF, reranked over the top 50, passed through a cutoff, then answered with citations or abstained.","src":"flowchart LR\n  U[Query] --> N[Clean and classify]\n  N --> K[BM25 leg]\n  N --> V[Vector leg]\n  K --> F[RRF fusion]\n  V --> F\n  F --> R[Rerank top 50]\n  R --> G{Cutoff}\n  G -->|pass| L[LLM with citations]\n  G -->|fail| A[Abstain]","caption":"A starting point, not the answer. Each box has a dial that can remove or replace it."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-l7-q1","type":"single","tag":"flip","difficulty":2,"q":"The retrieval stage must fit in 800 ms, down from a 2 s budget. Your stack has a serial LLM query rewrite, parallel BM25 and vector legs, RRF and a managed reranker. Which change comes first?","options":["Drop the BM25 leg, which the vector leg already covers","Swap the reranker for an LLM reranker to protect quality","Raise the candidate count so the reranker can recover","Remove the serial LLM rewrite and keep the original query"],"answer":3,"explain":"The rewrite is a serial LLM call before any search, so it is the costliest stage to remove, and the original query keeps identifiers intact. Dropping BM25 gives up identifier recall, an LLM reranker adds latency, and more candidates add work. Latency numbers for each stage are not published, so measure them."},{"id":"w05-l7-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":3,"q":"Select every metric that belongs in the rollout plan for this stack.","options":["Recall@k of the fused candidates, split into identifier, paraphrase and mixed queries","Abstention rate, and how often an abstention was wrong","Share of answers whose cited span supports the claim","Number of tokens in the system prompt"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Recall by query class shows whether each leg earns its place, abstention quality shows whether the cutoff is calibrated, and support rate shows whether citations mean anything. Prompt length is a cost input, not a quality metric for this question."},{"id":"w05-l7-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":2,"q":"The interviewer asks: why not drop BM25 and use dense retrieval with a better embedding model?","options":["Embeddings blur exact identifiers and BM25 is a strong baseline, so measure identifier recall before dropping it","Dense retrieval cannot be combined with metadata filters in any engine","Embedding models are unable to index more than a few hundred documents","Dense retrieval is always slower than BM25 by a factor of ten or more"],"answer":0,"explain":"BEIR shows BM25 is a reliable baseline and dense models are often weaker out of domain, and identifiers are where embeddings blur most, so the decision should rest on identifier recall on your data. Dense retrieval works with filters, scales far beyond a few hundred documents, and its latency is not a fixed multiple of BM25."}],"defend":{"prompt":"Our assistant answers questions that mix exact identifiers, such as error codes, part numbers and ticket ids, with paraphrased policy questions in the same chat box. Design the retrieval stack and tell me how you would know it works.","timebox":"4 min","clarify":["What share of traffic is identifiers, paraphrases or both, and has anyone measured it?","How large and how volatile is the corpus, and how are the documents structured?","What is the permission model, and how fast must a revocation take effect?","What is the latency budget for retrieval, and does the chat history matter?","What does a wrong answer cost, and may the assistant say it does not know?","May chunk text leave our network for a managed or hosted reranker?"],"options":[{"name":"Hybrid (BM25 plus vector) with RRF","when":"The query mix is mixed and there are no labels yet.","tradeoff":"Two retrievals per query. The fused order is coarse, so precision at the top is only fair."},{"name":"Hybrid plus a reranker and a cutoff","when":"Precision at the top matters and one more stage fits the budget.","tradeoff":"Adds a hop and a cost. Recall must come first, and score cutoffs drift, so keep them coarse."},{"name":"Dense only with a tuned embedding model","when":"Queries are almost all paraphrases and the model fits the domain.","tradeoff":"Exact identifiers blur, and it is weaker out of domain."},{"name":"Agentic retrieval","when":"Questions are multi-part, history matters and the latency budget is relaxed.","tradeoff":"Preview for planning levels as of 2026-10-13. Adds a planning pass and separate token billing."}],"modelAnswer":[{"t":"p","md":"**1. Clarify the dials.** I would ask for the query mix, corpus size and change rate, permission model, retrieval latency budget, the cost of a wrong answer and whether text may leave the network. Without those, any stack is a guess."},{"t":"p","md":"**2. List the options, including the boring one.** BM25 alone is cheap and nails identifiers. Dense alone handles paraphrase and blurs codes. Hybrid fuses both. Hybrid plus a reranker adds precision. Agentic retrieval plans subqueries for harder questions."},{"t":"p","md":"**3. Pick for the stated dials.** For mixed traffic with no labels I would start with hybrid retrieval fused by RRF, because it needs no score normalisation and hedges both query classes. I would add a reranker over the top 50 candidates once recall is verified, with a coarse cutoff that triggers abstention, and I would make the answer cite spans. If the original query is rewritten, I would keep it as one of the fused searches so identifiers survive."},{"t":"p","md":"**What would flip the choice.**"},{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["Latency halves: remove serial LLM calls such as the rewrite first, keep the keyword leg.","Labelled queries become available: test a convex combination against RRF, since evidence says it can win with a small labelled set.","Data may not leave the network: use a self-hosted cross-encoder instead of a hosted rerank API.","Questions become corpus-wide or multi-part: test a graph or agentic approach, and count how many such questions exist first.","Access differs per user: apply permission filters before ranking, and never share a cached context across users."]},{"t":"p","md":"**4. Failure modes and detection.**"},{"t":"table","head":["Failure","How I would detect it"],"rows":[["Identifier queries miss the right runbook","Recall@k on an identifier slice of the labelled set"],["Reranker never helps","Gold chunk rank before rerank, since it only reorders the top 50"],["Abstention rate jumps after a service update","Alert on the abstention rate and re-run the labelled set"],["Rewrite drops identifiers","Compare recall with and without the rewrite per query class"],["Citation does not support the claim","Support rate from a sampled entailment check"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**5. Metrics and rollout.** Offline: recall@k and nDCG sliced by query class on a labelled set, then answer-level groundedness and citation support. Online: shadow the new stack against the current one, canary a small share with alerts on abstention rate, latency per stage and support rate, then widen."},{"t":"p","md":"**Close with the flip conditions.** I would change the stack if latency halves (drop serial LLM calls first), if labelled queries appear (test convex combination), if text may not leave the network (self-host the reranker), if questions turn corpus-wide or multi-part (test graph or agentic retrieval) or if permissions differ per user (filter before ranking)."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"The line that earns marks","md":"Say what you would measure before you say what you would build, then name the one metric that would make you remove a stage."}],"rubric":[{"pt":"Asks about query mix, permissions, latency and the cost of a wrong answer before naming components","w":3},{"pt":"Offers at least two real options and includes the boring one","w":2},{"pt":"Chooses hybrid with rank-based fusion and explains why identifiers need the keyword leg","w":3},{"pt":"Places the reranker after recall, sizes candidates and sets a coarse cutoff with abstention","w":2},{"pt":"Keeps the original query when rewriting and names the identifier-dropping risk","w":2},{"pt":"States flip conditions for latency, permissions, labels and question shape","w":2},{"pt":"Names failure modes with a detection signal for each","w":2},{"pt":"Gives metrics sliced by query class and a staged rollout","w":1}],"followUps":[{"q":"Users start asking for the main causes across all incident reports. What changes?","a":"That is a global question, which flat top-k handles poorly. I would measure how many such questions arrive, then test a deferred graph approach or a summary index, and weigh the indexing cost against a corpus that changes."},{"q":"RRF or a weighted score sum?","a":"RRF first, because it needs no normalisation or labels. With a few hundred labelled queries I would test convex combination, since the evidence shows it can beat RRF and that RRF is sensitive to its parameters. The labelled set decides."},{"q":"How do you set the abstention cutoff?","a":"Coarsely, on a labelled set that includes unanswerable questions, and I would alert on the abstention rate, because reranker score distributions shift when the service model updates."}],"redFlags":["Names a product before asking about the query mix","Treats a reranker as a fix for poor recall","Thresholds on RRF scores or hard-codes a fine reranker cutoff","Never mentions abstention, citations or how the stack is measured"]},"refs":["r1","r3","r9","r10","r11","r36","r37"],"takeaways":["Clarify the query mix, permissions, latency and cost of a wrong answer before choosing a stack","Hybrid with RRF is the default for mixed traffic, and a labelled set tells you when to change it","Rerank after recall, cut coarsely and design the abstention path","Measure by query class, then roll out in shadow and canary"]}],"topics":[{"id":"w05-t01","order":1,"group":"Baseline","title":"RAG pipeline anatomy","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Retrieval-augmented generation fetches evidence at question time and hands it to the model. The pipeline has a fixed set of stages, and each one can lose the answer.","useIf":"You are building or reviewing a RAG system and need a map of where answer quality is won or lost.","avoidIf":"The knowledge is small, stable and shared, so a plain long prompt does the job without a retrieval stack (see the RAG vs long context topic).","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A RAG system splits one hard job, answering from private knowledge, into stages that can each be measured. The idea comes from the original RAG paper, which paired a generator with a dense index of Wikipedia [[tr9]]. Today the stages are: **ingest** and **chunk** the sources, **embed** and **index** them, then at question time **transform** the query, **retrieve** candidates, **fuse** the lists, **rerank**, **cut off** weak evidence, **assemble** the context, **generate** and **cite**."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w05-t01-d1","title":"Query-time path of an advanced RAG pipeline","alt":"A question is rewritten, searched by keyword and vector retrievers, fused, reranked, cut off, then passed to the model, which answers with citations.","src":"flowchart LR\n  Q[Question] --> R[Rewrite]\n  R --> K[Keyword search]\n  R --> V[Vector search]\n  K --> F[Fuse]\n  V --> F\n  F --> RR[Rerank]\n  RR --> C[Cutoff]\n  C --> G[Generate]\n  G --> A[Answer with citations]"},{"t":"p","md":"**Naive RAG** is one embedding search followed by one prompt. **Advanced RAG** adds query transformation before retrieval and fusion, reranking and filtering after it. **Modular RAG** treats those pieces as swappable parts that a router can skip or loop [[tr8]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Quality is lost in a predictable order. The generator cannot use evidence that was never retrieved, so **retrieval recall is the ceiling** for answer quality. Anthropic reports a top-20 retrieval failure rate of 5.7% for its baseline. Contextual embeddings cut it to 3.7%, adding contextual BM25 to 2.9%, and adding reranking brought the total reduction to 67% [[r26]]. Those are vendor numbers, but they show the lesson: each retrieval stage you add moves a measurable failure rate."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Measure stages, not the final answer","md":"Log the query, the rewrite, per-retriever ranks, fused and reranked order, the cutoff decision and the final context. Then score retrieval (is the gold chunk in the top k?) separately from generation (given the gold chunk, is the answer right?)."}],"whereToUse":["Question answering over private documents that change faster than you can retrain a model","Support assistants that must cite a policy or manual page","Internal search copilots where users type identifiers and paraphrases into one box","Any system where an auditor asks which source produced a given sentence"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Knowledge is too big or too fast-changing to place in every prompt","Answers must be traceable to a source","Different users may see different documents","You can label a small set of questions with their gold chunks"],"avoid":["The whole corpus fits in a cached prompt and rarely changes","The task needs reasoning over the entire corpus at once, where top-k chunks cannot hold the evidence","No one will measure retrieval separately, so failures stay invisible"],"flips":["Corpus under roughly 200,000 tokens and stable: test a long prompt first [[r26]]","Questions need whole-corpus themes: consider a graph or tree index instead of flat top-k","Questions are multi-part: add decomposition or agentic retrieval before tuning the reranker"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Naive RAG (embed, top-k, prompt)","Prototype, single-topic corpus, paraphrase questions","Identifiers, multi-part questions, noisy corpora","Cheap, but plausible wrong answers"],["Advanced RAG (hybrid, rerank, cutoff)","Mixed query types, mid-size corpora","Latency budget is under a second","More stages to tune and monitor"],["Agentic retrieval","Multi-part, context-dependent questions","Single-fact lookups, GA-only rules","Extra model calls, preview features"],["Long prompt or cache","Small stable corpus, shared access","Large or per-user corpora","Token cost and accuracy drop with length"],["Fine-tuning only","Style and format changes","Facts that change, need for citations","Stale knowledge, no provenance"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Fluent, confident answers that are wrong","cause":"Retrieval missed the evidence and the model filled the gap from memory","fix":"Add a cutoff with abstention, require citations, and measure recall@k on labelled questions"},{"symptom":"Tuning the prompt for weeks with no gain","cause":"The fault is upstream in parsing, chunking or retrieval","fix":"Run the oracle test: give the model the gold chunk. If it still fails, tune generation; if not, fix retrieval"},{"symptom":"Quality drops after a re-index","cause":"Embedding model, chunk size or analyzer changed without a regression set","fix":"Keep a golden query set and compare recall@k before and after every pipeline change"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w05-t01-s1","title":"Plausible but wrong, prototype stage","situation":"A team shipped a naive RAG prototype. Reviewers say about one answer in five is fluent and wrong. The team proposes a bigger model.","constraints":["One engineer, two weeks","A hundred real questions exist but no gold chunks","No latency target yet"],"unknowns":["Is the evidence present in the corpus at all?","Do the wrong answers cluster by query type?"],"question":"What do you do first?","options":[{"name":"Label gold chunks for the failing questions and measure retrieval recall@k and generation separately","verdict":"best","why":"It tells you which stage to fix before you spend on anything. A wrong answer with the gold chunk missing is a retrieval fault.","tradeoff":"Costs a day of labelling before any visible improvement."},{"name":"Add a reranker and a query rewriter at once","verdict":"good","why":"Both often help, and advanced RAG usually needs them.","tradeoff":"You cannot tell which one helped, and a rewrite can erase identifiers."},{"name":"Switch to a larger generation model","verdict":"poor","why":"If the evidence was never retrieved, a better writer only fabricates more fluently.","tradeoff":"Right only after the oracle test shows the model fails even with the gold chunk in context."}],"flips":[{"if":"The oracle test shows the model fails with the gold chunk in context","then":"Tune the prompt, context order and model, not retrieval."},{"if":"The failures are all multi-part questions","then":"Add decomposition or agentic retrieval for that slice."}],"takeaway":"Find the failing stage before you buy a fix."},{"id":"w05-t01-s2","title":"Whole corpus is 90,000 tokens","situation":"A product team wants a RAG stack for one manual set that changes twice a year. Every employee may read all of it.","constraints":["Corpus is about 90,000 tokens","Shared access","Moderate query volume"],"unknowns":["Is prompt caching available on the chosen model?","Do answers need passage-level citations?"],"question":"Which design do you start with?","options":[{"name":"Put the manual in a cached prompt and measure","verdict":"best","why":"It is under Anthropic's rule of thumb of 200,000 tokens, it is stable and access is shared, so no retrieval stage can lose evidence [[r26]].","tradeoff":"Per-query token cost and long-context degradation; revisit if the manual grows."},{"name":"Hybrid search with reranker over chunked manuals","verdict":"good","why":"Cheaper per query at scale and ready for growth.","tradeoff":"More moving parts for a corpus that does not need them yet."},{"name":"GraphRAG over the manuals","verdict":"poor","why":"Indexing cost and operational weight for a small stable corpus with lookup questions.","tradeoff":"Fits when the corpus is large and questions ask for themes across it."}],"flips":[{"if":"The corpus grows past what fits comfortably in the window","then":"Move to hybrid retrieval with a reranker."},{"if":"Access becomes per user","then":"A shared cached prompt cannot hide documents, so retrieve with a security filter."}],"takeaway":"Size and update rate decide whether you need a pipeline at all."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-t01-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Which stage sets the ceiling on answer quality in a RAG system?","options":["Generation prompt wording","Citation formatting","Retrieval recall","Token streaming"],"answer":2,"explain":"If the evidence is not retrieved the model cannot use it. Prompt wording and formatting refine what is already present, and streaming affects latency only."},{"id":"w05-t01-q2","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the query-time stages in order.","items":["Rewrite the query","Retrieve candidates","Fuse the lists","Rerank","Cut off weak evidence","Generate with citations"],"explain":"Transformation comes before retrieval, fusion merges the retriever outputs, reranking reorders the fused shortlist, the cutoff drops weak evidence and generation comes last."},{"id":"w05-t01-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"The model gives a wrong answer even when you paste the gold chunk into the prompt. Where is the fault?","options":["Retrieval recall","Generation or context handling","Chunk size at ingestion","The fusion constant"],"answer":1,"explain":"The oracle test removes retrieval from the picture. A failure with gold evidence in context points to the prompt, context order or the model. The other options sit upstream of what the oracle test bypasses."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Name the query-time stages of an advanced RAG pipeline.","back":"Query transformation, retrieval, fusion, reranking, cutoff, context assembly, generation, citation."},{"front":"What is the oracle test for a bad RAG answer?","back":"Put the gold chunk in the prompt. Still wrong means a generation fault. Right means retrieval missed it."},{"front":"Naive, advanced and modular RAG: what separates them?","back":"Naive is one search then one prompt. Advanced adds pre- and post-retrieval steps. Modular makes stages swappable and routable."}],"related":["w05-t17","w05-t04","w05-t15","w04-t01","w05-l1","w05-l6"],"refs":["tr8","tr9","r26","r8"]},{"id":"w05-t02","order":2,"group":"Baseline","title":"Lexical and sparse retrieval (BM25, SPLADE)","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Lexical retrieval matches the words of the query against an inverted index and scores with BM25. Learned sparse models such as SPLADE keep the inverted index but learn which terms matter.","useIf":"Users search with identifiers, names, codes, dates or jargon, or you need explainable matches and no embedding cost.","avoidIf":"Queries are paraphrases that share few words with the documents and nothing else covers vocabulary mismatch.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"An **inverted index** maps each term to the documents that contain it. **BM25** scores a document for a query by summing, per query term, how rare the term is across the index (IDF) and how often it appears in the document (TF), with two controls. `k1` sets how fast extra occurrences stop adding score, and `b` sets how strongly long documents are penalised [[tr6]]. In Azure AI Search, BM25 is fixed on services created after July 2020, the defaults are `k1` 1.2 and `b` 0.75, and `@search.score` has no upper bound [[tr5]] [[tr6]]."},{"t":"formula","title":"BM25 in its standard form","expr":"score(q,d) = sum over terms t in q of IDF(t) * tf * (k1 + 1) / (tf + k1 * (1 - b + b * |d| / avgdl))","vars":[{"k":"tf","v":"occurrences of the term in document d"},{"k":"k1","v":"term-frequency saturation, 1.2 by default in Azure"},{"k":"b","v":"length normalisation from 0 to 1, 0.75 by default in Azure"},{"k":"|d| / avgdl","v":"document length relative to the average length"}],"example":"With `k1` at 0, a term counts once whether it appears one time or fifty. Larger `k1` lets repeated terms keep adding score, which favours documents that match many query terms [[tr6]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Learned sparse retrieval** keeps the inverted-index serving model but replaces counts with weights a neural model learns. SPLADE adds explicit sparsity regularisation and a log-saturation effect on term weights, so queries and documents become sparse vectors that include expansion terms the text never contained [[tr1]]. You gain some synonym coverage and still get index-friendly serving. The Azure vector pages read on 2026-10-13 do not document SPLADE-style sparse or late-interaction retrieval, so using them means another engine (UNVERIFIED for Azure support)."},{"t":"p","md":"Why keep lexical retrieval in 2026? BEIR found BM25 a reliable baseline out of domain, where dense models often lose [[r11]]. Azure's own guidance says product codes, specialised jargon, dates and people's names do better with keyword search [[r43]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Analyzer settings decide whether an identifier matches","md":"A tokenizer that splits `ERR-4012` into `err` and `4012` changes what BM25 sees. Test your identifiers through the analyzer you configured, and keep a field that preserves them whole."}],"whereToUse":["First-stage retrieval for support, legal and engineering corpora full of codes, clause numbers and part numbers","The keyword leg of a hybrid query, fused with a vector leg","Search over short structured fields such as titles, SKUs and names","A cheap baseline against which every dense or hybrid experiment is judged"],"whenToUse":{"use":["A large share of queries contain exact tokens users expect to match","You need to show why a document matched","Out-of-domain robustness matters more than paraphrase coverage","You cannot afford embedding and re-embedding costs"],"avoid":["Users describe symptoms in their own words and the documents use other vocabulary","Cross-language queries with no shared tokens","You rely on BM25 alone for conceptual questions"],"flips":["Paraphrase share grows: add a vector leg and fuse with RRF","You control the engine and need expansion without embeddings at query time: consider learned sparse retrieval","Identifiers dominate: weight the keyword leg up or route those queries to BM25 first"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["BM25","Identifiers, names, dates, jargon, out-of-domain","Vocabulary mismatch, paraphrase","Analyzer tuning, no semantics"],["Learned sparse (SPLADE style)","You need expansion with inverted-index serving","Your engine has no sparse-vector support","Model inference at index and query time, another stack"],["Dense vectors","Paraphrase and conceptual questions","Rare terms, exact codes","Embedding cost, ANN index memory"],["Hybrid (BM25 plus dense)","Mixed query types","Latency budget is tight, no labelled data to tune","Two retrievals and a fusion step"],["Late interaction (ColBERT style)","Fine-grained token matching at high quality","Storage is constrained","Index size can grow about tenfold before compression [[r12]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Exact error code returns related but wrong pages","cause":"The analyzer split the code into tokens that are common elsewhere","fix":"Index the code in a whole-token field, and test identifiers through the analyzer"},{"symptom":"Results reorder between identical queries","cause":"Tied scores have undefined order, and replicas compute index statistics per replica","fix":"Add a tiebreaker such as `$orderby=search.score() desc, Rating desc`, and do not compare raw scores across runs [[tr5]]"},{"symptom":"Every result has `@search.score` of 1","cause":"The query was a wildcard, fuzzy, regex or empty search, which are not scored","fix":"Use a scored full-text query when you need ranking"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w05-t02-s1","title":"Runbook search by error code","situation":"Engineers paste error codes and service names into an assistant. The runbook corpus is a few hundred thousand chunks and rarely changes.","constraints":["Most queries contain an exact code","Runbooks use the same codes as the logs","Moderate budget"],"unknowns":["What share of queries are paraphrases?","Does the analyzer keep codes intact?"],"question":"What do you build first?","options":[{"name":"BM25 with a whole-token field for codes, then add vectors only if paraphrase misses show up","verdict":"best","why":"Codes are the use case lexical retrieval was made for, and it is cheap and explainable.","tradeoff":"Paraphrased symptoms will miss until you add a vector leg."},{"name":"Vector-only retrieval with a strong embedding model","verdict":"poor","why":"Dense retrievers generalise poorly to rare entities and exact strings [[tr2]].","tradeoff":"Right when queries are symptoms in plain language and documents are prose."},{"name":"Hybrid from day one with a semantic ranker","verdict":"good","why":"Covers both query types.","tradeoff":"Higher cost and complexity before you know the paraphrase share."}],"flips":[{"if":"A third of queries are paraphrased symptoms","then":"Add a vector leg and fuse with RRF."},{"if":"The engine supports learned sparse vectors and you need expansion","then":"Evaluate a SPLADE-style model against BM25 on your labelled queries."}],"takeaway":"Exact tokens favour lexical retrieval."},{"id":"w05-t02-s2","title":"Tiny team, mixed language corpus","situation":"A two-person team indexes support articles written in several languages. Users write in their own language, and some articles are translations.","constraints":["Queries and documents may not share tokens","No ML platform team","Quality matters more than cost"],"unknowns":["Which languages and how many?","Do the analyzers exist for each language?"],"question":"Is BM25 alone acceptable?","options":[{"name":"Add a multilingual embedding vector leg and fuse with BM25","verdict":"best","why":"Cross-language matches need semantic similarity, while BM25 still catches product names that stay untranslated.","tradeoff":"Embedding cost and a second index to keep in sync."},{"name":"BM25 alone with per-language analyzers","verdict":"good","why":"Works when users search in the document language.","tradeoff":"Fails on cross-language queries."},{"name":"Translate every document into English before indexing","verdict":"poor","why":"A costly pipeline that still loses nuance and names.","tradeoff":"Reasonable only for a small corpus with a single target language."}],"flips":[{"if":"Users always search in the language of the document","then":"BM25 with per-language analyzers is enough."},{"if":"Quality matters less than cost and users only need roughly relevant articles","then":"Keep BM25 with per-language analyzers and skip the embedding bill, accepting misses on cross-language queries."}],"takeaway":"Lexical retrieval cannot cross a vocabulary gap."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-t02-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"In BM25, what does raising `k1` do?","options":["Penalises long documents more","Raises the IDF of rare terms","Switches scoring to cosine similarity","Lets repeated occurrences of a term keep adding score for longer"],"answer":3,"explain":"`k1` controls term-frequency saturation. Length penalty is `b`, IDF comes from index statistics, and BM25 does not use cosine similarity."},{"id":"w05-t02-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Select the conditions that favour keeping BM25 in the stack.","options":["Queries contain product codes and names","You need to show why a hit matched","Every query is a conceptual paraphrase","Out-of-domain robustness matters"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Exact tokens, explainability and out-of-domain robustness favour lexical retrieval. Pure paraphrase workloads favour dense retrieval, though a hybrid leg can still help."},{"id":"w05-t02-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each term to its role.","pairs":[{"l":"`k1`","r":"Term-frequency saturation"},{"l":"`b`","r":"Document length normalisation"},{"l":"SPLADE","r":"Learned sparse term weights with expansion"},{"l":"`@search.score`","r":"Unbounded BM25 relevance value"}],"explain":"`k1` and `b` are the two BM25 controls, SPLADE learns sparse weights and the Azure BM25 score has no upper limit."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What are the Azure BM25 defaults for `k1` and `b`?","back":"`k1` is 1.2 and `b` is 0.75. BM25 is fixed on services created after July 2020."},{"front":"What does SPLADE add to sparse retrieval?","back":"Learned term weights with expansion terms, sparsity regularisation and log saturation, still served from an inverted index."},{"front":"Why can identical BM25 queries reorder results?","back":"Tied scores have undefined order, and each replica computes index statistics separately."}],"related":["w05-t03","w05-t04","w05-l1","w04-t02"],"refs":["tr1","tr5","tr6","r11","r12","r43","tr2"]},{"id":"w05-t03","order":3,"group":"Baseline","title":"Dense vector retrieval","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Dense retrieval embeds the query and every chunk into vectors and returns the nearest chunks. It matches meaning rather than words, and it always returns neighbours, relevant or not.","useIf":"Users paraphrase, describe symptoms in their own words or search across languages, and the documents use different vocabulary.","avoidIf":"Queries hinge on rare entities, codes or exact strings and you have no keyword leg beside the vectors.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A **bi-encoder** embedding model maps text to a vector. Chunks are embedded once at ingestion. At query time the query goes through the same model and an approximate nearest neighbour (ANN) index returns the closest chunks by cosine similarity or another metric. Because query and chunk never see each other inside the model, millions of chunks can be searched fast [[tr7]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w05-t03-d1","title":"Dense retrieval at query time","alt":"A query is embedded, searched in a vector index, and the nearest chunks come back with similarity scores.","src":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant U as User\n  participant E as Embedding model\n  participant I as Vector index\n  U->>E: Query text\n  E->>I: Query vector\n  I-->>U: Top k chunks and scores"},{"t":"p","md":"**Strengths.** Synonyms, paraphrase and conceptual questions match without shared words. Azure puts it this way: the advantage of vector search is finding information that is conceptually similar to the query even when no keyword matches [[r43]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Weaknesses.** In the EntityQuestions study, dense retrievers drastically underperformed sparse methods on simple entity-rich questions, and generalised only to common entities unless the question pattern was seen in training [[tr2]]. BEIR found dense models often weaker than BM25 out of domain [[r11]]. Two operational traps follow. First, **nearest neighbours always exist**, so a gibberish query still returns k chunks. You need a threshold or an abstention rule. Azure's vector `threshold` (preview in `2026-08-01-preview`) drops weak matches before fusion even when fewer than `k` remain [[r35]]. Second, scores are model specific: Azure documents cosine similarity from 0.333 to 1.00, and a cutoff tuned for one embedding model is wrong for another [[r1]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Clean the query like the chunks","md":"Apply the same text normalisation to the query that you applied to chunks before embedding, for example lowercasing. A mismatch quietly lowers similarity [[r8]]."}],"whereToUse":["Help-centre and FAQ search where users describe problems in their own words","Cross-language retrieval with a multilingual embedding model","The vector leg of hybrid search, paired with BM25","Recommendation of related documents or similar tickets"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Paraphrase and vocabulary mismatch are common","You can test several embedding models on your own queries","Documents are prose rather than codes or tables","You have a plan for thresholds and abstention"],"avoid":["Queries are mostly identifiers, names or exact phrases","The domain is highly specialised and the embedding model has not seen it","You cannot afford re-embedding when you change models"],"flips":["Exact-match failures appear in the logs: add a BM25 leg and fuse","Domain jargon is rare: evaluate a domain-tuned or larger model on labelled queries","Weak matches reach the model: add a vector threshold or a reranker-based cutoff"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Dense only","Paraphrase, conceptual, cross-language queries","Rare entities, codes, exact strings","Always returns k hits, even for no-answer queries"],["BM25 only","Exact tokens, out-of-domain, explainability","Vocabulary mismatch","Misses paraphrases entirely"],["Hybrid","Mixed query traffic","Budget allows only one retrieval","Two searches and a fusion step"],["Dense plus reranker","Shortlist is good but order is poor","Relevant chunk is outside the shortlist","Per-query reranker cost"],["Fine-tuned embeddings","Stable domain with labelled pairs","No training data, fast-changing vocabulary","Training and re-embedding effort"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A query for a part number returns thematically related parts","cause":"The embedding blurs rare tokens into a neighbourhood of similar text","fix":"Add a keyword leg and fuse, or route identifier-shaped queries to BM25"},{"symptom":"Nonsense questions still produce confident answers","cause":"Nearest-neighbour search returns k results regardless of relevance","fix":"Apply a vector threshold or a reranker cutoff and let the system abstain"},{"symptom":"Scores shift after an embedding model upgrade and cutoffs break","cause":"Similarity distributions are specific to the model","fix":"Recalibrate thresholds on a labelled set whenever the model or version changes"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w05-t03-s1","title":"FAQ bot over two thousand questions","situation":"A consumer product has a stable FAQ of about two thousand entries. Customers phrase questions in many ways, and the FAQ never contains codes.","constraints":["Small corpus","Paraphrase-heavy queries","Low volume, modest budget"],"unknowns":["Are there product names or SKUs in queries?","Is a no-answer path required?"],"question":"Which retrieval design fits?","options":[{"name":"Dense retrieval with a similarity threshold and a fallback message","verdict":"best","why":"Paraphrase is where dense retrieval shines, the corpus is small and a threshold handles no-answer queries.","tradeoff":"Threshold needs calibration on real queries and recalibration if the model changes."},{"name":"Hybrid with semantic ranker","verdict":"good","why":"Handles any identifiers that appear later.","tradeoff":"More cost and moving parts than this corpus needs today."},{"name":"BM25 only","verdict":"poor","why":"Customer wording rarely matches FAQ wording.","tradeoff":"Right when users search with exact product names or error codes."}],"flips":[{"if":"Customers start pasting order numbers or SKUs","then":"Add a BM25 leg and fuse."},{"if":"The FAQ grows to millions of entries and memory cost becomes the main concern","then":"Keep dense retrieval but compress vectors with quantization and test recall, instead of adding a second engine."}],"takeaway":"Paraphrase traffic is the dense sweet spot."},{"id":"w05-t03-s2","title":"Patent-style corpus with rare terms","situation":"Analysts search a large corpus of technical filings. Queries contain rare compound names and numbered claims alongside conceptual descriptions.","constraints":["Large corpus","High recall requirement","Labelled queries available"],"unknowns":["How rare are the entities?","Is the embedding model trained on this domain?"],"question":"Can you ship dense retrieval alone?","options":[{"name":"Hybrid retrieval and measure recall@k against dense-only on the labelled queries","verdict":"best","why":"Rare entities are the known weak spot of dense retrievers [[tr2]], and the labelled set tells you how much the keyword leg adds.","tradeoff":"Two retrievals per query and a fusion step to tune."},{"name":"Dense only with a larger embedding model","verdict":"poor","why":"A bigger model can still fail on rare strings, and you pay more for every re-embedding.","tradeoff":"Reasonable if the evaluation shows no gain from a keyword leg."},{"name":"BM25 only","verdict":"good","why":"Strong on the rare terms and out of domain.","tradeoff":"Misses conceptual descriptions with different vocabulary."}],"flips":[{"if":"Evaluation shows dense-only matches hybrid recall","then":"Drop the keyword leg and save a retrieval."},{"if":"Recall on rare compound names is the weak slice","then":"Keep the keyword leg and weight it up for those queries; a larger embedding model rarely fixes exact-token misses."}],"takeaway":"Measure the keyword leg's lift before you drop it."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-t03-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A vector-only assistant answers a nonsense question with a confident answer from an unrelated chunk. What is the most direct cause?","options":["The ANN index is corrupt","The embedding model is too small","Nearest-neighbour search always returns k results and nothing stops weak ones","BM25 was disabled"],"answer":2,"explain":"Dense search returns the nearest vectors whatever their distance. A threshold or abstention rule is what filters them. Index corruption and model size are not required to produce this behaviour, and BM25 is not part of a vector-only design."},{"id":"w05-t03-q2","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Which change in traffic most clearly argues for adding a keyword leg to a dense retriever?","options":["More queries containing part numbers and error codes","Longer natural-language questions","More users","Larger chunks"],"answer":0,"explain":"Exact tokens are the dense blind spot [[tr2]]. Longer questions and more users do not change retrieval mode, and chunk size is a separate dial."},{"id":"w05-t03-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Select the true statements about thresholds on vector similarity.","options":["They are specific to the embedding model","They can drop weak matches before fusion","A threshold tuned on one model carries over to another unchanged","They remove the need for evaluation"],"answers":[0,1],"explain":"Similarity distributions depend on the model, and Azure's preview vector threshold drops weak matches before fusion [[r35]]. A threshold does not transfer across models and still needs a labelled set to tune."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why does dense retrieval return results for a nonsense query?","back":"Nearest-neighbour search always returns the k closest vectors. Only a threshold or abstention rule can stop weak ones."},{"front":"What did EntityQuestions show about dense retrievers?","back":"They drastically underperformed sparse methods on simple entity-rich questions, generalising only to common entities."},{"front":"Why must the query be cleaned like the chunks?","back":"A normalisation mismatch between query and chunk text, such as casing, quietly lowers similarity."}],"related":["w05-t02","w05-t04","w05-t06","w04-t12","w04-t15","w05-l1"],"refs":["tr2","tr7","r8","r11","r35","r43","r1"]},{"id":"w05-t04","order":4,"group":"Baseline","title":"Hybrid search and RRF","minutes":9,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Hybrid search runs keyword and vector retrieval in parallel and merges the ranked lists. Reciprocal rank fusion (RRF) merges by rank, so incomparable scores never need normalising.","useIf":"Query traffic mixes exact identifiers with paraphrases, and you want one ranked list without tuning score scales.","avoidIf":"All queries are of one kind, or you have labelled data and a tuned convex combination measurably beats RRF.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"BM25 scores have no upper limit. Cosine similarity in Azure sits between 0.333 and 1.00. Adding them directly would let one retriever dominate by scale alone. **RRF** sidesteps the problem: each list gives a document `1 / (rank + k)`, and the contributions are summed. Azure applies it whenever two or more queries run in parallel: a text query plus one vector query is two executions, and a text query plus two vector queries over five vector fields is eleven [[r1]]."},{"t":"formula","title":"Reciprocal rank fusion with weights","expr":"RRF(d) = sum over lists i of  w_i / (k + rank_i(d))","vars":[{"k":"k","v":"fusion constant, 60 in the original paper. Not the neighbour count `k` of a vector query"},{"k":"w_i","v":"list weight, default 1.0. In Azure it is `vectorQueries[].weight`"}],"example":"At `k = 60`, rank 1 gives 1/61 = 0.0164 and rank 50 gives 1/110 = 0.0091. Rank 3 in both lists gives 2/63 = 0.0317. Two lists top out at 0.0328 [[r9]]."},{"t":"lab","id":"rrf-playground","intro":"Fuse a keyword list and a vector list yourself. Try the identifier preset first, change `k` and weights, and watch which document wins and why."},{"t":"p","md":"**What is documented.** Cormack et al. fixed `k = 60` in a pilot on TREC collections, found it near-optimal but 'not critical', and RRF beat Condorcet Fuse, CombMNZ and several learning-to-rank methods in their runs [[r9]]. Azure calls `k` a constant and mentions a small value such as 60, but does not state the exact value it uses or a request parameter to change it (UNVERIFIED on 2026-10-13) [[r1]]. Elasticsearch exposes `rank_constant` with default 60 [[r32]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Azure dials.** `vectorQueries[].weight` scales a query's contribution. `maxTextRecallSize` (default 1,000, preview in `2026-08-01-preview`) bounds the text side. Without paging a hybrid query returns the top 50 fused results, and the docs suggest starting with `k` 30 to 50 and `top` 10 to 20. If the semantic ranker follows, make `k` and `maxTextRecallSize` sum to at least 50 [[r2]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"RRF is not tuning-free","md":"Bruch et al. found a convex combination of normalised scores beat RRF in and out of domain, found RRF sensitive to its parameters, and needed only a small labelled set [[r10]]. RRF is a strong default when you have no labels. With labels, test both."}],"whereToUse":["Enterprise search where users type part numbers, names and natural-language questions in one box","RAG retrieval ahead of a semantic ranker or cross-encoder","Multi-field vector indexes where title and body embeddings are queried together","Search stacks that already expose hybrid fusion, such as Azure AI Search, Elasticsearch and OpenAI file search"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Query traffic is mixed","Scores from the retrievers are not comparable","You have no labelled data to tune a weighted score","You want a default that rarely needs tuning"],"avoid":["Every query is an identifier, where BM25 alone is cheaper","You need a calibrated confidence from the fused score, which RRF cannot give","Latency allows only one retrieval"],"flips":["Labelled queries are available: compare a tuned convex combination against RRF [[r10]]","One retriever clearly dominates: raise its weight, as `weight` allows","You need abstention: threshold the reranker score or each leg, never the fused RRF score"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["RRF","No labels, incomparable scores, several lists","Score magnitude carries signal you want to keep","Ignores confidence, parameter sensitivity [[r10]]"],["Convex combination of normalised scores","A small labelled set exists","Scores are unstable or you cannot normalise them","Needs normalisation and tuning"],["Learned ranker over features","Large labelled data and many signals","Little data","Training, serving and drift"],["Single retriever","One query type dominates","Mixed traffic","Blind spots remain"],["Semantic reranker only on one list","Shortlist recall is already high","Relevant chunks are missed by that retriever","Cannot find new documents [[r3]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Hybrid results look almost identical to BM25 alone","cause":"The vector leg has a tiny `k`, a very low weight or is returning weak neighbours","fix":"Inspect per-leg ranks, raise vector `k` and check weights"},{"symptom":"A strongly relevant exact match sinks below mediocre agreement hits","cause":"RRF ignores score magnitude, so a runaway rank 1 in one list counts like any rank 1","fix":"Weight that list up, or route identifier queries to BM25 first"},{"symptom":"A cutoff on the fused score gives random abstentions","cause":"RRF score is bounded by about 1/k per list and depends on the number of lists, not relevance","fix":"Threshold each leg or the reranker score. Use RRF for order only [[r1]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w05-t04-s1","title":"Mixed traffic, no labels","situation":"A new internal assistant launches next month. Users paste error codes and also ask policy questions in plain language. There is no evaluation set yet.","constraints":["No labelled queries","Azure AI Search available","Latency budget allows two parallel searches and a reranker"],"unknowns":["What share of queries are identifiers?","Can you collect a labelled set during the pilot?"],"question":"How do you fuse the lists?","options":[{"name":"Hybrid query with RRF at default weights, then a semantic ranker, and log queries for later tuning","verdict":"best","why":"RRF needs no normalisation or labels, and hybrid plus semantic ranking is the documented default for mixed traffic [[r43]].","tradeoff":"You give up score-magnitude information and a possible gain from tuning."},{"name":"Normalise both scores and tune a convex combination","verdict":"poor","why":"Without labels you cannot choose the mix or trust the normalisation.","tradeoff":"Right once you have a labelled set, where it can beat RRF [[r10]]."},{"name":"BM25 only until the pilot ends","verdict":"good","why":"Cheap and handles the identifiers.","tradeoff":"Paraphrase questions miss, and you never see hybrid data."}],"flips":[{"if":"You collect a few hundred labelled queries","then":"Compare weighted RRF and a convex combination on recall@k and nDCG."},{"if":"The latency budget allows only one search call","then":"Run vector-only or BM25-only based on the observed query mix, and add hybrid once a second parallel call fits."}],"takeaway":"RRF is the label-free default."},{"id":"w05-t04-s2","title":"Labelled set, one retriever dominates","situation":"A team has 800 labelled queries. Offline tests show the vector leg finds the gold chunk far more often than BM25, except for identifier queries.","constraints":["Labelled set available","Weights are exposed on vector queries","Evaluation runs in CI"],"unknowns":["Is the gap stable across query types?","Is a query router affordable?"],"question":"What do you change?","options":[{"name":"Tune weights and `k` against the labelled set, and compare with a convex combination","verdict":"best","why":"You have the data that makes tuning meaningful, and the fusion function study found tuned convex combinations strong [[r10]].","tradeoff":"Tuned values can drift when the corpus or models change, so keep the CI check."},{"name":"Keep RRF defaults because RRF needs no tuning","verdict":"poor","why":"Equal weights waste a known advantage, and RRF is parameter sensitive.","tradeoff":"Fine when no labels exist."},{"name":"Drop the keyword leg","verdict":"good","why":"Simplest if identifier queries are rare.","tradeoff":"Identifier queries then fail."}],"flips":[{"if":"Identifier queries are easy to detect","then":"Route them to BM25 and keep hybrid for the rest."},{"if":"The labelled set shrinks to a few dozen queries","then":"Keep RRF defaults: weights tuned on so few queries overfit, so wait for more labels."}],"takeaway":"Labels turn tuning from guesswork into measurement."},{"id":"w05-t04-s3","title":"Weak neighbours on no-answer queries","situation":"Hybrid retrieval returns 50 fused results for every query, including questions the corpus cannot answer. The team wants a cutoff.","constraints":["Semantic ranker is enabled","Wrong answers carry moderate risk","A golden set with unanswerable queries exists"],"unknowns":["How stable is the reranker score across updates?","What false-answer rate is tolerable?"],"question":"Where do you put the cutoff?","options":[{"name":"Coarse cutoff on the reranker score plus a vector threshold on the vector leg, calibrated on the golden set","verdict":"best","why":"These signals relate to relevance. Azure warns against granular reranker cutoffs because the distribution shifts with model updates, so keep it coarse and monitor [[r3]].","tradeoff":"Needs recalibration after service updates."},{"name":"Threshold on the fused RRF score","verdict":"poor","why":"RRF values depend on the number of lists and `k`, not on relevance.","tradeoff":"Fine for ordering only."},{"name":"Always answer from the top 5","verdict":"good","why":"Simple, and acceptable for low-risk assistants.","tradeoff":"No-answer queries get fabricated answers."}],"flips":[{"if":"The service model updates and abstention rate jumps","then":"Recalibrate on the golden set and keep the bands coarse."},{"if":"The semantic ranker is not enabled","then":"Cut on the vector leg's own similarity threshold and a minimum fused rank, since RRF scores carry no absolute meaning."}],"takeaway":"Order with RRF, decide with relevance signals."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-t04-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"With `k = 60` and equal weights, a document is rank 3 in the keyword list and rank 3 in the vector list. What is its RRF score?","answer":0.0317,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"RRF points","hint":"Sum 1/(60+rank) over both lists.","explain":"Each list gives 1/63 = 0.015873. Two lists give 0.031746, about 0.0317. A document found in only one list at rank 3 would score half of that."},{"id":"w05-t04-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A team thresholds on the fused RRF score and abstention looks random. Why?","options":["RRF scores are probabilities","The semantic ranker overwrote it","Vector `k` was too large","The score depends on `k` and the number of lists, not on relevance"],"answer":3,"explain":"RRF adds `1/(k+rank)` terms, so values reflect rank agreement and list count. They are not probabilities, and the semantic ranker reports a separate score."},{"id":"w05-t04-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Select the situations in which a tuned convex combination is worth testing against RRF.","options":["You have a few hundred labelled queries","One retriever is clearly stronger","You have zero evaluation data","Scores can be normalised reliably"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Labels, a dominant retriever and normalisable scores favour a tuned combination [[r10]]. With no evaluation data you cannot tune it, so RRF is the safer default."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does RRF compute for a document?","back":"The sum over lists of `weight / (k + rank)`, with ranks from 1. Missing documents add nothing."},{"front":"Why can you not threshold on an RRF score?","back":"It is bounded by about 1/k per list and depends on the number of lists, so it orders results but does not measure relevance."},{"front":"How does hybrid search feed the Azure semantic ranker?","back":"Only the top 50 results go to the ranker, so vector `k` plus `maxTextRecallSize` should sum to at least 50."},{"front":"Which paper argues RRF is not tuning-free?","back":"Bruch et al. 2022: a convex combination beat RRF and RRF was sensitive to its parameters."}],"related":["w05-l2","w05-t05","w05-t06","w05-t02","w05-t03","w04-t18"],"refs":["r1","r2","r3","r9","r10","r32","r43"]},{"id":"w05-t05","order":5,"group":"Ranking","title":"Rerankers","minutes":9,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A reranker rescoring a shortlist with a model that reads query and chunk together. It improves order, not recall: it cannot find chunks the first stage missed.","useIf":"The right chunk is usually in the shortlist but not at the top, and you can afford extra latency and cost per query.","avoidIf":"The right chunk is not in the candidate set, or the latency budget leaves no room for a second model pass.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"First-stage retrievers are built for speed over millions of chunks, so they score query and chunk separately. A **cross-encoder** feeds the query and one candidate through a transformer together and outputs a single relevance score, with attention across both. It is more accurate and too slow for a whole corpus, so it rescores only a shortlist, for example 100 candidates [[tr7]]. BEIR found re-ranking and late-interaction models gave the best zero-shot average, at high compute cost [[r11]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w05-t05-d1","title":"Retrieve wide, rerank narrow","alt":"A first stage returns many candidates, the top 50 go to the reranker, and a handful of the best go to the model.","src":"flowchart LR\n  A[Corpus] --> B[Hybrid retrieval]\n  B --> C[Top 50 candidates]\n  C --> D[Reranker]\n  D --> E[Top 5 to 10]\n  E --> F[Prompt]"},{"t":"table","caption":"Reranker families and their dials","head":["Family","How it scores","Notes from the sources"],"rows":[["Azure semantic ranker","Managed ranking inside the search service","Reranks the top 50 only and cannot find new documents. About 2,000 tokens per document feed summarisation, and the summary string is capped at 2,048 tokens (256 before November 2024). `@search.rerankerScore` runs 0 to 4 [[r3]]"],["Cohere Rerank API","Hosted cross-encoder","`rerank-v4.0-pro` and `rerank-v4.0-fast` take 32k context, `rerank-v3.5` takes 4096 tokens. Results carry `index` and `relevance_score`. YAML strings recommended for structured documents [[r31]]"],["Self-hosted cross-encoder","Open model you run","You control data residency and cost, and you own GPUs and updates [[tr7]]"],["LLM reranker","Prompted model orders a window of candidates","RankGPT found instructed GPT-class models competitive with supervised rerankers. Cost is an extra model call per window [[r13]]"],["ColBERT late interaction","Token-level interaction over precomputed vectors","Encodes query and document independently [[tr11]]. Index grows about tenfold, which ColBERTv2 compresses [[r12]]. Not documented in the Azure vector pages read on 2026-10-13"]],"highlightCol":2},{"t":"p","md":"**Where reranking pays.** In Anthropic's tests the top-20 retrieval failure rate fell from 5.7% to 3.7% with contextual embeddings and to 2.9% with contextual BM25 added. Adding reranking brought the reduction to 67% [[r26]]. **Where it cannot help.** A relevant chunk at candidate position 80 never reaches an Azure reranker that takes 50, so raise first-stage recall before blaming the ranker."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Azure semantic ranker limits that bite","md":"`search=*` or an empty search disables it, `orderBy` with semantic returns HTTP 400, and it is billed when `queryType=semantic` and the search string is not empty. Score distributions shift with model updates, so avoid fine cutoffs [[r3]]."}],"whereToUse":["After hybrid retrieval, where fused order is rough and the model sees only 5 to 10 chunks","Corpora with many near-duplicate or topically similar chunks, where order matters","Long-form answers where the top chunk must truly answer the question","Systems that return extractive captions or answers directly from the ranker"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The gold chunk sits in the top 50 but rarely in the top 5","You can afford extra latency per query (author estimate: tens to hundreds of milliseconds, so measure on your data)","A managed option exists in your search service","Order quality drives answer quality"],"avoid":["Candidate recall is the problem","Strict latency budgets such as voice, with no spare milliseconds","You need stable absolute scores for thresholds across model updates"],"flips":["Data cannot leave your network: use a self-hosted cross-encoder","Documents are long: check the ranker's input limit, since only a summary or window is read","Query volume is huge: shrink the candidate window or rerank only the hard queries"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["No reranker","Latency is tight, first-stage precision is high","Order of the top 10 is noisy","Cheapest, lowest accuracy"],["Managed semantic ranker","You already use Azure AI Search and want in-service processing","Candidate set exceeds 50 or you need stable thresholds","Premium billing, preview-versus-GA differences by feature"],["Hosted rerank API","Fast to adopt, long context needed","Data residency or egress is restricted","Per-request cost, external dependency"],["Self-hosted cross-encoder","Control over data and tuning","Small team without GPU operations","Infrastructure and model upkeep"],["LLM reranker","Highest flexibility, reasoning over criteria","Tight latency or volume","Extra model call per candidate window"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The relevant document never appears even with the ranker on","cause":"It was outside the top 50 candidates, and the ranker only reorders","fix":"Raise first-stage recall: hybrid, query transformation, `k` and `maxTextRecallSize` so they sum to at least 50 [[r2]]"},{"symptom":"Abstention rate jumps after a service update","cause":"The reranker score distribution shifted","fix":"Recalibrate coarse bands on the golden set and monitor score percentiles"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w05-t05-s1","title":"Azure-native stack, order is noisy","situation":"A hybrid index sits in Azure AI Search. Evaluation shows the gold chunk is in the top 50 for nearly every query but only in the top 5 for about half.","constraints":["Data must stay inside the search service boundary","Latency budget allows 300 ms extra","English and German content"],"unknowns":["Is semantic ranker available in the region and tier?","How are scores used downstream?"],"question":"What do you add?","options":[{"name":"Enable the semantic ranker and set coarse bands for any cutoff","verdict":"best","why":"It fits the in-service data constraint and targets exactly the symptom of good recall with weak order [[r3]].","tradeoff":"Premium billing, and scores drift with model updates."},{"name":"Add an LLM reranker over the top 50","verdict":"good","why":"Flexible and often accurate.","tradeoff":"Higher latency and cost, and data leaves the service."},{"name":"Increase `k` to 200 and send everything to the model","verdict":"poor","why":"It floods the context and does not fix order.","tradeoff":"Fine for a model with a large window when ordering matters little."}],"flips":[{"if":"The gold chunk is often outside the top 50","then":"Fix first-stage recall before adding or tuning a ranker."},{"if":"The deployment is outside Azure","then":"Use a hosted rerank API or a self-hosted cross-encoder."}],"takeaway":"Rerank when the order is bad, not when the shortlist is bad."},{"id":"w05-t05-s2","title":"Regulated data, own search engine","situation":"A bank runs OpenSearch inside its network. Documents cannot go to an external API. Analysts accept a two-second response.","constraints":["No external data egress","A small ML platform team exists","Moderate volume"],"unknowns":["GPU capacity?","Languages required?"],"question":"Which reranker?","options":[{"name":"Self-hosted cross-encoder over the top 50 to 100","verdict":"best","why":"It keeps data inside the boundary and the latency budget tolerates a model pass [[tr7]].","tradeoff":"You operate GPUs, evaluate model updates and tune on your data."},{"name":"Hosted rerank API","verdict":"poor","why":"It sends document text outside the network.","tradeoff":"Right when residency rules allow a vendor and you want zero operations."},{"name":"No reranker, trust the hybrid order","verdict":"good","why":"Zero added latency or ops.","tradeoff":"Top-5 order may be noisy, which hurts answer quality."}],"flips":[{"if":"The platform team cannot run GPUs","then":"Ship without a reranker first and revisit with a vendor approved for the data."},{"if":"The response budget tightens to well under a second","then":"Shrink the rerank window to about 20 to 30 candidates or use a smaller distilled cross-encoder."}],"takeaway":"Residency often chooses the reranker for you."},{"id":"w05-t05-s3","title":"Voice assistant with a tight budget","situation":"A voice agent must speak within about a second of the end of the question. Retrieval is allotted 250 ms.","constraints":["Retrieval budget 250 ms","High volume","Short factual questions"],"unknowns":["How precise is first-stage retrieval on a labelled set?","Can the reranker run on a small candidate window?"],"question":"How do you handle reranking?","options":[{"name":"Measure first-stage precision, and rerank only a small window (for example 20) if it fits the budget","verdict":"best","why":"Cost scales with candidates, so a small window may fit, and the measurement shows whether reranking is needed.","tradeoff":"A smaller window lowers the chance the gold chunk is included."},{"name":"Rerank 100 candidates with an LLM","verdict":"poor","why":"An extra model call per window blows the budget [[r13]].","tradeoff":"Right for offline or asynchronous research workflows."},{"name":"Skip reranking permanently","verdict":"good","why":"Simple and fast.","tradeoff":"Gives up accuracy that might be affordable."}],"flips":[{"if":"The budget grows to a second or more","then":"A full 50-candidate rerank becomes affordable."},{"if":"First-stage precision at 20 is already very high on the golden set","then":"Skip the reranker and keep the saved latency, since it would reorder little."}],"takeaway":"Candidate window size is the latency dial."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-t05-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"After enabling the Azure semantic ranker, a relevant document still never appears. Debug output shows it at position 74 before reranking. What is the cause?","options":["Only the top 50 candidates go to the ranker","The ranker scores it too low","Semantic ranker cannot read English","The `orderBy` clause is empty"],"answer":0,"explain":"Position 74 is outside the top 50 that the ranker receives, so it is never rescored. Fix first-stage recall. The other options do not match the evidence."},{"id":"w05-t05-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each reranker to its defining property.","pairs":[{"l":"Cross-encoder","r":"Scores query and chunk together with attention across both"},{"l":"ColBERT","r":"Independent encodings with token-level interaction, large index"},{"l":"LLM reranker","r":"Prompted model orders a window, extra call per window"},{"l":"Azure semantic ranker","r":"Reranks the top 50 and cannot find new documents"}],"explain":"These are the properties each source describes: cross-encoders attend across query and document, ColBERT delays interaction, LLM rerankers add a model call and Azure's ranker only reorders its 50 candidates."},{"id":"w05-t05-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which is the strongest reason to choose a self-hosted cross-encoder over a hosted rerank API?","options":["It is always more accurate","It needs no GPUs","It removes the need for first-stage recall","Document text must not leave your network"],"answer":3,"explain":"Residency is the decisive dial. Accuracy depends on the model and data, self-hosting needs compute, and no reranker fixes missing candidates."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why can a reranker not fix poor recall?","back":"It only reorders the candidates it receives. Azure's semantic ranker takes the top 50 and cannot find new documents."},{"front":"What is the Azure `@search.rerankerScore` range?","back":"0.00 to 4.00. The distribution shifts with model updates, so use coarse cutoffs only."},{"front":"Cross-encoder versus bi-encoder?","back":"A cross-encoder scores query and chunk together, which is accurate but slow. A bi-encoder embeds them separately, which is fast for first-stage search."}],"related":["w05-t04","w05-t06","w05-l3","w04-t08","w14-t06"],"refs":["tr7","tr11","r3","r2","r11","r12","r13","r26","r31"]},{"id":"w05-t06","order":6,"group":"Ranking","title":"Score thresholds and abstention","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A cutoff decides which retrieved chunks the model may see, and abstention is what the system does when nothing clears the bar. Both depend on scores that are not probabilities.","useIf":"Wrong answers cost more than missing answers, or your corpus cannot answer every question users ask.","avoidIf":"You would rely on a hard-coded score constant that nobody recalibrates, or you have no labelled unanswerable queries to test it on.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Retrieval always returns something. A **cutoff** drops weak chunks, and **abstention** turns an empty or weak shortlist into an honest answer such as 'not found', a clarifying question or a hand-off. The hard part is choosing the signal. Each score has its own scale and its own drift."},{"t":"table","caption":"Signals to cut on","head":["Signal","Scale","Caution"],"rows":[["Azure `@search.rerankerScore`","0.00 to 4.00","The distribution shifts with infrastructure and model updates, so use coarse cutoffs only [[r3]]"],["Vector `threshold` (preview, `2026-08-01-preview`)","For example `{\"kind\":\"vectorSimilarity\",\"value\":0.8}`","Drops weak matches before fusion, even when fewer than `k` remain. Specific to the embedding model [[r35]]"],["OpenAI `ranking_options.score_threshold`","0.0 to 1.0","Depends on the `ranker` choice [[r30]]"],["RRF score","About 1/k per list","Reflects rank agreement and list count, not relevance. Never cut on it [[r1]]"]],"highlightCol":2},{"t":"flow","title":"A confidence-aware gate","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Shortlist","md":"Reranked top chunks with scores."},{"title":"Gate","md":"Count chunks above the cutoff and compare the gap to the top score.","tag":"decide"},{"title":"Strong","md":"Answer with citations."},{"title":"Weak","md":"Broaden: rewrite, more candidates or another source."},{"title":"None","md":"Abstain: say not found, ask a question or hand off."}]},{"t":"p","md":"**Calibration is a measurement, not a guess.** Build a golden set of answerable and unanswerable queries. For each cutoff, record how many answerable queries are still answered (coverage) and how many unanswerable queries are answered anyway (false answers). Pick the point that matches the cost of each error, and recheck after every model or service update. Relative signals such as the gap between the top score and the next, or the number of chunks above the cutoff, move less than absolute scores, though you must still calibrate them (author suggestion). Anthropic's guidance adds the generation side: give the model explicit permission to say it does not know [[r41]]. Corrective RAG goes further by scoring retrieval quality and triggering a different action when it is weak [[r19]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Silent drift","md":"A cutoff that worked at launch can quietly turn into 'always abstain' or 'never abstain' after a model update. Track the abstention rate and score percentiles as dashboards and alerts."}],"whereToUse":["Medical, legal and financial assistants where a wrong answer is worse than no answer","Support bots that must hand off to a person when the knowledge base has no answer","Citation-required systems that refuse to answer without evidence","Pipelines that escalate to a broader retrieval or a web source when the local shortlist is weak"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Unanswerable queries are common","False answers are costly","You can label a golden set that includes no-answer queries","You can monitor abstention rate over time"],"avoid":["Queries are all answerable by construction, such as lookups from a known catalog","You would cut on RRF scores or an uncalibrated constant","A hard refusal frustrates users more than a hedged best-effort answer"],"flips":["Risk rises: tighten the cutoff and prefer abstention","Users complain about too many refusals: add a 'closest matches' fallback instead of a bare refusal","A service model update lands: recalibrate before trusting old thresholds"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Absolute score cutoff","Stable model, labelled set, simple rule","Scores drift across updates","Silent miscalibration"],["Relative cutoff (gap, top n)","Score scale is unstable","A uniformly weak shortlist looks fine relative to itself","Still needs calibration"],["Answerability classifier or LLM judge","Subtle no-answer cases","Tight latency","Extra model call and its own errors"],["Corrective loop (retry or broaden)","The corpus often has partial coverage","Cost and latency limits","More calls, risk of loops"],["No cutoff, rely on the prompt","Low-risk assistants","High-stakes domains","Fabricated answers"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Abstention rate jumps overnight","cause":"The service or embedding model changed and the score distribution shifted","fix":"Alert on abstention rate and p10, p50, p90 scores. Recalibrate on the golden set"},{"symptom":"The bot answers questions the corpus cannot answer","cause":"No cutoff, or a threshold on RRF or a miscalibrated absolute score","fix":"Cut on reranker or per-leg signals, add unanswerable queries to the golden set"},{"symptom":"Users see bare refusals for answerable questions","cause":"Cutoff is too strict or the first stage misses the gold chunk","fix":"Check recall first, then loosen the cutoff and add a broaden step before abstaining"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w05-t06-s1","title":"Clinical guideline assistant","situation":"Clinicians ask about dosing and contraindications. The guideline corpus is incomplete, and a wrong dose is a serious risk.","constraints":["High risk","Corpus does not cover every drug","Latency of a few seconds is fine"],"unknowns":["What false-answer rate is acceptable?","Who handles abstentions?"],"question":"How do you set the cutoff?","options":[{"name":"Calibrate a strict cutoff on a golden set with unanswerable queries, abstain with a clear 'not in the guidelines' message and route to a human","verdict":"best","why":"The risk profile prefers missing answers to wrong ones, and the gate is measurable.","tradeoff":"More abstentions and a human queue."},{"name":"Answer from the top 3 chunks and rely on the prompt to hedge","verdict":"poor","why":"Hedging does not stop a confident wrong dose.","tradeoff":"Acceptable for low-risk FAQ assistants."},{"name":"Add an LLM judge as the only gate","verdict":"good","why":"Catches subtle mismatches.","tradeoff":"Extra latency and its own error rate, so use it beside a score cutoff."}],"flips":[{"if":"Risk drops, such as a wellness FAQ","then":"Loosen the cutoff and show closest matches."},{"if":"The corpus later covers nearly every drug and golden-set coverage is high","then":"Keep the strict cutoff but expect fewer abstentions, and keep routing the remaining misses to a human."}],"takeaway":"The price of a false answer sets the cutoff."},{"id":"w05-t06-s2","title":"Score drift after a service update","situation":"An absolute reranker cutoff of 2.0 worked for months. After a platform update the assistant abstains on queries that were answered before.","constraints":["Cutoff is hard-coded","A golden set exists","No release has changed the app"],"unknowns":["Did the embedding model or index change?","Are abstention dashboards available?"],"question":"What is the best response?","options":[{"name":"Re-run the golden set, recompute coverage and false answers by cutoff, set coarse bands and add drift alerts","verdict":"best","why":"Azure warns the reranker distribution shifts with updates, so calibration must be repeatable [[r3]].","tradeoff":"Ongoing evaluation cost."},{"name":"Lower the cutoff until abstentions stop","verdict":"poor","why":"It trades one miscalibration for another and may admit weak chunks.","tradeoff":"Only a stopgap while the golden set runs."},{"name":"Remove the cutoff","verdict":"good","why":"Stops refusals at once.","tradeoff":"Brings back fabricated answers for unanswerable queries."}],"flips":[{"if":"Drift happens often","then":"Add a relative cutoff or answerability check that moves less with the model."},{"if":"There is no golden set to recalibrate on","then":"Build a small labelled set of answerable and unanswerable queries first; until then, fail safe with a conservative cutoff and human review."}],"takeaway":"Treat cutoffs as monitored configuration, not constants."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-t06-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"At a cutoff, 170 of 200 answerable queries are answered, and 20 of 100 unanswerable queries are answered anyway. What percentage of all answers given are false?","answer":10.5,"tolerancePct":3,"unit":"%","hint":"False answers divided by all answers given.","explain":"Answers given are 170 + 20 = 190. False answers are 20. 20 / 190 = 10.5%. Coverage on answerable queries is 85%, which is a different measure."},{"id":"w05-t06-q2","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"A team cuts on the fused RRF score and abstains on any value below 0.02. Why is this unreliable?","options":["RRF is always below 0.02","RRF reflects rank agreement and list count, not relevance","RRF cannot be sorted","The vector leg has no weight"],"answer":1,"explain":"RRF sums 1/(k+rank) terms, so its range depends on the number of lists and `k`. It orders results but does not measure relevance."},{"id":"w05-t06-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Select the actions that make a cutoff safer over time.","options":["Include unanswerable queries in the golden set","Alert on abstention rate","Hard-code the value once","Recalibrate after model or service updates"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"A cutoff needs evidence of both error types, monitoring and recalibration. A hard-coded constant silently decays when score distributions shift."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why avoid fine-grained cutoffs on the Azure reranker score?","back":"The score distribution shifts with infrastructure and model updates, so use coarse bands, monitor and recalibrate."},{"front":"What does the vector `threshold` parameter do?","back":"It drops low-scoring vector matches before fusion, even if fewer than `k` remain. Preview in `2026-08-01-preview`."},{"front":"How do you calibrate an abstention cutoff?","back":"Measure coverage and false answers by cutoff on a golden set that includes unanswerable queries, then pick by error cost."}],"related":["w05-t05","w05-t14","w05-l3","w06-t03","w14-t06"],"refs":["r1","r3","r19","r30","r35","r41"]},{"id":"w05-t07","order":7,"group":"Query transformation","title":"Query rewriting","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Query rewriting turns what the user typed into what the index can match: cleaned, expanded or made standalone using the conversation. It also adds a model call and the risk of changing the meaning.","useIf":"Queries are short, vague, full of pronouns from earlier turns or use different vocabulary from the documents.","avoidIf":"Queries are identifiers or exact phrases that a rewrite could paraphrase away, or the latency budget is tight.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Three different jobs hide under 'rewriting'. **Cleaning** applies the same normalisation to the query that you applied to chunks before embedding, for example lowercasing [[r8]]. **Standalone rewriting** turns a follow-up such as 'and for contractors?' plus the chat history into a complete question. **Expansion** produces variants with other vocabulary. The Azure Architecture Center lists augmentation, decomposition, rewriting and HyDE as query translation methods [[r8]]."},{"t":"flow","title":"A safe rewrite pipeline","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Collect","md":"Latest question and the last few turns."},{"title":"Rewrite","md":"A small model writes one standalone question.","tag":"LLM call"},{"title":"Guard","md":"Check that identifiers, quoted phrases and numbers survived."},{"title":"Search both","md":"Run the original and the rewrite in parallel."},{"title":"Fuse","md":"Merge the lists with RRF, then rerank."}]},{"t":"p","md":"**Platform options.** Azure AI Search has a preview generative rewrite, `queryRewrites: \"generative|count-N\"` with N from 1 to 10. It needs `queryLanguage` set to a supported locale (71 are listed), runs rewrites as extra queries scored by BM25 or RRF, then rescored by the semantic ranker. `debug: queryRewrites` is for testing only [[r28]]. OpenAI's Retrieval API offers `rewrite_query=true` [[r30]]. Agentic retrieval takes chat history and plans subqueries itself [[r4]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"A rewrite can delete the token that mattered","md":"A model that 'improves' `ERR-4012 after upgrade` into 'problems after software update' has thrown away the one exact match. Keep the original query in the mix, preserve quoted strings and identifiers, and compare recall against a no-rewrite baseline before shipping."},{"t":"p","md":"**Cost.** A rewrite is a serial model call before retrieval, so it adds its full latency to every query unless you route only the queries that need it, such as those with pronouns or very few words (author suggestion). Measure the lift on a labelled set, because a rewrite that helps follow-ups can hurt identifier queries."}],"whereToUse":["Multi-turn chat assistants where follow-up questions depend on earlier turns","Search boxes where users type two or three words","Corpora written in formal vocabulary while users use colloquial terms","Pipelines that add a query language or locale before a service-side rewrite"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Follow-ups need context to be searchable","Queries are very short or vague","A labelled set shows recall lift over no rewrite","Latency allows one extra small-model call"],"avoid":["Identifier-heavy queries unless the identifier is guarded","Strict latency budgets without a routing rule","A rewrite model that sees untrusted documents or has no guard against injected instructions"],"flips":["Recall@10 drops on identifier queries: keep the original query and fuse","Only follow-ups benefit: rewrite only when the history is non-empty","Questions are multi-part: use decomposition rather than one rewrite"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["No rewrite, clean only","Clear, self-contained queries","Follow-ups and vague queries","Cheapest"],["Standalone rewrite with history","Chat with pronouns and ellipsis","Single-turn search","A serial LLM call, drift risk"],["Service-side generative rewrite (Azure, preview)","You want variants scored inside the service","You need GA-only features","Preview, needs `queryLanguage`, extra queries [[r28]]"],["Multi-query expansion","Recall is low on ambiguous queries","Precision is the problem","N searches and dedupe"],["Agentic planning","Complex, context-dependent questions","Simple lookups, tight latency","Extra model calls, mostly preview [[r4]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Recall drops on error-code queries after enabling rewrite","cause":"The model paraphrased away the identifier","fix":"Search the original and the rewrite together, and guard identifiers and quoted text"},{"symptom":"Follow-up answers are about the wrong topic","cause":"The rewrite used too much or too little history, or the wrong turn","fix":"Pass a bounded window of turns and log the rewritten query for review"},{"symptom":"Latency rises on every query","cause":"A serial LLM rewrite runs for single-turn queries that did not need it","fix":"Route: rewrite only when there is history or the query is very short"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w05-t07-s1","title":"Chat assistant with follow-ups","situation":"Users ask 'What is the notice period?' then 'And for contractors?'. The second query returns generic contractor pages.","constraints":["Multi-turn chat","Latency budget of two seconds","No identifiers in these questions"],"unknowns":["How many turns of history matter?","Is a small fast model available?"],"question":"What do you add?","options":[{"name":"A standalone-question rewrite from a bounded window of recent turns, logged for review","verdict":"best","why":"It supplies the missing context, and the latency fits the budget.","tradeoff":"One extra model call, and a rewrite can drift, so review a sample."},{"name":"Embed the last user message only","verdict":"poor","why":"'And for contractors?' contains no topic to retrieve.","tradeoff":"Fine for single-turn search."},{"name":"Concatenate the whole chat history into the query","verdict":"good","why":"Keeps context without a model call.","tradeoff":"Dilutes the query with unrelated terms, hurting precision."}],"flips":[{"if":"Latency is very tight","then":"Use a small model or rule-based carry-over of the last entity."},{"if":"Follow-ups depend on several earlier turns and answers","then":"Use a larger rewrite window with a stronger model, and log rewrites so drift is visible."}],"takeaway":"Rewrite to repair missing context."},{"id":"w05-t07-s2","title":"Support search with error codes","situation":"Engineers type short queries like `ERR-4012 upgrade`. A proposed LLM rewrite will 'make queries more descriptive'.","constraints":["Identifier-heavy traffic","Tight latency","BM25 plus vector hybrid in place"],"unknowns":["What share of queries have history?","Can you A/B against no rewrite?"],"question":"Do you ship the rewrite?","options":[{"name":"Skip rewrite for single-turn identifier queries, or run original and rewrite in parallel with a guard","verdict":"best","why":"The exact token is the value, and fusing original and rewrite keeps it.","tradeoff":"Extra retrieval cost when both run."},{"name":"Replace the query with the rewrite everywhere","verdict":"poor","why":"It risks deleting the identifier and adds latency.","tradeoff":"Right for conversational traffic with no codes."},{"name":"Use the Azure generative rewrite at count-5","verdict":"good","why":"Variants are scored beside the original query inside the service.","tradeoff":"Preview, extra queries and a locale requirement [[r28]]."}],"flips":[{"if":"Most traffic becomes natural-language symptoms","then":"Enable rewriting broadly and keep the original query in the fusion."},{"if":"Rewrites are shown to raise latency past the budget with no recall gain","then":"Drop the rewrite and rely on hybrid plus the reranker."}],"takeaway":"Keep the original query in the mix."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-t07-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"After adding an LLM rewrite, recall@10 rises on conversational queries and falls on error-code queries. What is the most likely fix?","options":["Raise the chunk size","Disable the vector leg","Increase the rewrite temperature","Run the original query beside the rewrite and guard identifiers"],"answer":3,"explain":"The rewrite is paraphrasing identifiers away. Keeping the original in the fusion and guarding identifiers restores exact matches. The other options do not address the loss, and a higher temperature adds drift."},{"id":"w05-t07-q2","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Select the jobs that count as query rewriting.","options":["Lowercasing the query as the chunks were lowercased","Turning a follow-up into a standalone question","Generating alternative phrasings","Shrinking chunk overlap"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Cleaning, standalone rewriting and expansion are all rewriting. Chunk overlap is an ingestion setting."},{"id":"w05-t07-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which Azure parameter must be set for the preview generative query rewrite to work?","options":["`queryLanguage` set to a supported locale","`maxTextRecallSize` above 5,000","`orderBy` on the score","`k` above 50"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Azure rewrite page requires `queryLanguage` set to a supported locale. The other parameters are not prerequisites [[r28]]."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What are the three jobs called query rewriting?","back":"Cleaning to match chunk normalisation, standalone rewriting from chat history, and expansion into variants."},{"front":"What is the main risk of an LLM query rewrite?","back":"Drift: it can paraphrase away identifiers or change meaning. Keep the original query in the fusion."},{"front":"What does Azure's `queryRewrites` accept?","back":"`generative|count-N` with N from 1 to 10, plus a supported `queryLanguage`. Preview."}],"related":["w05-t08","w05-t09","w05-t10","w05-l4","w14-t06"],"refs":["r4","r8","r28","r30"]},{"id":"w05-t08","order":8,"group":"Query transformation","title":"HyDE (hypothetical document embeddings)","minutes":7,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"HyDE asks a model to write a hypothetical answer, embeds that answer instead of the question, and retrieves the real documents nearest to it.","useIf":"Queries are short or phrased unlike the documents, you have no relevance labels to fine-tune with, and the model knows the domain.","avoidIf":"The domain is proprietary or full of identifiers the model would invent, or the latency budget cannot absorb a generation before retrieval.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A short question and a long answer passage often land far apart in embedding space. **HyDE** closes the gap. An instruction-following model writes a **hypothetical document** that answers the query. This text may contain false details. A contrastive encoder embeds it, and the nearest real documents are retrieved. The encoder acts as a filter, because its compressed representation drops fabricated specifics and keeps the topical shape. The method works zero-shot, without relevance labels [[r14]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w05-t08-d1","title":"HyDE request path","alt":"The user query goes to a model that writes a hypothetical answer, which is embedded and used to search the index, returning real documents.","src":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant U as User\n  participant L as LLM\n  participant E as Encoder\n  participant I as Vector index\n  U->>L: Question\n  L->>E: Hypothetical answer\n  E->>I: Answer vector\n  I-->>U: Real documents"},{"t":"p","md":"**When it helps.** Short or vague queries over a domain the model understands, where documents are explanatory prose and the retriever has not been fine-tuned. **When it misleads.** If the model lacks domain knowledge, the hypothetical answer describes the wrong thing and retrieval confidently follows it. For identifiers, SKUs or internal names the model will invent plausible but wrong strings, and a dense encoder will chase them. The Azure Architecture Center names HyDE among query translation methods, next to rewriting and decomposition [[r8]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Cost.** It adds a serial generation, of a paragraph, before any retrieval. In practice teams limit the hypothetical text to a short length (author suggestion) and keep the original query on the keyword leg, so a hallucinated hypothetical only affects the vector leg. Evaluate against a baseline of the plain query embedding and a baseline of the plain query plus a rewrite."},{"t":"callout","kind":"why","title":"Why the fake answer is useful","md":"The goal is not a correct answer. It is a vector that sits where relevant documents sit. Answer-shaped text is closer to answer passages than a question is, even when its facts are wrong [[r14]]."}],"whereToUse":["Zero-shot retrieval over a new corpus with no labelled queries","Scientific or public-domain explanatory content that the model already knows","Short, underspecified questions where the embedding of the question alone is poor","Experiments to estimate headroom before investing in a fine-tuned retriever"],"whenToUse":{"use":["No relevance labels exist for fine-tuning","Queries are short and documents are long explanatory prose","The model knows the subject area","Latency allows an extra generation before search"],"avoid":["Exact identifiers, SKUs or internal terms dominate","The model has never seen the domain","Retrieval latency is tight","You have not compared it to a plain-query baseline"],"flips":["The corpus is proprietary: ground the hypothetical text with a first-pass retrieval or skip HyDE","Identifier queries appear: run the original query on a BM25 leg and fuse","Labelled data arrives: fine-tune or adapt the retriever instead of generating on every query"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["HyDE","Short queries, zero-shot, domain the model knows","Proprietary jargon, identifiers","Serial generation, hallucinated seeds"],["Plain query embedding","Queries already look like the documents","Query and passage styles differ","Cheapest"],["Query rewrite or expansion","Vocabulary mismatch","The model cannot add useful terms","Drift risk, serial call"],["Multi-query fusion","Ambiguous queries","One strong query would do","N retrievals"],["Fine-tuned embeddings","Labelled data and stable domain","No data","Training and re-embedding"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Retrieval follows an invented product name","cause":"The hypothetical document contained a plausible but wrong entity and the encoder matched it","fix":"Keep the original query on a keyword leg, or skip HyDE for identifier-shaped queries"},{"symptom":"Results are on the wrong topic for niche questions","cause":"The model misunderstood the domain, so the hypothetical answer points elsewhere","fix":"Add retrieved context to the hypothesis prompt, or use a rewrite instead"},{"symptom":"Latency doubled for little gain","cause":"Every query pays for a generation, including those the plain embedding handles","fix":"Route only short or low-confidence queries through HyDE"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w05-t08-s1","title":"Zero-shot search over a public science corpus","situation":"A team indexes open-access review articles. Users ask short questions such as 'why do vaccines need boosters'. There are no labelled queries and no budget to fine-tune.","constraints":["No labels","General-domain content the model knows","A few seconds of latency is fine"],"unknowns":["How long are the articles?","Can you test on a small labelled sample?"],"question":"How do you improve retrieval?","options":[{"name":"Try HyDE for the vector leg, keep the original on BM25 and compare recall on a small hand-labelled sample","verdict":"best","why":"Short queries against explanatory prose are HyDE's target, the model knows the domain and the keyword leg guards against bad hypotheticals.","tradeoff":"An extra generation per query and a sample you must label."},{"name":"Fine-tune an embedding model","verdict":"poor","why":"There are no labelled pairs and the budget is small.","tradeoff":"Right once you have thousands of labelled pairs in a stable domain."},{"name":"Ship plain query embeddings","verdict":"good","why":"Zero added cost and a fair baseline.","tradeoff":"Short questions embed poorly against long passages."}],"flips":[{"if":"HyDE shows no recall lift on the sample","then":"Keep the plain embedding and drop the generation cost."},{"if":"The domain is niche and the model often writes confident but wrong hypothetical answers","then":"Skip HyDE: a wrong draft pulls retrieval toward wrong passages, so use plain embeddings or a fine-tuned embedder."}],"takeaway":"HyDE shines on short queries in domains the model knows."},{"id":"w05-t08-s2","title":"Internal engineering knowledge base","situation":"Queries mention internal service names and ticket ids. The model has never seen these services.","constraints":["Proprietary vocabulary","Identifier-heavy traffic","Latency under a second for retrieval"],"unknowns":["Is a labelled set available?","How often do queries lack identifiers?"],"question":"Is HyDE a good fit?","options":[{"name":"No. Use hybrid retrieval and rewrite only conversational follow-ups","verdict":"best","why":"The model would invent plausible service behaviour, and exact names belong on a keyword leg.","tradeoff":"Pure paraphrase questions get less help."},{"name":"HyDE on every query","verdict":"poor","why":"It adds latency and hallucinated seeds around unknown names.","tradeoff":"Fits public-domain, explanatory content."},{"name":"HyDE seeded with retrieved snippets","verdict":"good","why":"Grounding reduces invention.","tradeoff":"It needs a first retrieval, so latency and complexity rise."}],"flips":[{"if":"Queries become long natural-language descriptions with few names","then":"Test HyDE again on that slice."},{"if":"The retrieval latency budget loosens to several seconds","then":"A generation step becomes affordable, but still gate HyDE on measured recall for the vector leg only."}],"takeaway":"Unknown vocabulary breaks the hypothetical document."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-t08-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"In HyDE, what is embedded and used to search the index?","options":["The original question","The top retrieved document","A model-written hypothetical answer","The chat history"],"answer":2,"explain":"HyDE embeds a generated hypothetical document. The encoder discards fabricated details, and the nearest real documents are returned [[r14]]. The other options are different techniques."},{"id":"w05-t08-q2","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Which change most clearly argues against HyDE?","options":["Queries get shorter","The corpus is in English","You have no labels","The corpus uses internal service names the model has never seen"],"answer":3,"explain":"Unknown vocabulary leads the model to invent specifics, and the encoder chases them. No labels and short queries actually favour HyDE."},{"id":"w05-t08-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Select the costs or risks of HyDE.","options":["A serial generation before retrieval","Hypothetical text may contain false details","It requires relevance labels","It can mislead if the model lacks domain knowledge"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"HyDE adds generation latency, may invent details and misleads when the model lacks the domain. It is zero-shot, so it needs no labels [[r14]]."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does HyDE embed?","back":"A hypothetical answer written by a model, not the question. Nearest real documents are then retrieved."},{"front":"Why can a false hypothetical document still retrieve well?","back":"The encoder compresses the text and drops fabricated specifics, keeping a vector near relevant passages."},{"front":"Name a safe way to use HyDE next to identifiers.","back":"Use the hypothetical text only on the vector leg and keep the original query on the BM25 leg."}],"related":["w05-t07","w05-t09","w05-l4","w04-t12"],"refs":["r14","r8"]},{"id":"w05-t09","order":9,"group":"Query transformation","title":"Multi-query and step-back","minutes":7,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Multi-query fans one question out into several variants, retrieves for each and fuses the results. Step-back adds a more general question so the retriever also finds background and principles.","useIf":"Recall is low because a single phrasing misses relevant chunks, or the answer depends on a general principle the specific question does not name.","avoidIf":"Precision is the problem, the question is multi-part (use decomposition), or each extra search is too costly.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Multi-query.** A model writes N alternative phrasings or angles of the question. Each runs as its own search, and the ranked lists are merged, commonly with reciprocal rank fusion. RAG-Fusion packages this idea as multiple generated queries plus RRF. Its author found answers were more accurate and comprehensive, but some drifted off topic when generated queries were not relevant enough to the original [[tr4]]. Azure's preview `queryRewrites: \"generative|count-N\"` does the fan-out inside the service, with N from 1 to 10 [[r28]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Step-back.** Step-back prompting asks the model to derive a higher-level concept or principle before reasoning. The paper reports gains on reasoning tasks: PaLM-2L improved about 7% on MMLU Physics and 11% on Chemistry, 27% on TimeQA and 7% on MuSiQue [[r15]]. Those numbers are for reasoning benchmarks, not retrieval. In RAG the practical use is to retrieve for both the specific question and its abstraction, so the model receives the details and the governing rule."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w05-t09-d1","title":"Fan-out, fuse and dedupe","alt":"A question is expanded into variants and a step-back question, each searched in parallel, then merged with fusion and duplicates removed before reranking.","src":"flowchart LR\n  Q[Question] --> M[LLM expansion]\n  M --> V1[Variant 1]\n  M --> V2[Variant 2]\n  M --> SB[Step-back question]\n  Q --> O[Original query]\n  V1 --> F[Fuse and dedupe]\n  V2 --> F\n  SB --> F\n  O --> F\n  F --> R[Rerank]"},{"t":"p","md":"**Costs and controls.** N variants mean N searches (in Azure each extra query counts as another execution [[r1]]), one generation call before them, and many duplicate chunks. Dedupe by chunk id before reranking, keep the original query as one of the lists, cap N (3 to 5 is a common starting point, author suggestion) and let a reranker restore precision after the fusion."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Variants widen recall and also noise","md":"Every off-topic variant pulls off-topic chunks into the fusion. Measure precision at the cutoff, not only recall, and drop variants whose lists share nothing with the original."}],"whereToUse":["Short or ambiguous questions where one phrasing misses documents","Corpora with varied vocabulary, such as support articles from several authors","Science and engineering questions where a general principle explains the answer","Pipelines already using RRF, where extra lists fit naturally"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Recall at the shortlist is the weak point","Questions are ambiguous or vocabulary varies","A reranker follows the fusion","You can afford N parallel searches"],"avoid":["The failure is a multi-part question, where sub-questions beat paraphrases","Latency or search cost is strictly capped","Identifiers dominate and variants drift away from them"],"flips":["Questions have independent sub-parts: switch to decomposition","Variants add noise: reduce N or require overlap with the original list","A background rule matters: add a step-back question beside the specific one"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Multi-query plus RRF","Ambiguous query, vocabulary mismatch","Precision-bound or multi-part questions","N searches, dedupe, noise [[tr4]]"],["Step-back question","A general principle supports the answer","Pure lookups","Extra retrieval and generation"],["Single rewrite","One clear improvement exists","Multiple plausible readings","Drift"],["Decomposition","Distinct sub-questions","One ambiguous phrase","Extra reasoning and serial hops"],["Bigger k, no transform","Cheap recall gain","Context budget is small","More tokens to the reranker or model"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Answers wander into related but unasked topics","cause":"Generated variants strayed from the original intent and fused noisy chunks [[tr4]]","fix":"Keep the original list, cap N, and require agreement or rerank before the cutoff"},{"symptom":"The same chunk appears many times in the context","cause":"Variants retrieved overlapping results and no dedupe ran","fix":"Deduplicate by chunk id after fusion and before reranking"},{"symptom":"Costs and latency grew with no recall gain","cause":"Variants were near-paraphrases of the same words","fix":"Ask for diverse angles and measure list overlap. Reduce N when overlap is high"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w05-t09-s1","title":"Ambiguous short questions, recall is low","situation":"Users type three-word questions such as 'reset access token'. The corpus uses several terms for the same thing. Offline tests show the gold chunk appears at rank 12 on average.","constraints":["Short ambiguous queries","A reranker is in place","A few extra searches are affordable"],"unknowns":["How many labelled queries exist?","What is the latency budget?"],"question":"What do you try?","options":[{"name":"Multi-query with N of 3 plus the original, fuse with RRF, dedupe and rerank","verdict":"best","why":"Variants cover vocabulary variation, the original anchors intent and the reranker restores precision.","tradeoff":"Three extra searches and one generation per query."},{"name":"Increase chunk overlap","verdict":"poor","why":"It targets ingestion boundaries, not vocabulary mismatch.","tradeoff":"Useful when answers straddle chunk boundaries."},{"name":"Replace the query with one rewrite","verdict":"good","why":"Cheaper than fan-out.","tradeoff":"One guess at the right wording and a drift risk."}],"flips":[{"if":"Precision at the cutoff drops after fan-out","then":"Reduce N and require overlap with the original list."},{"if":"No reranker exists and each extra search costs real money","then":"Use a single rewrite or the original query alone, since fan-out without a reranker mostly adds noise."}],"takeaway":"Fan out for recall, rerank for precision."},{"id":"w05-t09-s2","title":"Physics tutor with specific problems","situation":"Students ask specific problems, for example about the pressure of a gas after heating. The answer needs the general law, and the textbook states laws in separate sections.","constraints":["Specific questions, general explanations","Latency of a few seconds","Reasoning-style answers"],"unknowns":["Does the corpus label sections by principle?","Can the model name the principle reliably?"],"question":"Which transformation fits?","options":[{"name":"Step-back: retrieve for the specific question and for the principle behind it, merge, then answer","verdict":"best","why":"Step-back showed its gains on physics and chemistry reasoning [[r15]], and the textbook stores principles separately from examples.","tradeoff":"An extra generation and retrieval. The paper's numbers are for reasoning, not for retrieval metrics."},{"name":"Multi-query paraphrases","verdict":"good","why":"Improves recall for the specific wording.","tradeoff":"Does not add the general law."},{"name":"HyDE with a hypothetical solution","verdict":"poor","why":"A wrong worked solution can pull in wrong examples.","tradeoff":"Fits short factual queries where an answer-shaped passage helps."}],"flips":[{"if":"The question is a simple definition","then":"Skip step-back and use a plain query."},{"if":"Questions are short and ambiguous rather than specific","then":"Prefer multi-query paraphrases, since there is no specific detail to abstract away from."}],"takeaway":"Step-back retrieves the rule behind the example."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-t09-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"After adding multi-query, the context contains the same chunk four times and token cost rose. What step is missing?","options":["Deduplication by chunk id after fusion","A larger embedding model","A higher temperature","A shorter `k`"],"answer":0,"explain":"Variants retrieve overlapping chunks. Dedupe after fusion before reranking. The other options do not remove duplicates."},{"id":"w05-t09-q2","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which question shape is better served by decomposition than by multi-query?","options":["A three-word ambiguous query","A query with a typo","A question that compares two regions across two time periods","A definition request"],"answer":2,"explain":"Compound comparisons have distinct sub-questions that must each be answered. Paraphrases do not cover them. The other shapes suit rewriting or multi-query."},{"id":"w05-t09-q3","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Select the statements consistent with the sources.","options":["RAG-Fusion generates multiple queries and fuses with RRF","Step-back reported gains on reasoning tasks, not retrieval metrics","Azure `queryRewrites` accepts N from 1 to 10 (preview)","Multi-query never adds noise"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"RAG-Fusion, the step-back paper and the Azure page support the first three. The RAG-Fusion study itself noted off-topic drift when generated queries strayed [[tr4]]."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is RAG-Fusion?","back":"Generate multiple queries, retrieve for each and merge the lists with reciprocal rank fusion."},{"front":"What is step-back prompting?","back":"Ask for a higher-level concept or principle first, then reason. In RAG, retrieve for both the specific and the abstract question."},{"front":"Name two controls that keep multi-query cheap and clean.","back":"Cap N and dedupe by chunk id. Keep the original query as one list and rerank after fusion."}],"related":["w05-t07","w05-t10","w05-t04","w05-l4"],"refs":["tr4","r15","r28","r1"]},{"id":"w05-t10","order":10,"group":"Query transformation","title":"Decomposition and multi-hop retrieval","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Decomposition splits a compound question into sub-questions. Multi-hop retrieval chains searches, where each hop depends on what the last one found. Both need stop conditions.","useIf":"A question has several parts, or its answer needs a fact that you can only look up after finding another fact.","avoidIf":"Questions are single-fact lookups, or you cannot bound hops, tokens and time.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"One embedding of one question cannot cover 'compare notice periods for contractors and employees in the UK and Germany'. **Decomposition** asks a model to split it into focused sub-questions. If they are independent, run them **in parallel** and merge the evidence. If they depend on each other ('Who manages the owner of service X?'), you need **multi-hop**: retrieve, read, form the next query, retrieve again. IRCoT interleaves retrieval with chain-of-thought sentences, so what to retrieve depends on what has been derived. With GPT-3 it improved retrieval by up to 21 points and downstream QA by up to 15 points on four multi-step datasets [[tr3]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w05-t10-d1","title":"Iterative retrieval with stop conditions","alt":"A question is planned into sub-queries, retrieved, checked for sufficiency, and either answered or sent around again until a limit is reached.","src":"stateDiagram-v2\n  [*] --> Plan\n  Plan --> Retrieve\n  Retrieve --> Check\n  Check --> Answer: enough evidence\n  Check --> Plan: gap found and budget left\n  Check --> Abstain: budget spent\n  Answer --> [*]\n  Abstain --> [*]"},{"t":"p","md":"**Platform version.** Azure AI Search agentic retrieval plans focused subqueries from the query and chat history, runs all subqueries in parallel and semantically reranks each. At `medium` effort it adds a semantic classifier and one retry with a revised plan [[r5]]. That is a bounded loop. `maxRuntimeInSeconds` has a minimum of 10, a default of 90 and a maximum of 600 (the 600 applies to the Search retrieve request only) [[r38]]. Most of this is preview, as of 2026-10-13 [[r4]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Stop conditions are the design.** Combine: a maximum number of hops, a token or time budget, a sufficiency check ('does the evidence answer the question?'), and a no-progress rule (the new query retrieves nothing new). Without them a loop wastes money on unanswerable questions."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Errors compound across hops","md":"A wrong first-hop answer poisons every later query. Log each sub-question, its evidence and the decision. Prefer parallel decomposition when sub-questions are independent, since serial hops add their latencies."}],"whereToUse":["Comparison questions across entities, regions or time periods","Chain questions such as 'which team owns the service that failed?'","Research assistants that must gather evidence from several documents before answering","Agentic retrieval layers that plan subqueries over multiple knowledge sources"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Questions have separable parts","Later queries depend on earlier findings","You can bound loops by hops, time and tokens","Single-shot recall is demonstrably low on the multi-part slice"],"avoid":["Most questions are simple lookups","You cannot afford several model and search calls per question","There is no sufficiency check or stop rule"],"flips":["Sub-questions are independent: run them in parallel rather than serially","Only a slice of traffic is multi-part: route that slice and keep single-shot for the rest","Latency is capped: limit to one plan pass, as the `low` effort does with one planning pass [[r5]]"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Parallel decomposition","Independent sub-questions","Dependent chains","Several searches, merge logic"],["Iterative multi-hop (IRCoT style)","Each step depends on the last","Tight latency","Serial latency, error propagation [[tr3]]"],["Agentic retrieval service","You want managed planning and activity logs","GA-only rules","Preview, planning model billing [[r4]]"],["Single query with large k","Question is simple","Compound questions","Cheap, misses parts"],["Graph or tree index","Whole-corpus or entity-link questions","Fast-changing corpora","Heavy indexing"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A loop keeps searching on an unanswerable question","cause":"No hop, token or no-progress limit","fix":"Set max hops and a time budget, and abstain when budget is spent"},{"symptom":"Final answer ignores one part of the question","cause":"The decomposition dropped a sub-question or the merge lost its evidence","fix":"Log sub-questions and check that every one has evidence before answering"},{"symptom":"Latency is several times higher than single-shot","cause":"Independent sub-questions were run in series","fix":"Run independent sub-questions in parallel and reserve serial hops for dependent ones"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w05-t10-s1","title":"Compare two jurisdictions","situation":"An HR assistant gets questions such as 'compare notice periods for contractors and employees in the UK and in Germany'. Single-shot retrieval returns UK employee pages only.","constraints":["Four independent facts per question","Latency budget of four seconds","Stable policy corpus"],"unknowns":["What share of traffic is compound?","Is a planner model available?"],"question":"How do you retrieve?","options":[{"name":"Decompose into four sub-questions, retrieve in parallel, merge and answer with a check that all four have evidence","verdict":"best","why":"The parts are independent, so parallel retrieval keeps latency near one search plus planning.","tradeoff":"A planning call and four searches per compound question."},{"name":"Raise k to 100 on the original query","verdict":"poor","why":"One embedding favours the dominant sub-topic, and more chunks flood the context.","tradeoff":"Fine for a single-topic question with scattered answers."},{"name":"Serial multi-hop, one sub-question at a time","verdict":"good","why":"Works but is slower.","tradeoff":"Latency adds up for no benefit when parts are independent."}],"flips":[{"if":"One answer determines the next query","then":"Use iterative hops for that chain."},{"if":"Most questions have one or two facts and latency is tight","then":"Skip decomposition and use one hybrid query, reserving decomposition for detected multi-part questions."}],"takeaway":"Independent parts run in parallel."},{"id":"w05-t10-s2","title":"Ownership chain across tickets","situation":"Engineers ask 'Which on-call group owns the dependency that caused ticket 5512?'. The ticket names a service, the service page names a dependency and the dependency page names a group.","constraints":["Three dependent hops","Cost matters","Unanswerable queries exist"],"unknowns":["How deep can chains go?","What should happen when the chain breaks?"],"question":"What stop design do you use?","options":[{"name":"Iterative retrieval with a cap of three or four hops, a sufficiency check and abstention on budget exhaustion","verdict":"best","why":"Each hop depends on the last, so serial steps are required, and the caps bound cost.","tradeoff":"Serial latency and error propagation."},{"name":"Unlimited loop until the model says it is sure","verdict":"poor","why":"Unanswerable queries loop forever and spend tokens.","tradeoff":"Only acceptable with a strict external budget."},{"name":"One-shot retrieval with the ticket text","verdict":"good","why":"Cheapest and fast.","tradeoff":"Rarely finds the group page, since the answer needs the chain."}],"flips":[{"if":"The ownership data lives in a structured table","then":"Use a tool or lookup for the chain instead of text retrieval."},{"if":"Cost no longer matters and recall on deep chains is critical","then":"Raise the hop cap modestly, but keep the sufficiency check and a hard ceiling."}],"takeaway":"Every loop needs a ceiling."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-t10-q1","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which question suits parallel decomposition?","options":["Who manages the owner of service X?","Define latency","Compare refund rules in three countries","What did the last answer say?"],"answer":2,"explain":"The three countries are independent sub-questions. The first is a dependent chain, the third a lookup and the fourth a follow-up."},{"id":"w05-t10-q2","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Select good stop conditions for an iterative retrieval loop.","options":["Maximum hops","Token or time budget","A sufficiency check on the evidence","Keep going until the model is confident"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Hop, budget and sufficiency limits bound the loop. Model confidence alone can run forever on unanswerable questions."},{"id":"w05-t10-q3","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What does Azure's `medium` reasoning effort add compared with `low`?","options":["A semantic classifier and one retry with a revised plan","Unlimited loops","Removal of reranking","A non-LLM planner"],"answer":0,"explain":"At `medium` the service adds a semantic classifier and one retry with a revised plan. It is a bounded retry, not an unlimited loop, and reranking stays [[r5]]."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Decomposition versus multi-hop?","back":"Decomposition splits independent sub-questions that can run in parallel. Multi-hop chains searches where each depends on the last."},{"front":"What does IRCoT do?","back":"It interleaves retrieval with chain-of-thought steps so each retrieval depends on what has been derived so far."},{"front":"List four stop conditions for a retrieval loop.","back":"Maximum hops, token or time budget, sufficiency check, and no-new-evidence rule."}],"related":["w05-t09","w05-t13","w05-t14","w05-l5","w10-t01"],"refs":["tr3","r4","r5","r38"]},{"id":"w05-t11","order":11,"group":"Query transformation","title":"Metadata filters and self-query","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Self-query lets a model turn a natural-language question into a semantic query plus a structured metadata filter. The filter is model output, so it must be validated and must never replace authorization.","useIf":"Questions carry implicit constraints such as year, product, region or document type, and your chunks carry those fields as metadata.","avoidIf":"A filter would decide who may see data, or the metadata is sparse, inconsistent or unknown to the model.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A question like 'security policies for contractors since 2024' mixes meaning ('security policies') with constraints (audience, date). Embeddings blur constraints. **Self-query** asks a model to emit structured output: the text to search, plus a filter over declared fields such as `audience`, `year` and `doctype`. The search engine applies the filter exactly. OpenAI's retrieval exposes attribute filters on file metadata [[r30]]. Azure agentic retrieval stores `searchIndexParameters.queryHints` on a search index knowledge source: filter hints and boosts that guide the query-planning model [[r29]]."},{"t":"flow","title":"Validated self-query","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Extract","md":"The model returns JSON that matches a declared schema.","tag":"LLM call"},{"title":"Validate","md":"Allowlist fields, operators, types and enum values. Reject the rest."},{"title":"Resolve","md":"Application code turns 'last quarter' into dates."},{"title":"Combine","md":"AND the filter with the authorization filter built from the caller's identity."},{"title":"Search","md":"If results are empty, relax the weakest constraint and retry."}]},{"t":"p","md":"**Safety rules.** First, the model's filter is **best effort**. Azure's docs say to use document-level access control or a deterministic filter for authorization boundaries, and generated filters combine with `baseFilter` and `filterAddOn` using AND [[r29]]. Put the tenant, group or ACL condition in the deterministic part. Second, treat the generated filter as untrusted input: a prompt-injected user message or retrieved text can steer it, so parse structured output, check fields and values against an allowlist and never concatenate model text into an OData or SQL string. Third, a filter that is too narrow returns zero results. That is a silent recall collapse, so fall back by relaxing it."},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Azure query hints, status on 2026-10-13","md":"Query hints are preview (`2026-08-01-preview`). They are not applied at `minimal` effort. At other efforts a GPT-4o or GPT-4.1 family planner returns HTTP 400 if stored hints contain a filter, and the page lists the GPT-5 family as planner models [[r29]] [[r39]]."}],"whereToUse":["Catalog and document search with clear facets such as brand, year, language and document type","Policy assistants where questions name an audience or a version","Multi-source knowledge bases where the planner should pick sources and constraints","Support search where product and release fields narrow candidates before ranking"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Questions contain explicit facets that exist as indexed fields","Field values come from a controlled vocabulary the model can see","You validate every generated filter","A deterministic authorization filter is applied separately"],"avoid":["The filter would enforce permissions or tenancy","Metadata is missing for many chunks, so strict filters hide good answers","You pass model text into a query string without parsing"],"flips":["Constraints are security-related: build them from identity in code, not from the question","Many empty-result queries: relax or drop the weakest constraint and retry","Date phrases are fuzzy: resolve them in application code with a known calendar"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Self-query with validation","Natural-language facets over indexed fields","Sparse or messy metadata","A model call, filter errors cause silent misses"],["UI facets chosen by the user","A search page with filter controls","Chat interfaces","Users must know the facets"],["Deterministic filter from identity","Authorization, tenancy, ACLs","Soft preferences from the question","Needs identity plumbing"],["Boosting instead of filtering","Constraints are preferences","Constraints must hold","Unwanted items can still appear"],["No filter, rely on embeddings","Few facets, small corpus","Exact constraints such as year","Mixed-version answers"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Zero results for a query that has an answer","cause":"The model produced a filter that is too narrow or used a value that does not exist","fix":"Validate values against the enum list and relax the weakest constraint on empty results"},{"symptom":"A user reads another tenant's documents","cause":"The tenant filter was model generated or trusted from the question","fix":"Build authorization filters in code from the caller identity and AND them with generated filters [[r29]]"},{"symptom":"Request fails with an invalid filter error","cause":"The model emitted a field or operator that the index does not define","fix":"Constrain output with a schema, reject unknown fields and log rejected filters"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w05-t11-s1","title":"Product documentation by version","situation":"Questions often name a product version ('in release 5.2, how do I rotate keys'). Chunks carry `product`, `version` and `doctype` fields, and all docs are public.","constraints":["Public content, no permission issues","Three well-defined facets","Moderate traffic"],"unknowns":["How complete is the metadata?","Do users spell versions consistently?"],"question":"How do you use the version?","options":[{"name":"Self-query into a schema with enum-checked fields, validate, and relax on empty results","verdict":"best","why":"Facets are indexed and exact, so a filter beats embeddings, and validation limits damage from model errors.","tradeoff":"A model call and fallback logic."},{"name":"Leave the version to the embedding","verdict":"poor","why":"Embeddings blur version numbers, and answers mix releases.","tradeoff":"Acceptable when versions rarely matter."},{"name":"Free-form model-written OData filter strings","verdict":"good","why":"Flexible.","tradeoff":"Invalid syntax and injection risk, so a schema is safer."}],"flips":[{"if":"Many chunks lack version metadata","then":"Boost instead of filtering, or fix ingestion first."},{"if":"Users rarely mention a version and usually want the latest","then":"Apply a default latest-version filter in code and let the query override it explicitly."}],"takeaway":"Exact facets deserve exact filters."},{"id":"w05-t11-s2","title":"Multi-tenant assistant","situation":"A SaaS assistant serves many customers from one index. Users type 'show our contracts' and sometimes 'show Acme's contracts'.","constraints":["Per-tenant isolation is mandatory","Adversarial users are possible","Shared index"],"unknowns":["How is tenant identity established?","Can chunks carry a tenant field?"],"question":"Where does the tenant constraint come from?","options":[{"name":"Build the tenant filter in code from the authenticated identity and AND it with any validated self-query filter","verdict":"best","why":"Authorization must be deterministic. The self-query filter only narrows within the allowed set.","tradeoff":"Needs identity plumbing and a tenant field on every chunk."},{"name":"Let the model infer the tenant from the question","verdict":"poor","why":"A user can name another tenant, or inject text that does.","tradeoff":"Never appropriate for authorization."},{"name":"Post-filter retrieved chunks by tenant before building the prompt","verdict":"good","why":"Keeps other tenants' text out of the prompt, and works as defence in depth.","tradeoff":"Wastes top-k and can leave few or no chunks when most hits belong to other tenants."}],"flips":[{"if":"Tenants have separate indexes","then":"Route the query to the tenant's index and use self-query only for facets."},{"if":"Tenant isolation is a convenience feature rather than a mandatory control","then":"A validated self-query filter plus logging may suffice, but a code-built filter is still cheap and safer."}],"takeaway":"The model proposes filters, code decides access."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-t11-q1","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"Who should build the filter clause that enforces a user's document permissions?","options":["Application code from the caller's authenticated identity","The model, from the question text","The user, as a request parameter","The reranker"],"answer":0,"explain":"Authorization must be deterministic. Model-generated and user-supplied filters can be steered by injection, and the reranker does not filter. Azure's docs also advise a deterministic filter for authorization [[r29]]."},{"id":"w05-t11-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A self-query assistant returns nothing for questions that clearly have answers. The logged filter is `year eq 2024 and doctype eq 'policy' and audience eq 'staff'`. What is the first fix?","options":["Raise `k`","Increase temperature","Relax the weakest constraint and retry when results are empty","Switch to a larger embedding model"],"answer":2,"explain":"An over-narrow filter hides relevant chunks. Relaxing on empty results restores recall. `k`, temperature and embedding size do not change what the filter excludes."},{"id":"w05-t11-q3","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Select the facts about Azure query hints on 2026-10-13.","options":["They are preview","They are best effort","They are applied at `minimal` effort","Generated filters are combined with `baseFilter` using AND"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Query hints are a preview feature, are best effort and are combined using AND. They are not applied at `minimal` effort [[r29]]."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does self-query output?","back":"A semantic query plus a structured metadata filter over declared fields, validated before it reaches the search engine."},{"front":"Why must authorization not rely on a model-generated filter?","back":"The model is steerable and best effort. Build access filters in code from identity and AND them with generated ones."},{"front":"What do you do when a generated filter returns zero results?","back":"Relax the weakest constraint and retry, and log the rejected or empty filter."}],"related":["w04-t18","w07-t08","w07-t06","w05-t07","w05-t13"],"refs":["r29","r30","r39"]},{"id":"w05-t12","order":12,"group":"Advanced patterns","title":"GraphRAG and LazyGraphRAG","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"GraphRAG builds an entity graph and community summaries so a system can answer whole-corpus questions that top-k chunks cannot. LazyGraphRAG defers the expensive model work to query time.","useIf":"Users ask global or thematic questions such as 'what are the main themes across these reports?', or answers require linking entities across documents.","avoidIf":"Questions are single-fact lookups, the corpus changes quickly, or per-user permissions apply inside community summaries.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Top-k vector RAG retrieves the chunks nearest a question. A question like 'what are the recurring root causes across all incident reports?' has no single nearest chunk. **GraphRAG** indexes differently. An LLM extracts entities, relationships and claims from text units and builds a graph. The graph is clustered hierarchically with the Leiden algorithm, and the LLM writes a **summary for every community**. Global queries run over those summaries. The query modes are Global, Local, DRIFT and Basic, and the indexing methods are `standard` (the LLM does all reasoning, the default) and `FastGraphRAG` (classic NLP replaces some LLM steps) [[r40]] [[r16]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w05-t12-d1","title":"GraphRAG indexing and global query","alt":"Documents become text units, entities and relationships form a graph, communities are summarised, and a global query is answered from the community summaries.","src":"flowchart LR\n  D[Documents] --> T[Text units]\n  T --> E[Entities and relations]\n  E --> G[Graph]\n  G --> C[Leiden communities]\n  C --> S[Community summaries]\n  Q[Global question] --> M[Map over summaries]\n  S --> M\n  M --> A[Answer]"},{"t":"p","md":"**The cost problem and LazyGraphRAG.** Full GraphRAG spends many LLM calls at indexing time. Microsoft Research's LazyGraphRAG (blog dated 2024-11-25) reports indexing cost identical to vector RAG and 0.1% of full GraphRAG, answer quality comparable to GraphRAG Global Search on global queries at more than 700 times lower query cost, and, at 4% of the query cost of GraphRAG global search, better results than all compared methods. It exposes one cost-quality dial, the relevance test budget. These are the vendor's own benchmark numbers. An editor's note dated 2025-06-06 says it is integrated into Microsoft Discovery and Azure Local in public preview [[r17]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Sibling idea.** RAPTOR clusters and summarises chunks recursively into a tree, so retrieval can pull from several abstraction levels. Paired with GPT-4 it raised the best QuALITY result by 20% absolute, and indexing LLM calls grow with corpus size [[r18]]. GraphRAG and RAPTOR are research code, so budget for engineering."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Summaries mix documents","md":"A community summary draws on many source documents. If readers hold different permissions, a shared summary can leak facts from documents a reader may not see. This is a reasoned limit, not a quoted one: build summaries per permission scope or avoid the pattern."}],"whereToUse":["Sensemaking over a stable corpus of reports, interviews, tickets or filings","Investigations that follow entities and relationships across many documents","Executive summaries of themes, trends and outliers","Corpora where local lookups already work and global questions are the gap"],"whenToUse":{"use":["A meaningful share of questions are global or thematic","The corpus is entity-dense and relatively stable","You can pay for indexing or accept a deferred-cost variant","Source tracing is possible through the graph"],"avoid":["Most questions are single-fact lookups that hybrid search answers","The corpus changes daily, forcing rebuilds","Permissions differ per reader inside the corpus"],"flips":["Indexing budget is tight: test LazyGraphRAG-style deferral, noting the vendor numbers","Corpus updates frequently: prefer hybrid retrieval and an agentic planner","Only structure across levels is needed: consider a RAPTOR-style summary tree"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Hybrid top-k RAG","Local lookups, fast-changing corpora","Whole-corpus themes","Cheap, misses global questions"],["GraphRAG","Global and multi-hop questions, entity links","Frequent updates, tight indexing budget","Heavy LLM indexing, research code"],["LazyGraphRAG","You want graph-style answers with low indexing cost","You need GA-level maturity","Vendor numbers, public preview in some products [[r17]]"],["RAPTOR","Multi-level abstraction questions","Frequent re-summarisation cost","Recursive summarisation calls [[r18]]"],["Agentic retrieval","Multi-part questions over several sources","Whole-corpus aggregation","Planning calls, mostly preview"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Indexing cost explodes on a large corpus","cause":"The standard method uses LLM calls for all graph steps","fix":"Estimate on a sample, consider `FastGraphRAG` or a deferred-cost approach, and cap the corpus scope"},{"symptom":"Answers feel generic and cannot be traced to text","cause":"Global answers come from community summaries, not source passages","fix":"Expose the community and its source text units, and use Local mode for detail questions"},{"symptom":"Graph answers go stale","cause":"Updates require re-extraction and re-clustering","fix":"Batch updates on a schedule or limit the graph to the stable part of the corpus"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w05-t12-s1","title":"Themes across five thousand incident reports","situation":"Analysts need recurring root causes, affected components and trends across five thousand closed incident reports. The archive grows monthly. Lookups of single incidents already work with hybrid search.","constraints":["Stable archive with monthly additions","Global questions are the gap","Moderate indexing budget, all analysts see all reports"],"unknowns":["How many global questions per month?","Can analysts verify answers against sources?"],"question":"What do you add?","options":[{"name":"Pilot a graph-style index (LazyGraphRAG-style deferral or GraphRAG on a subset) beside the hybrid index and route global questions to it","verdict":"best","why":"Global questions are the documented gap of top-k retrieval [[r16]], and the archive is stable with shared access.","tradeoff":"Extra indexing, a routing decision and vendor-reported cost numbers you must verify on your data."},{"name":"Increase k and let the model summarise 200 chunks","verdict":"poor","why":"Top-k chunks cover a sliver of the corpus and flood the context.","tradeoff":"Reasonable for questions confined to one topic."},{"name":"Replace hybrid with GraphRAG entirely","verdict":"good","why":"One system.","tradeoff":"Lookups become costlier and slower than they need to be."}],"flips":[{"if":"The archive changes daily","then":"Keep hybrid retrieval, and compute themes on a scheduled batch."},{"if":"Almost every question is a single-incident lookup","then":"Skip the graph index entirely and keep hybrid retrieval, since indexing cost buys nothing."}],"takeaway":"Route global questions to a global index."},{"id":"w05-t12-s2","title":"Per-department confidential reports","situation":"Reports are visible by department only. A leader asks 'what themes appear across all departments'.","constraints":["Per-document permissions","High sensitivity","Cross-department summaries are requested"],"unknowns":["Is a cross-department view allowed at all?","Who owns the redaction rules?"],"question":"How do you answer thematic questions?","options":[{"name":"Build summaries per permission scope and aggregate only what the asker is entitled to see, or refuse cross-scope aggregation","verdict":"best","why":"A shared community summary mixes sources and can leak across permissions.","tradeoff":"More indexes and weaker global views."},{"name":"One global community summary set for everyone","verdict":"poor","why":"Summaries embed facts from documents some readers cannot see.","tradeoff":"Fine when every reader may see every document."},{"name":"Plain top-k with security filters","verdict":"good","why":"Respects permissions.","tradeoff":"Cannot answer corpus-wide themes."}],"flips":[{"if":"Leadership is allowed to see everything","then":"A single graph index for that role is acceptable."},{"if":"Only anonymised, count-level themes are needed across departments","then":"Compute them from a metadata-only index that carries no document text, so no restricted content crosses a permission scope."}],"takeaway":"Permissions and shared summaries clash."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-t12-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Which question is GraphRAG built for, and top-k vector RAG handles poorly?","options":["What is the refund window in policy 12?","Define MTTR","Which file holds ticket 5512?","What are the main themes across all reports?"],"answer":3,"explain":"Global, whole-corpus questions have no single nearest chunk. The others are local lookups that hybrid search handles [[r16]]."},{"id":"w05-t12-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each method to its description.","pairs":[{"l":"GraphRAG","r":"Entities and relationships, Leiden communities, community summaries"},{"l":"LazyGraphRAG","r":"Defers LLM work to query time, vendor-reported low cost"},{"l":"RAPTOR","r":"Recursive cluster and summarise tree of chunks"},{"l":"FastGraphRAG","r":"Indexing method that swaps some LLM steps for classic NLP"}],"explain":"These match the sources: GraphRAG docs, the Microsoft Research LazyGraphRAG blog and the RAPTOR paper."},{"id":"w05-t12-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"The corpus changes daily and most questions are single-fact lookups. What do you choose?","options":["Full GraphRAG","Hybrid retrieval with a reranker","RAPTOR with nightly full rebuilds","No retrieval"],"answer":1,"explain":"Daily changes make graph and tree rebuilds costly, and lookups do not need them. Hybrid retrieval with a reranker fits."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does GraphRAG add that top-k RAG lacks?","back":"A graph of entities and relationships with hierarchical community summaries, which lets it answer global questions."},{"front":"What cost numbers did Microsoft report for LazyGraphRAG (2024-11-25)?","back":"Indexing like vector RAG and 0.1% of GraphRAG, over 700 times lower query cost for comparable global quality. Vendor benchmark."},{"front":"Why can community summaries leak data?","back":"They blend facts from many documents, so readers with different permissions can see information they should not."}],"related":["w05-l5","w05-t13","w04-t10","w07-t06","w05-t01"],"refs":["r16","r17","r18","r40"]},{"id":"w05-t13","order":13,"group":"Advanced patterns","title":"Agentic retrieval and Foundry IQ","minutes":10,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Agentic retrieval in Azure AI Search plans focused subqueries with an LLM, runs them in parallel across knowledge sources and merges the reranked results. Foundry IQ packages knowledge bases for agents.","useIf":"Questions are multi-part or depend on chat history, you can accept preview features and an extra planning call, and you want an activity log and references.","avoidIf":"You need GA-only features beyond minimal extractive retrieval, questions are single-fact lookups, or latency leaves no room for planning.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"In classic RAG your code builds the query. In **agentic retrieval** the app calls a **knowledge base** with the `retrieve` action and passes the query plus chat history. At `low` and `medium` effort an LLM plans focused subqueries and picks **knowledge sources**. All subqueries run in parallel (keyword, vector or hybrid), each is semantically reranked and the results are merged. Merged content is always returned. Source references and an execution `activity` log are optional (`includeActivity: true`). You need one knowledge base and at least one knowledge source [[r4]] [[r6]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w05-t13-d1","title":"Agentic retrieval request path","alt":"The app sends a query and chat history to a knowledge base, a planner model writes subqueries, sources are searched in parallel and reranked, and merged content with references and activity returns.","src":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant A as App or agent\n  participant K as Knowledge base\n  participant P as Planner LLM\n  participant S as Knowledge sources\n  A->>K: retrieve query and history\n  K->>P: Plan subqueries\n  P-->>K: Subqueries and sources\n  K->>S: Parallel searches and rerank\n  S-->>K: Ranked results\n  K-->>A: Merged content, references, activity"},{"t":"table","caption":"Reasoning effort (preview, checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Effort","What happens","Notes"],"rows":[["`minimal`","No LLM planning, searches every source in the knowledge base","`outputMode` must be `extractiveData`, no answer synthesis or web source, up to 10 sources. The only effort in stable `2026-04-01`"],["`low` (default if unset)","One planning pass","5,000 answer tokens, 50 docs to semantic ranking (10 with L3 classification)"],["`medium`","Adds a semantic classifier and one retry with a revised plan","10,000 answer tokens, 20 docs with L3, select regions only (15 listed)"],["`auto`","Light first pass, escalates up to medium","Needs `2026-08-01-preview` and a model on the knowledge base"]],"highlightCol":1},{"t":"p","md":"Set effort on the knowledge base and override per request with `retrievalReasoningEffort.kind` [[r5]]. In the stable API only `intents` input, `minimal` effort and extractive output exist. Planning, answer synthesis, `messages` input, `auto` and SSE streaming need `2026-08-01-preview` [[r4]]. `maxRuntimeInSeconds` runs 10 to 600, default 90 [[r38]]. Search bills retrieval tokens, and Azure OpenAI bills planning and answer-synthesis tokens separately. Planner models include the `gpt-5` family, and the page updated 2026-09-17 marks GPT-4o and GPT-4.1 families deprecated [[r39]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Foundry IQ** is the managed knowledge layer: one knowledge base can serve many agents (Foundry Agent Service, Microsoft Agent Framework, custom apps through the Search REST API). Sources include Blob, SharePoint, OneLake and the public web, with chunking, embeddings and metadata extraction automated for indexed sources [[r7]]. **Azure OpenAI On Your Data retires on 2026-10-14**, and Microsoft recommends Foundry Agent Service with Foundry IQ [[r33]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Identity fails open","md":"For indexed sources, ACLs must be ingested (`ingestionPermissionOptions`) and the user token passed in `x-ms-query-source-authorization`. Omit the identity and indexed sources return unfiltered results. If `ingestionPermissionOptions` was not set at creation, results are unfiltered whatever the header says [[r6]]."}],"whereToUse":["Assistants that field multi-part questions across SharePoint, Blob and web sources","Agents that share one managed knowledge base instead of each owning an index","Systems that need references and an activity log for audit and debugging","Teams migrating off Azure OpenAI On Your Data before its retirement"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Questions have several parts or rely on earlier turns","Several knowledge sources must be searched and merged","Preview status is acceptable and pinned versions are managed","You want the planning and reranking inside the service"],"avoid":["Your policy allows GA features only, which limits you to `minimal` extractive retrieval","Questions are simple lookups that one hybrid query answers","You cannot pass the user's identity to the retrieve call"],"flips":["GA-only requirement: use classic hybrid plus semantic ranker, or `minimal` effort","Latency is tight: stay at `low` or below, and set `maxRuntimeInSeconds` deliberately","Planner model on the deprecated list: move to a `gpt-5` family model before relying on filters or hints"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Classic hybrid plus semantic ranker (GA)","Single-shot questions, GA-only rules","Multi-part questions","You write query logic yourself"],["Agentic retrieval at `minimal`","Stable API, several sources, no planning","You need planning or answer synthesis","Extractive only"],["Agentic retrieval at `low` or `medium`","Complex questions with history","Strict latency, GA-only","Planning tokens, preview"],["Your own agent loop with a search tool","Custom logic and full control","You want managed reranking and logs","You build, test and secure the loop"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A caller sees documents they should not","cause":"The retrieve call omitted the user token header, or ACLs were not ingested","fix":"Set `ingestionPermissionOptions` at creation and always send `x-ms-query-source-authorization` [[r6]]"},{"symptom":"Code that worked on a preview version breaks","cause":"Preview contracts changed: knowledge agents became knowledge bases, and docs show both `maxOutputSize` and `maxOutputSizeInTokens`","fix":"Pin the API version, read the migration page and check the SDK spelling [[r34]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w05-t13-s1","title":"GA-only governance","situation":"A regulated team may only use generally available features. Users ask multi-part questions, and the current system is a single hybrid query.","constraints":["GA features only","Multi-part questions","Azure AI Search in place"],"unknowns":["Can the team build its own decomposition step?"],"question":"What do you do?","options":[{"name":"Keep classic hybrid plus semantic ranker, add application-side decomposition, and revisit agentic planning when it is GA","verdict":"best","why":"In the stable `2026-04-01` API only `minimal` extractive retrieval exists, so planning is preview [[r4]].","tradeoff":"You own the decomposition code."},{"name":"Enable `low` effort in production","verdict":"poor","why":"It needs `2026-08-01-preview`, which policy forbids.","tradeoff":"Right where preview features are allowed and pinned."},{"name":"Use `minimal` effort with the stable API","verdict":"good","why":"Fits policy and covers several sources.","tradeoff":"No planning or synthesis, so multi-part questions still need your logic."}],"flips":[{"if":"Preview is allowed in a sandbox first","then":"Pilot `low` effort there and compare recall on the multi-part slice."},{"if":"Questions are almost all single-part","then":"Stay on classic hybrid plus semantic ranker, since planning adds latency and cost without a benefit."}],"takeaway":"Policy decides the effort level."},{"id":"w05-t13-s2","title":"Multi-source copilot with permissions","situation":"An assistant must answer from SharePoint and Blob content. Different employees may read different files. Questions often follow earlier turns.","constraints":["Per-user permissions","Preview acceptable","Chat history matters"],"unknowns":["Were ACLs ingested at creation?","Is the user token available at the retrieve call?"],"question":"What must be in place?","options":[{"name":"Knowledge base with `ingestionPermissionOptions` set at creation, and the user token sent in `x-ms-query-source-authorization` on every retrieve","verdict":"best","why":"Without both, indexed sources return unfiltered results [[r6]].","tradeoff":"Identity plumbing and sync lag for ACL changes."},{"name":"Rely on the app's own check after retrieval","verdict":"poor","why":"Content may already be in the prompt, and results shrink unpredictably.","tradeoff":"Reasonable as defence in depth on top of source filtering."},{"name":"Use an app identity with broad read access","verdict":"good","why":"Simple to build.","tradeoff":"Everything must then be filtered elsewhere, so it breaks least privilege."}],"flips":[{"if":"Revocation must be instant","then":"Add a live source check on sensitive hits."},{"if":"All employees may read all the content","then":"Drop per-user token forwarding and use a single app identity, keeping the knowledge base simpler."}],"takeaway":"Permissions travel with the token."},{"id":"w05-t13-s3","title":"Latency-sensitive lookup assistant","situation":"A support assistant answers short factual questions within a one-second retrieval budget. Some users ask two-part questions.","constraints":["Retrieval budget about one second","Mostly single-fact questions","High volume"],"unknowns":["What share of questions are multi-part?","Can multi-part questions be routed?"],"question":"How do you use agentic retrieval?","options":[{"name":"Serve single-fact questions with one hybrid query, and route multi-part questions to `low` effort","verdict":"best","why":"Planning adds an LLM pass, so reserve it for questions that need it.","tradeoff":"You need a router and must measure its accuracy."},{"name":"Use `medium` for everything","verdict":"poor","why":"It adds a classifier and possible retry to every query.","tradeoff":"Right for a research assistant with no tight budget."},{"name":"Never use planning","verdict":"good","why":"Fastest and cheapest.","tradeoff":"Multi-part questions stay weak."}],"flips":[{"if":"The budget grows to several seconds","then":"Use `low` effort for everything."},{"if":"Two-part questions become the majority of traffic","then":"Route most traffic to `low` effort, and measure latency against the budget before widening it."}],"takeaway":"Spend planning only where questions need it."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-t13-q1","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"A retrieve call returns documents the caller must not see, and the request carried no identity header. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Indexed sources return unfiltered results when no user identity is passed","The semantic ranker ignores ACLs","The planner model is deprecated","The knowledge base is at `minimal` effort"],"answer":0,"explain":"Omitting the user token makes indexed sources return unfiltered results. This fails open. The other options do not remove permission trimming [[r6]]."},{"id":"w05-t13-q2","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Select the statements true of Azure agentic retrieval on 2026-10-13.","options":["Stable `2026-04-01` supports `minimal` extractive retrieval with `intents` input","Query planning at `low` needs `2026-08-01-preview`","SSE streaming needs `2026-08-01-preview`","All effort levels are GA"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Only `minimal` is in the stable API. Planning, synthesis, `auto` and SSE need the preview version [[r4]]."},{"id":"w05-t13-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each effort level to its behaviour.","pairs":[{"l":"`minimal`","r":"No LLM planning, searches every source"},{"l":"`low`","r":"One planning pass, default if unset"},{"l":"`medium`","r":"Adds a semantic classifier and one retry"},{"l":"`auto`","r":"Starts light and escalates up to medium"}],"explain":"These are the behaviours documented for each reasoning effort [[r5]]."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does agentic retrieval do at `low` effort?","back":"One LLM planning pass makes focused subqueries and picks sources. Subqueries run in parallel, are reranked and merged."},{"front":"Which API version holds stable agentic retrieval, and what does it allow?","back":"`2026-04-01`: `minimal` effort, `intents` input and extractive output. Planning, synthesis and SSE need `2026-08-01-preview`."},{"front":"How are permissions enforced in Foundry IQ indexed sources?","back":"Ingest ACLs with `ingestionPermissionOptions` and send the user token in `x-ms-query-source-authorization`. Without it results are unfiltered."}],"related":["w05-l5","w05-t10","w05-t11","w07-t11","w10-t01"],"refs":["r4","r5","r6","r7","r33","r34","r38","r39"]},{"id":"w05-t14","order":14,"group":"Advanced patterns","title":"Corrective and self-reflective RAG (CRAG, Self-RAG)","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Corrective RAG scores retrieved documents and changes course when they look weak. Self-RAG trains one model to decide when to retrieve and to critique passages and its own output.","useIf":"Retrieval quality varies by question, the corpus has gaps, and a wrong answer costs more than an extra model call.","avoidIf":"Latency and cost are tight, retrieval is already reliable, or you cannot bound the retry loop.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Plain RAG trusts whatever retrieval returns. Two families add a check. **CRAG** (Corrective Retrieval Augmented Generation) uses a lightweight **retrieval evaluator** that scores document quality and triggers different actions. When the corpus is weak it falls back to web search, and it filters the kept text with a decompose-then-recompose step. It is plug-and-play: it wraps an existing generator. **Self-RAG** is different. One language model is trained to decide when to retrieve and to critique retrieved passages and its own output using reflection tokens. It needs a fine-tuned model, and the paper reports 7B and 13B sizes [[r19]] [[r20]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w05-t14-d1","title":"A corrective loop with a ceiling","alt":"Retrieve documents, evaluate them, answer when good, rewrite and retry or broaden when weak, abstain when the retry budget is spent.","src":"flowchart TD\n  Q[Question] --> R[Retrieve]\n  R --> E{Evaluate evidence}\n  E -->|good| G[Generate with citations]\n  E -->|weak| W[Rewrite or broaden source]\n  W --> B{Budget left}\n  B -->|yes| R\n  B -->|no| A[Abstain]"},{"t":"p","md":"**What the evaluator can be.** A cross-encoder or reranker score with a calibrated cutoff, a small classifier, or an LLM judge per chunk. The cheaper the evaluator, the more often you can afford it. **What the fallback can be.** A rewritten query, more candidates, a second knowledge source, a web search or abstention. Web fallback has governance cost: data egress, untrusted pages that can carry injected instructions, and unclear provenance. Azure's `medium` agentic effort is a managed cousin: a semantic classifier plus one retry with a revised plan [[r5]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Cost and loops.** Each evaluation and retry is another call. Put a hard ceiling on retries (one or two is typical, author suggestion), cap total tokens and time, and log every decision so you can see whether corrections help. Measure the lift on a golden set that includes weak-retrieval and unanswerable cases. If it does not beat a plain pipeline with a good cutoff, do not ship it."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Try the cheap version first","md":"A reranker score cutoff plus abstention (see score thresholds) captures much of the corrective idea at a fraction of the cost. Add retry or web fallback when measurement shows the weak-retrieval slice is large."}],"whereToUse":["Assistants over incomplete corpora, where partial coverage is common","High-stakes answers that prefer a retry or refusal to a confident error","Pipelines that can legitimately fall back to a second source","Research settings where a fine-tuned reflective model is available"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Retrieval quality varies a lot between questions","A cheap evaluator exists and can be calibrated","A fallback source or abstention path is allowed","Extra latency of a retry is acceptable on the weak slice"],"avoid":["Retrieval is reliable and the cutoff already handles misses","Strict latency budgets without routing","Web fallback is not permitted for your data","You cannot fine-tune and cannot evaluate Self-RAG style models"],"flips":["Web access is banned: replace the web fallback with abstention or a second internal source","Evaluator calls dominate cost: use reranker scores instead of an LLM judge","A fine-tuned reflective model is available and evaluated: Self-RAG becomes an option"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Cutoff plus abstention","You want most of the safety cheaply","Corpus has gaps another source could fill","Refusals on answerable-elsewhere questions"],["CRAG style evaluator and fallback","Retrieval quality varies, fallback allowed","Tight latency, data egress limits","Extra calls, web trust issues [[r19]]"],["Self-RAG","A fine-tuned reflective model is in reach","You use hosted models only","Needs fine-tuning, 7B and 13B in the paper [[r20]]"],["Agentic retrieval at `medium`","Managed retry with revised plan","GA-only rules","Preview, planning tokens [[r5]]"],["Plain RAG","Reliable retrieval, low risk","High-stakes domains","Confident wrong answers"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Retry loops inflate cost and latency on unanswerable questions","cause":"No ceiling on retries or total tokens","fix":"Cap retries at one or two, set a token and time budget, and abstain when spent"},{"symptom":"The evaluator rejects good chunks","cause":"Its threshold was not calibrated, or the evaluator is out of domain","fix":"Calibrate on a golden set with labelled good and bad chunks, and monitor rejection rates"},{"symptom":"Web fallback inserts wrong or hostile text","cause":"Open-web pages are untrusted and may contain injected instructions","fix":"Restrict to approved domains, scan retrieved text and treat it as lower trust"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w05-t14-s1","title":"Support bot with coverage gaps","situation":"A product support bot sees about one in six questions for which the knowledge base has no matching article. Public web docs for the product are allowed.","constraints":["Gaps are common","Public web fallback allowed","Latency budget of four seconds"],"unknowns":["How accurate is the web fallback on past gaps?","Who reviews fallback answers?"],"question":"What do you build?","options":[{"name":"A reranker-score evaluator that routes weak shortlists to one retry, then an approved-domain web fallback, then abstention, with a retry ceiling","verdict":"best","why":"Gaps are frequent and the fallback is allowed, so a corrective loop recovers answers while the ceiling bounds cost.","tradeoff":"Extra latency on the weak slice and a calibrated evaluator to maintain."},{"name":"Always answer from the top 5","verdict":"poor","why":"Gap questions get fabricated answers.","tradeoff":"Acceptable for a low-risk FAQ where gaps are rare."},{"name":"Abstain whenever the cutoff fails","verdict":"good","why":"Safe and cheap.","tradeoff":"Gives up answers that approved web docs could supply."}],"flips":[{"if":"Web access is not permitted","then":"Replace the fallback with abstention and a hand-off."},{"if":"The latency budget drops to about a second","then":"Drop the retry and web step, and abstain or hand off directly when the shortlist is weak."}],"takeaway":"Correct when gaps are common and fallbacks are allowed."},{"id":"w05-t14-s2","title":"Hosted models only, regulated data","situation":"A bank uses hosted frontier models through an API. No fine-tuning of generator models is allowed, and web access is blocked.","constraints":["Hosted models only","No web","High risk"],"unknowns":["Is an internal second source available?","What is the tolerated retry latency?"],"question":"Which reflective approach fits?","options":[{"name":"CRAG-style evaluator on top of the existing generator, with abstention and a second internal source instead of web","verdict":"best","why":"CRAG is plug-and-play around any generator, while hosted-only rules out Self-RAG [[r19]].","tradeoff":"An evaluator to calibrate and an extra retrieval on the weak slice."},{"name":"Self-RAG with a fine-tuned 7B model","verdict":"poor","why":"It needs a fine-tuned reflective model, which policy rules out [[r20]].","tradeoff":"Fits teams that can train, host and evaluate the model."},{"name":"No evaluation, rely on the prompt to say 'I don't know'","verdict":"good","why":"Cheap and gives explicit permission to abstain [[r41]].","tradeoff":"The model cannot see that retrieval was weak, so errors slip through."}],"flips":[{"if":"A fine-tuned reflective model is allowed and evaluated","then":"Test Self-RAG against the CRAG-style design on the golden set."},{"if":"Web access becomes allowed for approved domains","then":"Add the web fallback to the CRAG-style design, restricted to approved sources and logged."}],"takeaway":"Hosted-only rules out Self-RAG."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-t14-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What is the main practical difference between CRAG and Self-RAG?","options":["CRAG needs a fine-tuned model and Self-RAG does not","Only Self-RAG can abstain","CRAG wraps an existing generator, while Self-RAG needs a fine-tuned model with reflection tokens","Only CRAG uses retrieval"],"answer":2,"explain":"CRAG is plug-and-play with a retrieval evaluator. Self-RAG trains one LM to retrieve and critique through reflection tokens [[r19]] [[r20]]."},{"id":"w05-t14-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Select the controls that keep a corrective loop bounded.","options":["A retry ceiling","A token and time budget","Abstention when the budget is spent","Unlimited retries until confidence is high"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Ceilings and abstention bound cost. Unlimited retries loop forever on unanswerable questions."},{"id":"w05-t14-q3","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"Why is open-web fallback a governance concern?","options":["Web pages are untrusted and may contain injected instructions","Web search has no relevance scores","It requires fine-tuning","It disables citations"],"answer":0,"explain":"Fetched pages are untrusted input and can carry instructions or false content. The other options are not true of web fallback."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does the CRAG retrieval evaluator do?","back":"Scores retrieved document quality and triggers different actions, including web search fallback, then filters with decompose-then-recompose."},{"front":"What does Self-RAG require?","back":"A fine-tuned language model that emits reflection tokens to decide when to retrieve and to critique passages and its output."},{"front":"What bounds a corrective loop?","back":"A retry ceiling, a token and time budget, and abstention when the budget is spent."}],"related":["w05-t06","w05-t13","w05-t17","w06-t03","w06-t02"],"refs":["r19","r20","r5","r41"]},{"id":"w05-t15","order":15,"group":"Advanced patterns","title":"RAG vs long context vs cache-augmented generation","minutes":9,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Three ways to put knowledge in front of a model: retrieve chunks per query, send the whole corpus every time, or preload and cache it. Corpus size, update rate and access model decide.","useIf":"You are deciding whether you need a retrieval stack at all, or whether a long prompt or a cached preload is enough.","avoidIf":"You would pick on a vendor's context-window headline alone, without testing accuracy at your corpus size and measuring cost per query.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**RAG** retrieves a few chunks per query. **Long context (LC)** puts the whole corpus in the prompt on every query. **Cache-augmented generation (CAG)** preloads a small, manageable knowledge set into the context and caches the runtime KV state, so no retrieval step runs per query. The CAG authors claim lower latency and fewer retrieval errors for constrained knowledge bases, and it is not suited to large or fast-changing corpora [[r22]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Evidence.** Li et al. found that, when resourced enough, long-context models beat RAG on average, but RAG is much cheaper. Their **Self-Route** sends each query to RAG or LC based on the model's self-reflection and keeps accuracy near LC at lower cost [[r21]]. Anthropic's rule of thumb: a knowledge base under 200,000 tokens, about 500 pages, can go straight into the prompt [[r26]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Why bigger windows are not free.** Accuracy is best when evidence sits at the start or end of the context and drops in the middle [[r24]]. NoLiMa tested 13 models at 32K tokens: 11 fell below 50% of their short-context baseline, and GPT-4o went from 99.3% to 69.7% [[r23]]. Chroma's Context Rot report (2025-07-14) found 18 LLMs less reliable as input grew, even on simple tasks [[r25]]."},{"t":"dials","id":"w05-t15-pick","title":"Pick how to supply knowledge","prompt":"Flip the dials to see which approach wins and why.","dials":[{"id":"size","label":"Corpus size","values":[{"id":"fits","label":"Fits the window (under about 200k tokens)"},{"id":"big","label":"Larger than the window"}]},{"id":"change","label":"Update rate","values":[{"id":"stable","label":"Stable for weeks"},{"id":"fast","label":"Changes daily or faster"}]},{"id":"access","label":"Access model","values":[{"id":"shared","label":"Same documents for everyone"},{"id":"peruser","label":"Per-user permissions"}]}],"options":[{"id":"rag","name":"RAG per query","summary":"Retrieve and rerank a few chunks each time."},{"id":"lc","name":"Long context every query","summary":"Send the corpus in the prompt on each request."},{"id":"cag","name":"Cache-augmented generation","summary":"Preload the corpus and cache its KV state."}],"scores":{"rag":{"size":{"fits":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but adds a pipeline a prompt could replace."},"big":{"s":2,"why":"The way to reach a corpus that cannot fit."}},"change":{"stable":{"s":0,"why":"An index is fine, no special edge."},"fast":{"s":2,"why":"Incremental indexing keeps answers current."}},"access":{"shared":{"s":0,"why":"No special effect."},"peruser":{"s":2,"why":"Security filters trim results per caller before ranking."}}},"lc":{"size":{"fits":{"s":1,"why":"No retrieval stage to lose evidence, but tokens are paid every query."},"big":{"s":-2,"why":"It does not fit, and accuracy drops with length."}},"change":{"stable":{"s":0,"why":"Works, with full token cost unless cached."},"fast":{"s":1,"why":"You always send current text."}},"access":{"shared":{"s":0,"why":"Simple when everyone sees everything."},"peruser":{"s":0,"why":"Assemble each user's documents, at high token cost."}}},"cag":{"size":{"fits":{"s":2,"why":"A small manageable corpus is its design case [[r22]]."},"big":{"s":-2,"why":"Not suited to large corpora [[r22]]."}},"change":{"stable":{"s":1,"why":"Stable content keeps the cache valid."},"fast":{"s":-2,"why":"Every update invalidates the cached state."}},"access":{"shared":{"s":1,"why":"One cached context serves everyone."},"peruser":{"s":-2,"why":"A shared cache cannot hide documents per user (reasoned limit)."}}}},"takeaway":"Corpus size and update rate pick the family. Per-user access pushes you toward retrieval with filters. Mixed workloads can add a Self-Route style router on top."}],"whereToUse":["A small product manual or policy set that rarely changes, where CAG or a cached prompt can replace a pipeline","A large, changing knowledge base where retrieval stays necessary","Assistants with a mixed workload of lookups and whole-corpus summaries, where routing helps","Prototypes that should test a long prompt before building retrieval"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Test a long or cached prompt first when the corpus is under about 200,000 tokens and stable","Use RAG when the corpus is large, changing or permissioned","Use routing when workloads split between lookups and global questions","Measure accuracy at your corpus size, not on a vendor benchmark"],"avoid":["Stuffing a large corpus because the window allows it, without measuring accuracy","CAG for fast-changing or per-user corpora","RAG for a tiny stable corpus where it only adds failure points"],"flips":["Access becomes per user: retrieve with security filters","Corpus passes the window: move to RAG","Per-query token cost dominates: cache the prefix or retrieve","Questions need the whole corpus at once: consider a graph or tree index"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["RAG","Large, changing, permissioned corpora","Questions need everything at once","Retrieval misses, a pipeline to run"],["Long context","Small corpus, simple build, no index to maintain","Large corpus, high volume","Token cost and accuracy loss with length [[r23]]"],["CAG","Small, stable, shared knowledge","Frequent updates, per-user access","Cache invalidation on every change [[r22]]"],["Self-Route","Mixed workloads","Uniform simple traffic","Router errors, two code paths [[r21]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Accuracy falls as the prompt grows, though the facts are present","cause":"Position effects and long-context degradation: evidence in the middle is used less [[r24]]","fix":"Order evidence at the start or end, shrink the context, or retrieve"},{"symptom":"Users see answers about documents they cannot access","cause":"A shared cached context contains documents for every role","fix":"Use security-filtered retrieval, or one cache per permission scope"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w05-t15-s1","title":"One product manual, twice a year","situation":"A team supports one 120,000-token manual set. It is revised twice a year and every customer-facing agent may read all of it.","constraints":["Corpus fits the window","Stable","Shared access"],"unknowns":["Does the platform offer prompt caching?","Is passage-level citation required?"],"question":"How do you supply the manual?","options":[{"name":"Preload and cache it (CAG or a cached prompt) and measure accuracy against a RAG baseline","verdict":"best","why":"It is the design case for CAG: small, stable, shared knowledge, with no retrieval stage to lose evidence [[r22]].","tradeoff":"Revisit when the manual grows or access becomes per role."},{"name":"Full hybrid RAG with a reranker","verdict":"good","why":"Scales and gives chunk-level citations.","tradeoff":"More components than this corpus needs today."},{"name":"Send the manual uncached on every query","verdict":"poor","why":"You pay full token cost each time for content that never changes.","tradeoff":"Acceptable for a prototype or very low volume."}],"flips":[{"if":"The manual grows beyond the window","then":"Move to RAG."},{"if":"Different customers may read different manuals","then":"Use RAG with security filters, or one cache per permission scope, since a shared cache would leak content."}],"takeaway":"Small, stable and shared favours preload and cache."},{"id":"w05-t15-s2","title":"Three million tokens, edited daily","situation":"A wiki of about three million tokens changes every day. Employees see different spaces.","constraints":["Corpus far exceeds the window","Daily updates","Per-user permissions"],"unknowns":["How fast must revocation apply?","Are there whole-corpus questions?"],"question":"Which approach?","options":[{"name":"Hybrid RAG with incremental indexing and security filters","verdict":"best","why":"Size, update rate and permissions each point to retrieval.","tradeoff":"Pipeline, ACL sync and retrieval failures to monitor."},{"name":"CAG with a nightly rebuilt cache","verdict":"poor","why":"It does not fit the window, goes stale daily and a shared cache cannot hide documents.","tradeoff":"Right for a small, stable, shared corpus."},{"name":"Long context with the user's visible pages","verdict":"good","why":"Respects permissions in principle.","tradeoff":"Does not fit and degrades with length."}],"flips":[{"if":"Questions ask for themes across the whole wiki","then":"Add a graph or tree index for those."},{"if":"The wiki shrinks to a small, stable set of public pages","then":"Preloading with a cache becomes viable, and simple RAG is still the safer default if updates continue."}],"takeaway":"Large, changing and permissioned means retrieval."},{"id":"w05-t15-s3","title":"Mixed lookups and whole-document tasks","situation":"An assistant over a 400,000-token legal binder handles 80% quick lookups and 20% 'summarise everything about clause X' tasks. Cost per query matters.","constraints":["Mixed workload","Cost sensitive","Shared access, stable binder"],"unknowns":["Can a router classify queries reliably?","How long can the model's window stay accurate?"],"question":"How do you split the work?","options":[{"name":"Route per query: RAG for lookups and a cached long-context pass for whole-document tasks, validated on a golden set","verdict":"best","why":"Self-Route shows routing keeps accuracy near LC at lower cost [[r21]], and the stable binder suits a cached prefix.","tradeoff":"A router to maintain and two paths to test."},{"name":"Long context for every query","verdict":"poor","why":"Cost scales with volume, and accuracy drops with length [[r23]].","tradeoff":"Fine for a few high-value queries a day."},{"name":"RAG for everything","verdict":"good","why":"Cheap and simple.","tradeoff":"Whole-document tasks miss evidence outside top-k."}],"flips":[{"if":"The router misroutes often","then":"Fall back to RAG with a larger k for the global tasks."},{"if":"Nearly all queries become whole-document tasks","then":"Use the cached long-context pass as the default and keep RAG only for quick lookups."}],"takeaway":"Route when the workload splits."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-t15-q1","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"A team proposes CAG for HR policies where each employee may see a different subset. What is the main problem?","options":["CAG cannot use KV caches","CAG needs fine-tuning","CAG forbids citations","A shared cached context cannot hide documents per user"],"answer":3,"explain":"A shared preloaded context contains every document, so per-user trimming is impossible. This is a reasoned limit. Use security-filtered retrieval instead."},{"id":"w05-t15-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A 150,000-token corpus is sent uncached on every query. A RAG pipeline sends 6,000 tokens per query. How many times more input tokens does the long-context design send per query?","answer":25,"tolerancePct":2,"unit":"times","hint":"Divide 150,000 by 6,000.","explain":"150,000 / 6,000 = 25 times as many input tokens. Prompt caching would discount the repeated prefix, which changes cost but not the accuracy risks of long contexts."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is cache-augmented generation?","back":"Preload a small, manageable knowledge set into the context and cache its KV state, so no retrieval runs per query."},{"front":"What does Self-Route do?","back":"Routes each query to RAG or long context based on model self-reflection, keeping accuracy near long context at lower cost."},{"front":"What did NoLiMa find at 32K tokens?","back":"11 of 13 models fell below 50% of their short-context baseline. GPT-4o dropped from 99.3% to 69.7%."}],"related":["w05-l6","w05-t12","w02-t11","w02-t14","w07-t16"],"refs":["r21","r22","r23","r24","r25","r26"]},{"id":"w05-t16","order":16,"group":"Output and debugging","title":"Citations and answer formatting","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Citations tie each claim to the source text it came from. They prove provenance, not correctness, so a good design adds span-level pointers, a verification step and a UI that lets users check.","useIf":"Answers must be traceable for trust, audit or compliance, or users need to verify claims quickly.","avoidIf":"You would treat the presence of a citation as proof that the answer is supported.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Three levels of citation exist. **Document level** names the file. **Chunk level** points to the retrieved passage. **Span level** points to the exact sentence or character range that supports a claim. Finer is more useful and harder to get right."},{"t":"table","caption":"How citations are produced","head":["Approach","How it works","Notes from the sources"],"rows":[["Prompt-based ids","Label chunks `[1]`, `[2]` in the prompt and ask the model to cite them","Works anywhere, model can mis-cite or invent ids, so verify"],["Anthropic Citations","Enable `citations` on `document` or `search_result` blocks. Types include `char_location`, `page_location` and `content_block_location`","`cited_text` does not count toward output tokens. Works with prompt caching, token counting and batch. Incompatible with structured outputs: enabling both returns HTTP 400 [[r27]]"],["OpenAI file search","Answers carry `file_citation` annotations","Raw hits return only with `include: [\"file_search_call.results\"]` [[tr12]]"],["Azure agentic retrieval","Response has `references` and an `activity` log","Indexed sources can return service-made citation URLs (preview). Remote sources have no backing index and no citation URLs [[r6]]"]],"highlightCol":2},{"t":"p","md":"**A citation proves provenance, not entailment.** The cited span may not support the claim. ALCE, a benchmark for LLM answers with citations, found that on the ELI5 dataset even the best models lacked complete citation support 50% of the time [[tr10]]. Verify with an entailment (NLI) check or an LLM judge on each claim-span pair, or have the model **quote first**: for documents over 20k tokens Anthropic advises asking for word-for-word quotes before answering, and giving explicit permission to say 'I don't know' [[r41]]."},{"t":"flow","title":"Cite, then check","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Number the evidence","md":"Give each chunk a stable id and metadata (title, page, URL)."},{"title":"Answer with spans","md":"Ask for claims that name chunk ids, or use native citations."},{"title":"Verify","md":"Check each claim against its span. Drop or flag unsupported claims.","tag":"check"},{"title":"Render","md":"Show inline markers, hover previews and a link to the source location."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Format and citation features can conflict","md":"If you need schema-validated JSON and native citations from the same provider call, check compatibility first. Anthropic returns HTTP 400 when citations and structured outputs are both on [[r27]]. A common workaround is two calls, or prompt-based ids inside your schema (author suggestion)."}],"whereToUse":["Policy and compliance assistants where each statement needs a source page","Research and legal tools where users read the cited span before trusting an answer","Customer support answers that link to the article section used","Evaluation pipelines that score citation precision and recall automatically"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Users or auditors must trace claims to sources","You can store chunk ids, page numbers and URLs as metadata","You can add a verification step for high-risk claims","The UI can show the cited span"],"avoid":["Treating any citation as proof of support","Chunk metadata is missing, so links cannot point anywhere","Structured output is required and your provider cannot combine it with native citations"],"flips":["Risk is high: add per-claim entailment checks and require quotes first","Latency is tight: cite at chunk level and verify only a sample","Documents are long: prefer span-level citations over whole-document links"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Document-level links","Low-risk answers, quick to build","Users must find the exact passage","Weak verifiability"],["Chunk ids in the prompt","Any model or provider","Models mis-cite or invent ids","Needs a verifier"],["Native span citations (provider feature)","You want exact spans from the API","You need structured output in the same call","Provider lock-in, compatibility limits [[r27]]"],["Quote-first answers","Long documents, high accuracy needs","Latency matters","Extra output tokens [[r41]]"],["Post-hoc attribution by retrieval","Citing an answer that was already written","Claims are paraphrased across several chunks","May attach a plausible but wrong source"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A citation points to a passage that does not say what the answer claims","cause":"The model cites by plausibility, and nothing checks entailment [[tr10]]","fix":"Verify each claim against its cited span, and flag or remove unsupported claims"},{"symptom":"Citations break after re-indexing","cause":"Chunk ids or page offsets changed","fix":"Use stable ids and store source URL, page and offsets as metadata"},{"symptom":"Request fails with HTTP 400 when enabling citations","cause":"Native citations were combined with structured outputs on a provider that forbids it","fix":"Split into two calls or move citation ids into your own schema"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w05-t16-s1","title":"Legal research with reviewer sign-off","situation":"Lawyers review AI-drafted summaries of case documents. Every statement must be checkable against a specific passage, and mistakes carry legal risk.","constraints":["High risk","Long documents","Reviewers will click through"],"unknowns":["Is native span citation available on the chosen provider?","Who owns the verification step?"],"question":"How do you design citations?","options":[{"name":"Span-level citations with quote-first prompting and an automated entailment check that flags unsupported claims for the reviewer","verdict":"best","why":"Provenance alone is not enough in a high-risk setting, and quotes plus a check catch mis-citation [[r41]] [[tr10]].","tradeoff":"More tokens, latency and a verifier to maintain."},{"name":"A source link at the end of each answer","verdict":"poor","why":"The reviewer cannot tell which sentence came from which passage.","tradeoff":"Fine for a low-risk FAQ."},{"name":"Chunk-level citations without verification","verdict":"good","why":"Cheaper and still traceable to a passage.","tradeoff":"Mis-citation can slip through."}],"flips":[{"if":"Risk is low and speed matters","then":"Use chunk-level links and sample-based checks."},{"if":"Reviewers cannot open source documents from the UI","then":"Show the quoted passage inline next to each claim so the check does not depend on a click-through."}],"takeaway":"Risk sets how much you verify."},{"id":"w05-t16-s2","title":"Typed JSON answers plus citations","situation":"A claims-processing service needs schema-valid JSON output and a citation for each field value. The provider's native citation feature is under evaluation.","constraints":["Strict schema required","Citations required per field","Batch workload"],"unknowns":["Can the provider combine citations and structured output?","Is a second verification call affordable?"],"question":"What do you build?","options":[{"name":"Put chunk ids inside your schema (per-field `source_id` and `quote`), then verify the quote exists in the cited chunk with code","verdict":"best","why":"It works whichever provider restricts combining native citations with structured output [[r27]], and a string match on the quote is a cheap verifier.","tradeoff":"You maintain prompt and schema, and quotes can still paraphrase, so allow a fuzzy match."},{"name":"Enable native citations and structured outputs together","verdict":"poor","why":"Anthropic returns HTTP 400 when both are on [[r27]].","tradeoff":"Right if your provider documents that the combination is supported."},{"name":"Two calls: structured extraction, then a separate citation call","verdict":"good","why":"Cleanly separates concerns.","tradeoff":"Doubles calls and the second call can disagree with the first."}],"flips":[{"if":"The provider later supports both together","then":"Switch to native citations and drop the custom verifier for spans."},{"if":"The schema is relaxed to free text with inline citations","then":"Use the provider's native citations directly and sample-check them."}],"takeaway":"Check feature compatibility before designing the output."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-t16-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"A model cites chunk 3 for a claim, and chunk 3 does not support it. What does this show?","options":["Citations prove correctness","Chunk ids were too short","The reranker failed","A citation proves provenance, not entailment, so supports need checking"],"answer":3,"explain":"A pointer to a source does not show the source supports the claim. Verify with NLI, quote-first or a judge. ALCE found incomplete citation support about half the time on ELI5 for the best models [[tr10]]."},{"id":"w05-t16-q2","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"A service enables Anthropic `citations` and a JSON schema output in one request and gets HTTP 400. What is the cause?","options":["Citations are incompatible with structured outputs","`cited_text` exceeded the output limit","Prompt caching is on","The documents were too short"],"answer":0,"explain":"Enabling both returns HTTP 400. Prompt caching, token counting and batch are compatible with citations [[r27]]."},{"id":"w05-t16-q3","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put a cite-then-check flow in order.","items":["Number the evidence chunks","Generate claims that name chunk ids","Verify each claim against its span","Render markers with links to the source"],"explain":"You need stable ids before generation, verification after generation, and rendering last so the UI shows only checked claims."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Citation versus entailment: what is the difference?","back":"A citation shows where a claim came from. Entailment shows the source actually supports it. Check it separately."},{"front":"What is the incompatibility in Anthropic's Citations feature?","back":"Citations cannot be combined with structured outputs. Enabling both returns HTTP 400."},{"front":"What does OpenAI file search return for citations?","back":"`file_citation` annotations on the answer. Raw hits need `include: [\"file_search_call.results\"]`."}],"related":["w06-t03","w06-t02","w05-t17","w05-t06","w05-l6"],"refs":["r27","r41","r6","tr10","tr12"]},{"id":"w05-t17","order":17,"group":"Output and debugging","title":"Debugging a bad RAG answer","minutes":9,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Trace a bad answer from symptom to component. Check the evidence first, then retrieval, ranking, context assembly and generation, one stage at a time.","useIf":"An answer is wrong, missing, stale or unsupported, and you need to know which stage to fix before changing anything.","avoidIf":"You would tune prompts or swap models before looking at what was retrieved.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Most bad RAG answers are retrieval faults dressed as generation faults. The method is a bottom-up checklist. Move up a stage only when the stage below is proven good. Keep a **trace** per request: the raw and rewritten query, filters, per-retriever ranks, fused and reranked order with scores, the cutoff decision, the final context and the prompt, and the answer with its citations. Platforms help: Azure agentic retrieval returns an `activity` log when you set `includeActivity: true` [[r6]], and OpenAI file search returns raw hits only with `include: [\"file_search_call.results\"]` [[tr12]]."},{"t":"flow","title":"Debug from the bottom up","direction":"v","steps":[{"title":"1. Is the answer in the corpus?","md":"Search the source for the fact. Missing means ingestion, parsing or a real coverage gap."},{"title":"2. Is it in the index, and visible to this caller?","md":"Query by exact text. Check chunking, metadata and ACL filters."},{"title":"3. Is it in the candidates?","md":"Look at per-leg ranks. Not in the top candidates means a recall fault: retriever mode, query transformation, `k`."},{"title":"4. Does it survive ranking and cutoff?","md":"In the candidates but dropped means reranker, fusion or an over-strict cutoff."},{"title":"5. Is it in the prompt, and well placed?","md":"Check context size, truncation and order. Evidence in the middle is used less [[r24]]."},{"title":"6. Oracle test: gold chunk in the prompt","md":"Still wrong means a generation fault. Right means one of stages 1 to 5.","tag":"decisive"}]},{"t":"table","caption":"Symptom to likely component","head":["Symptom","Likely component","First check"],"rows":[["Exact code or name not found","Lexical leg or analyzer","Does BM25 return it? Test the analyzer"],["Right topic, wrong passage","Chunking or ranking","Rank of the gold chunk before and after rerank"],["Works for admins, not for staff","ACL filter or identity","Run the query with each identity and compare candidates"],["Answer cites a chunk that does not support it","Generation and citation","Entailment check of claim against span"],["Stale answer","Ingestion freshness","Index timestamp for the source document"],["Confident answer to an unanswerable question","Cutoff and abstention","Scores of the top chunks, cutoff value"]],"highlightCol":1},{"t":"p","md":"**Two habits make the method fast.** First, keep a small golden set of real questions with their gold chunks, so every hypothesis becomes a rank or recall@k check rather than an opinion. Second, replay failures with the failing user's real identity and filters, because many apparent quality bugs are permission, freshness or language-handling bugs that vanish under a broad test account."},{"t":"p","md":"Fix the stage you proved, then re-run the **golden set** so the fix does not break other queries. Track recall@k and context precision for retrieval, and faithfulness for generation, as separate metrics."}],"whereToUse":["Triage of user-reported wrong answers by a support or platform engineer","Regression analysis after an index, embedding or prompt change","Building the logging schema for a new RAG service","Interview answers about how you would isolate a retrieval fault from a generation fault"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Every time a user flags an answer, trace before you tune","After any change to chunking, embeddings, filters or prompts","When quality differs by user, language or query type","To decide whether the next investment is retrieval or generation"],"avoid":["Changing several stages at once, which hides which change helped","Debugging from the final answer alone, without the trace","Declaring a model fault before running the oracle test"],"flips":["The oracle test fails: move to prompt, context order and model","The gold chunk is absent from candidates: fix recall before ranking","Only one identity is affected: look at filters and ACL sync first"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Bottom-up trace (this topic)","You need a reliable root cause","No logs exist yet","Needs logging and a golden set"],["Prompt tweaking first","The oracle test already failed","Evidence is missing","Hides retrieval faults"],["Swap the model","Generation faults with gold context","Retrieval faults","Cost, regressions"],["Add a reranker or hybrid blindly","Gold chunk is in candidates but low","Gold chunk is absent","Latency and cost without gain"],["LLM judge for whole answers only","Fast scoring at scale","You need to localise the fault","No stage attribution"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A fix improves one question and breaks others","cause":"No golden set, so regressions are invisible","fix":"Keep labelled queries with gold chunks and rerun them after every change"},{"symptom":"The gold chunk is retrieved but the answer ignores it","cause":"It sits mid-context among many chunks, or the prompt does not require using it [[r24]]","fix":"Shrink and reorder the context, require citations and run the oracle test"},{"symptom":"Tests pass, production fails for some users","cause":"Tests ran with a broad identity, while real users hit ACL filters or different tenants","fix":"Replay the failing query with the real user's identity and compare candidates"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w05-t17-s1","title":"The right chunk is at rank 31","situation":"A user reports a wrong answer. The trace shows the gold chunk at rank 31 in the fused list. The reranker takes the top 50, and the context holds five chunks.","constraints":["Gold chunk is in the candidates","Reranker cuts to five chunks","Hybrid retrieval already runs"],"unknowns":["What rank does the gold chunk get after reranking?","How often do similar failures occur?"],"question":"Where is the fault?","options":[{"name":"Ranking: check the reranked position, improve reranking or chunk text, then widen the window if needed","verdict":"best","why":"The chunk is present in the candidates, so the fault sits after retrieval. The reranked rank tells you whether ranking or the cutoff dropped it.","tradeoff":"Needs score logging and golden-set reruns."},{"name":"Swap to a larger generation model","verdict":"poor","why":"The model never saw the chunk if it was cut before the prompt.","tradeoff":"Right only if the oracle test fails."},{"name":"Increase k to 200 for retrieval","verdict":"good","why":"More candidates can help recall.","tradeoff":"Does not solve a reranking or cutoff drop, and costs more."}],"flips":[{"if":"The gold chunk is at rank 120 and not in the candidates","then":"It is a recall fault: fix the retriever, query transformation or chunking."},{"if":"The gold chunk is at rank 3 after reranking but absent from the five-chunk context","then":"It is a context-assembly fault: check the cutoff, deduplication and token-budget trimming."}],"takeaway":"Locate the stage before you fix."},{"id":"w05-t17-s2","title":"Answer contradicts the retrieved chunk","situation":"The context contains the correct policy chunk at the top, yet the answer states the opposite. The oracle test with the same chunk also fails.","constraints":["Retrieval is correct","Context is short","The prompt asks for grounded answers"],"unknowns":["Does the prompt allow outside knowledge?","Does the chunk have a negation or table that is hard to parse?"],"question":"What do you change?","options":[{"name":"Treat it as a generation fault: tighten the prompt, require quote-then-answer, check chunk formatting, and test a stronger model","verdict":"best","why":"The oracle test isolates generation. Quotes make contradictions visible.","tradeoff":"More tokens and prompt maintenance."},{"name":"Retune hybrid weights","verdict":"poor","why":"Retrieval already surfaced the right chunk.","tradeoff":"Right when the gold chunk is missing or low."},{"name":"Add a citation checker only","verdict":"good","why":"It will flag the contradiction.","tradeoff":"It detects the error without fixing the cause."}],"flips":[{"if":"The chunk is a flattened table that loses its meaning","then":"Fix parsing and chunking, since the model saw bad evidence."},{"if":"The oracle test passes with the same chunk","then":"The fault lies in the live context: look at prompt assembly, other chunks that contradict the gold one, or truncation."}],"takeaway":"The oracle test separates retrieval from generation."},{"id":"w05-t17-s3","title":"Works for admins, fails for staff","situation":"Support engineers reproduce answers fine as admins. Staff users get 'not found' for the same question.","constraints":["Per-document permissions","Security filter in the index","Same query text"],"unknowns":["Were ACLs ingested for the staff documents?","Does the retrieval call use the user's identity?"],"question":"Where do you look?","options":[{"name":"Replay the query with a staff identity, compare candidates and ACL fields, and check ACL sync","verdict":"best","why":"An identity-dependent failure points to filters or ACL data, not the model.","tradeoff":"Needs test identities and a trace that records the filter."},{"name":"Increase the cutoff sensitivity","verdict":"poor","why":"The chunk is probably filtered out before ranking.","tradeoff":"Right when scores are low for everyone."},{"name":"Tell staff to rephrase","verdict":"good","why":"May work around some misses.","tradeoff":"Leaves the real defect."}],"flips":[{"if":"Staff and admins get identical candidates","then":"Look at the cutoff, prompt or language handling instead."},{"if":"Only the staff candidates are missing, and the ACL field values differ for the gold chunk","then":"It is an ACL sync or group-mapping fault: fix the index permission fields rather than retrieval."}],"takeaway":"Replay with the real identity."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w05-t17-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"You paste the gold chunk into the prompt and the model still answers wrongly. Which stage do you fix?","options":["Retrieval recall","Generation: prompt, context handling or model","Index freshness","ACL sync"],"answer":1,"explain":"The oracle test removes retrieval, ranking and filters. A failure with gold context in the prompt is a generation fault."},{"id":"w05-t17-q2","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the debugging checks in order, from first to last.","items":["Is the answer in the corpus?","Is it in the index and visible to this caller?","Is it in the retrieved candidates?","Does it survive reranking and the cutoff?","Is it in the prompt, well placed?"],"explain":"Go bottom-up: source, index and permissions, candidates, ranking and cutoff, then context assembly. Only then blame generation."},{"id":"w05-t17-q3","type":"match","tag":"diagnose","q":"Match each symptom to its likely stage.","pairs":[{"l":"Exact code never found","r":"Lexical leg or analyzer"},{"l":"Works for admins, not staff","r":"ACL filter or identity"},{"l":"Stale answer","r":"Ingestion freshness"},{"l":"Confident answer to an unanswerable question","r":"Cutoff and abstention"}],"explain":"Each symptom has a primary suspect: analyzers for exact tokens, filters for identity-specific misses, ingestion for staleness and the cutoff for no-answer cases."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the oracle test?","back":"Put the gold chunk in the prompt. Still wrong is a generation fault. Right means retrieval, ranking, filters or context assembly failed."},{"front":"What should a RAG trace record?","back":"Raw and rewritten query, filters, per-leg ranks, fused and reranked order with scores, cutoff decision, final context, prompt, answer and citations."},{"front":"In which order do you debug a bad answer?","back":"Corpus, index and ACLs, candidates, ranking and cutoff, prompt context, then generation."}],"related":["w05-t01","w05-l6","w14-t03","w14-t06","w07-t19"],"refs":["r6","r24","tr12"]}]});
